(window.__D=window.__D||{})["data/ch09.json"]={"n": 9, "title": "War and deportations (1908/1914–1923)", "events": [{"id": "H6-005", "date": "1914-06-12", "date_end": "1914-11-02", "date_text": "Spring – autumn 1914 (Phocaea, 12 June 1914)", "precision": "day", "title": "Expulsion of Greeks from Eastern Thrace and the Aegean coast (context)", "summary": "Special Organization bands, coordinated with the CUP, drive Greeks out of Eastern Thrace and the Aegean coast; at Phocaea the attack begins on 12 June 1914. 150,000–300,000 Ottoman Greeks are expelled to Greece or moved inland. Talat ends the campaign on 2 November 1914. Pontus was barely touched in this phase, but the methods were a precedent.", "people": ["Talat Pasha", "Eşref Kuşçubaşı", "Celal Bayar", "Rahmi Bey"], "sources_primary": ["Emmanouil Emmanouilidis in the Ottoman parliament, 6 July 1914", "French eyewitness Félix Sartiaux on Phocaea"], "sources_modern": ["Bjørnlund 2008", "Akçam, The Young Turks' Crime against Humanity (Princeton UP, 2012), p. 63 ('roughly three hundred thousand' fled or were expelled) and pp. 88-89 (the range of contemporary figures) [Turkish-born scholar abroad]", "Meichanetsidis, GSI 9:1 (2015)", "Erol, The Ottoman Crisis in Western Anatolia (2016)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The totals differ: Bjørnlund gives 150,000–200,000, Akçam about 300,000, and Meichanetsidis higher partial figures. Akçam (2012, pp. 88–89) lists the contemporary counts: by the Greek government's figures, 60,926 people (15,572 families) expelled from eastern Thrace in the first half of 1914 and about 150,000 leaving western Anatolia during the year; 150,000–200,000 before the war by a 1930 report of the US ambassador at Athens, which Akçam thinks conservative. Talat blamed the excesses on local men such as Rahmi Bey. One cable bears on Pontus: on 15 April 1914 the ministry's immigrant office asked Trebizond how many of the Greek emigrants bound for Salonica had been let on board without the 10-kuruş fare, and whether there were enough for another ship (BOA DH.ŞFR 40/13; Akçam 2012, p. 77).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Emmanouilidis' speech, 6 July 1914; Meichanetsidis 2015", "Neutral sources: Sartiaux; Bjørnlund 2008; Akçam 2012; Erol 2016"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Phocaea (Foça)", "lat": 38.67, "lon": 26.757, "role": "massacre and flight, 12 June 1914"}, {"name": "Malkara", "lat": 40.89, "lon": 26.904, "role": "Eastern Thrace expulsions, 1913–14"}, {"name": "Smyrna", "lat": 38.419, "lon": 27.129, "role": "regional centre"}]}, {"id": "H6-006", "date": "1914-07-07", "date_text": "7 July 1914", "precision": "day", "title": "Cemal Azmi becomes vali of Trebizond", "summary": "Cemal Azmi, a CUP man and co-founder of the Special Organization, takes over the vilayet of Trebizond. He will direct the destruction of the Armenians of the vilayet in 1915 and hand the city to Metropolitan Chrysanthos before fleeing the Russians in April 1916.", "people": ["Cemal Azmi"], "sources_modern": ["Kévorkian, The Armenian Genocide: A Complete History (2011)"], "confidence": "high", "basis": "armenian", "basis_detail": ["Armenian sources: Kévorkian 2011"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "seat of the vali"}]}, {"id": "C3-036", "date": "1914-08", "date_end": "1916-04", "date_text": "August 1914 to April 1916", "precision": "month", "title": "Chrysanthos' account: Greek loyalty guaranteed; Russian subjects and labour battalions spared", "summary": "Chrysanthos writes that in August 1914 he 'ὑπὸ προσωπικὴν αὐτοῦ εὐθύνην ἠγγυήθη … περὶ τῆς νομιμοφροσύνης τῶν Ἑλλήνων τῆς ὅλης … βιλαετίου' (guaranteed on his personal responsibility the loyalty of the Greeks of the whole vilayet) to the vali Cemal Azmi, and set up a network with the centres of Trebizond, Rhodopolis and Chaldia. Through the vali he had cancelled the mid-winter exile to Harput of 'τριακόσιοι' (300) Russian-subject Greeks. Because the vali ran army supply and the labour battalions, most Greek conscripts were given light work in Trebizond's army workshops, and church officers and teachers were exempted; in April 1916, ordered to follow the retreating army, the battalions were freed 'at the last hour'. A gendarmerie post was obtained to protect Santa from çetes.", "people": ["Chrysanthos", "Cemal Azmi"], "sources_primary": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.752–753 (account of a participant)", "Archive of Metropolitan Chrysanthos, no. 38, as cited by him"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "All of this rests on Chrysanthos. Other sources confirm Cemal Azmi's governorship and the labour battalions, but not these exemptions.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, pp.752–753"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "vali's seat; army workshops"}, {"name": "Harput", "lat": 38.7069, "lon": 39.2453, "role": "planned exile of 300 Russian subjects"}, {"name": "Santa (Dumanlı)", "lat": 40.66888, "lon": 39.80164, "role": "gendarmerie post"}]}, {"id": "AK-001", "date": "1914-09-05", "date_end": "1914-12-24", "date_text": "5 September – 24 December 1914", "precision": "day", "title": "The Interior Ministry has the Greek property of Trebizond, Ordu and Giresun listed", "summary": "On 5 September 1914 the Interior Ministry's Security Directorate asked the provinces of Aydın and Trebizond and the district of Canik (Samsun), all with large Greek communities, for a report on all the property and buildings of the Greek community, with their value and owners. Ten days later nearly every province was asked for the farms and estates owned by Greeks, and on 6 October Trebizond and Canik were ordered again. On 24 December 1914 the governor of Trebizond (then Cemal Azmi, H6-006) sent three notebooks giving the number, kind, value and owners of the Greek community's properties and buildings in the towns of Trebizond, Ordu and Giresun. Akçam, who found the cables in the Ottoman archive, notes that the lists were made while a commission was studying a Turkish-Greek exchange of populations, and thinks it likely that they were drawn up for it (Akçam 2012, pp. 37-39).", "people": ["Cemal Azmi"], "sources_primary": ["[Ottoman document] Security Directorate to Aydın, Trebizond and Canik, 5 September 1914, BOA DH.ŞFR 45/200 (Akçam 2012, p. 37, n. 25)", "[Ottoman document] Interior Ministry to Edirne, Adana, Aydın, Trebizond and Kastamonu and to Izmit, Bolu, Canik, Karesi, Antalya and Menteşe, 15 September 1914, BOA DH.EUM 3. Şube 2/24 (Akçam 2012, p. 38, n. 26)", "[Ottoman document] Security Directorate to Izmir, Trebizond and Canik, 6 October 1914, BOA DH.EUM 3. Şube 2/31 (Akçam 2012, p. 38, n. 27)", "[Ottoman document] the governor of Trebizond's reply of 24 December 1914, with three notebooks for Trebizond, Ordu and Giresun, BOA DH.EUM 3. Şube 2/31 (Akçam 2012, pp. 38-39, n. 29)"], "sources_modern": ["Akçam, The Young Turks' Crime against Humanity (Princeton UP, 2012), pp. 37-39 [Turkish-born scholar abroad]"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Akçam quotes the cables and the governor's covering reply; as far as we know the notebooks themselves have not been published. That they were made for a possible exchange of populations is Akçam's inference; the commission and its instructions for lists for an exchange are documented (BOA DH.EUM 3. Şube 2/26-A, cited by Akçam, p. 39).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Interior Ministry cables and the governor of Trebizond's reply (BOA), as quoted by Akçam 2012", "Neutral sources: Akçam 2012 (interpretation)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "register of Greek property, 24 Dec 1914"}, {"name": "Ordu", "lat": 40.984, "lon": 37.879, "role": "register of Greek property"}, {"name": "Giresun", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.389, "role": "register of Greek property"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "seat of the Canik district, asked for the same report"}]}, {"id": "H6-008", "date": "1914-10-29", "date_end": "1915-01-17", "date_text": "29 Oct 1914 – 17 Jan 1915 (Trebizond shelled in mid-November 1914)", "precision": "day", "title": "War with Russia; Trebizond shelled; Sarıkamış", "summary": "The Ottoman fleet's raid on the Russian Black Sea coast (29 October) brings war with Russia. The Russian Black Sea Fleet shells Trebizond in mid-November 1914; a Capuchin missionary's diary entry dated Trebizond, 16 November 1914, describes the bombardment, with women and children crowding into the Catholic church and garden and civilians killed or wounded. Enver's winter offensive at Sarıkamış (22 Dec 1914 – 17 Jan 1915) destroys most of the Third Army and exposes the eastern provinces.", "people": ["Enver Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["F. Lorenzo da Montemarciano, Memorie della Missione di Trebisonda, pp. 1r-2v, entry dated Trebizond, 16 Nov 1914 (Capuchin archive, AGC AC 149), via Kyriakides (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias 2026; Greek ed. 2015), p. 49 n. 45"], "sources_modern": ["Allen & Muratoff, Caucasian Battlefields (1953)", "McMeekin, The Ottoman Endgame (2015)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The accounts give only 'mid-November' for the bombardment; the earliest dated witness is the missionary's diary entry of 16 November 1914.", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Allen & Muratoff 1953; McMeekin 2015", "Neutral sources: Capuchin diary of F. Lorenzo (via Kyriakides & Nikoloudis 2026, p. 49)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "shelled by the Russian fleet, 17 Nov 1914"}, {"name": "Sarıkamış", "lat": 40.338, "lon": 42.573, "role": "Ottoman disaster, Dec 1914 – Jan 1915"}]}, {"id": "H6-009", "date": "1915", "date_end": "1918-02", "date_text": "1915–1918", "precision": "year", "title": "The first guerrilla bands (andartes)", "summary": "Deserters from the labour battalions form armed bands in the mountains. Santa organises its own defence in 1916 under Efkleidis Kourtidis. In the hills of Erbaa and Taşova a band led by Kapetan Iakof (killed near Endik Pınar, 1916) passes to the 20-year-old Anastasios Papadopoulos, 'Kotsa Nastas'. Bands are also active in the Çarşamba and Bafra hills; the Bafra-area leader Antonis Hacılefteryu, known as Anton Pasha, on whom the authorities had put a price of 5,000 lira, is murdered by a fellow Greek in August 1917, and his wife Pelagia leads the band until 1923 (Agtzidis 2005). Morris & Ze'evi accept that during the war these bands, 'composed chiefly of youngsters avoiding the draft', 'occasionally attacked Turkish villagers and gendarmes'. A Greek historian, Agtzidis (1992, p. 167), writes that the bands began as village self-defence, that calls for a 'general revolution' were made from 1916, and that their arms came from battles, from Russian Greek networks and from 'Russian aid'.", "people": ["Efkleidis Kourtidis", "Kapetan Iakof", "Anastasios Papadopoulos (Kotsa Nastas)", "Anton Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["Pontic refugee memoirs (via the Encyclopedia of Pontic Hellenism and Topalidis)", "[Greek government documents, translated] Brown & Ion (trans.), Persecutions of the Greeks in Turkey since the Beginning of the European War (New York: Oxford UP for the American-Hellenic Society, 1918), pp. 12-13", "[Greek Metropolitan, letter of 27 Feb 1917] Patriarchate 1919, p. 121 (about 300 deserters armed from a Russian torpedo boat)"], "sources_modern": ["Encyclopedia of Pontic Hellenism (Malliaris), via pontosworld", "Topalidis, 'Santa, Pontos after 1914'", "Çilingir 2014 ('Kaptanların Kaptanı Anton Paşa', HyeTert) and Demirer 2021 (Kaldıraç) (as cited on Wikipedia)", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 383 (wartime bands; their n. 13 cites Shaw, From Empire to Republic, vol. 2, 582–585)", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 383", "Agtzidis 1992, p. 167 (bands at first village self-defence; more than 18,000 armed at the peak, about 1920; calls for a 'general revolution' from 1916; arms from battles, Russian Greek networks and 'Russian aid') [Greek]", "Agtzidis, Έλληνες του Πόντου: Η γενοκτονία από τον τουρκικό εθνικισμό (Athens 2005), p. 161 of the electronic edition [Greek] (Anton Pasha the leading Greek band leader of 1916-18; a bounty of 5,000 lira; 'murdered by a compatriot' in August 1917; his wife, the kapetanissa Pelagia, led the band until August 1923)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Greek sources call the bands self-defence by men escaping death in the labour battalions. Morris & Ze'evi (p. 383) accept that wartime bands 'occasionally attacked Turkish villagers and gendarmes' and report that 'the Turks charged' that Russia armed and reinforced them; their note cites only Shaw, a historian close to the Turkish view who relied on Ottoman sources, so the Russian arming remains a Turkish charge. A Greek primary source points the same way: the Metropolitan of Amaseia wrote on 27 February 1917 that the pretext for the persecution was 'about 300 deserters' who had gone to Trebizond for arms brought by a Russian torpedo boat, and that the government had the right to punish them, but not the women, children and villages (Patriarchate 1919, p. 121). The detailed Turkish accounts of raids on Muslims in 1916–18 (Duman 2012; Aydın & Duman 2012), with road blocks and raids on convoys and grain stores around Çarşamba, are given under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H6-013, H7-004). The size of the bands in 1916–18 is unknown.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Pontic refugee memoirs; Encyclopedia of Pontic Hellenism; Topalidis", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Çilingir 2014; Demirer 2021 (name and death of Anton Pasha)", "Neutral sources: Morris & Ze'evi 2019, p. 383 (occasional attacks on Turkish villagers and gendarmes)", "Greek sources: Greek government documents (Brown & Ion 1918, pp. 12-13); Greek historiography", "Neutral sources: Morris & Ze'evi 2019, p. 383 (the statement on band attacks carries no specific source; their n. 13 refers to Shaw, a historian close to the Turkish position)", "Greek sources: Agtzidis 1992"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Santa (Dumanlı)", "lat": 40.6, "lon": 39.7, "role": "guerrilla area (Kourtidis), from 1916"}, {"name": "Topçam / Yaylacık hills near Erbaa", "lat": 40.78, "lon": 36.55, "role": "guerrilla area (Iakof, Kotsa Nastas), from 1915; approximate"}, {"name": "Çarşamba", "lat": 41.199, "lon": 36.722, "role": "guerrilla area 1916–18"}, {"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.567, "lon": 35.906, "role": "guerrilla area (Anton Pasha, d. 1917)"}, {"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "deserters held, report of 19 May 1916"}, {"name": "Sivas (Sebasteia)", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.016, "role": "where surrendered deserters were sent"}, {"name": "Giresun (Kerasous)", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.39, "role": "search for c. 300 deserters, winter 1916-17"}]}, {"id": "H6-010", "date": "1915-03", "date_end": "1915-04", "date_text": "March – April 1915", "precision": "month", "title": "First deportations of Greek notables; violence in Santa", "summary": "Leading Greeks of Eastern Thrace, Pontus and Constantinople are sent inland: on 8 March 1915 the Constantinople police deported some 200 Greek community leaders, intellectuals, clergy and businessmen (Morris & Ze'evi), while the Greek Legation reported up to 10,000 sent from the capital (2 March 1915). In April 1915 an Ottoman band hunting deserters burns and loots Greek houses in Santa and takes 50 men to the labour battalions, almost all of whom die.", "sources_primary": ["Greek Legation note, 2 March 1915", "Nymphopoulos 1953 (Santa memoir)"], "sources_modern": ["Hofmann, Abriss (AGA)", "Topalidis, 'Santa, Pontos after 1914' (pontosworld), citing Fotiadis 2019", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 387 (some 200 notables deported from Constantinople on 8 March 1915)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The Santa episode rests on a refugee's memoir, known to us through later Greek works. For Constantinople, about 200 notables (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 387) against up to 10,000 (Greek Legation note); the two figures may count different groups.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Greek Legation note, 2 March 1915; Nymphopoulos 1953 (Santa memoir); Topalidis citing Fotiadis 2019", "Neutral sources: Hofmann", "Neutral sources: Morris & Ze'evi 2019, p. 387"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Santa (Dumanlı)", "lat": 40.6, "lon": 39.7, "role": "raid on deserters' villages, April 1915 (approximate centroid of the seven Santa villages)"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "deportation of notables"}]}, {"id": "H6-011", "date": "1915-06-24", "date_end": "1915-07-31", "date_text": "Late June – July 1915", "precision": "month", "title": "The Armenian genocide in the Trebizond vilayet", "summary": "Armenian leaders are arrested in late June; the deportation order is posted and convoys leave from 1 July. Men are killed in the valleys behind the city; women and children are loaded onto boats 'for Samsun' and drowned at sea, as the US consul Heizer, the Italian consul Gorrini, an Ottoman deputy and a Turkish officer later testified. Topal Osman's band took part. About 30,000 Armenians lived in and around Trebizond before the war; about 500 survivors returned under the Russians in 1916. Chrysanthos, an eyewitness, translates the ten-article deportation proclamation dated 13 June 1331 (26 June 1915 N.S.), says Bahaeddin Şakir came from Erzurum in early June and with the vali gave secret orders for a general massacre, and describes about 300 young Armenians killed at sea off Platana, corpses in the Değirmendere, and Greek villagers (above all at Sana in Gemura) feeding Armenians hidden in the forests until April 1916.", "people": ["Cemal Azmi", "Yenibahçeli Nail Bey", "Topal Osman", "Oscar S. Heizer", "Giacomo Gorrini", "Hafız Mehmet"], "sources_primary": ["Heizer, report of 11 April 1919 (US National Archives RG 59)", "Gorrini, interview in Il Messaggero, 25 Aug 1915", "Hafız Mehmet in the Ottoman Chamber of Deputies, Dec 1918", "Trabzon court-martial records, Takvim-i Vekayi no. 3616 (6 Aug 1919)", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.754–760 (account of a participant)"], "sources_modern": ["Kévorkian 2011", "Akçam, The Young Turks' Crime against Humanity (Princeton UP, 2012), p. 199 (the deputy Hafız Mehmed Emin in the Chamber, 11 Dec 1918: Armenians put on boats and thrown into the sea), pp. 312-313 (testimony at the Trebizond court-martial, 3 April 1919; the verdict of 22 May 1919 on rape; the Red Crescent hospital), p. 259 (Talat's own register: 34,500 Armenians removed from the province of Trabzon, after Bardakçı 2008, p. 77), pp. 293-296 (conversions at Trebizond; at Ordu 160 of 250 families and at Giresun 200 of 400, by an Armenian party report of August 1915; the vali told would-be converts they would be deported as 'Mohammedan' Armenians, Consul Bergfeld, 9 July 1915) [Turkish-born scholar abroad]", "Dadrian, JGR 5:3 (2003)", "Suny 2015", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 181-184, after Kévorkian, The Armenian Genocide (2011), p. 469 (their n. 45): Şakir in Trabzon c. 22/23 June 1915; the vali tells his staff on 24 June; notables rounded up on 25 June; on 26 June the deportation date is announced for 1 July; the first 2,000 leave in three convoys on 1 July"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The dates: Morris & Ze'evi (pp. 181–184), after Kévorkian (2011, p. 469), give the vali's word to his staff on 24 June, the round-up of the notables on 25 June, the public announcement on 26 June and the first convoys on 1 July 1915, and date an earlier proclamation of the vali to 16 June; Chrysanthos' proclamation of 13 June 1331 (26 June 1915) agrees. The numbers: the Ottoman count of 1914 gives 37,549 Armenians in the vilayet (Karpat, as reprinted by Akçam 2012, p. 262), the Armenian Patriarchate's count more; about 30,000 lived in the city and its district. Talat's register records 34,500 removed (Akçam, p. 259). Chrysanthos' figure of 300 killed at sea is approximate.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Heizer report (US consul); Gorrini interview (Italian consul); Akçam 2012; Suny 2015", "Ottoman state records: Hafız Mehmet in the Ottoman Chamber; Trabzon court-martial verdict (Takvim-i Vekayi 3616)", "Armenian sources: Kévorkian 2011; Dadrian 2003", "Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "arrests, convoys, drownings at sea"}, {"name": "Değirmendere valley (Pyxites), Trebizond", "lat": 40.985, "lon": 39.76, "role": "start of the convoy road inland"}, {"name": "Gümüşhane", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.481, "role": "convoy route inland"}, {"name": "Erzincan", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 39.5, "role": "convoy route; killing sites at Kemah gorge"}, {"name": "Kemah", "lat": 39.602, "lon": 39.033, "role": "Kemah gorge killing site"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "Armenian arrests from April 1915; deportation from July"}]}, {"id": "AK-002", "date": "1915-06", "date_end": "1915-12-22", "date_text": "June – July 1915 (Samsun and Merzifon); reports to December 1915", "precision": "month", "title": "The Armenians of Samsun and Merzifon in 1915, and Armenians hidden in Greek villages", "summary": "Talat's own register of the deportations, published by Murat Bardakçı, gives 26,374 Armenians removed from the district of Samsun (Canik), against 27,058 Armenians in the Ottoman count of 1914. At Samsun the American consul William Peter reported in June 1915 that about 150 families had become Muslims and the rest had been sent inland; the German consul Kuckhoff wrote on 25 June that all the Armenian villages in and around Samsun had been Islamised under threat. Armenians at Ordu and Fatsa, and local imams and muftis, petitioned that converts be spared; an Armenian party report of August 1915 counted 160 of 250 families converted at Ordu and 200 of 400 at Giresun. On 9 July the district governor of Canik, Necmi Bey, reported that the last convoy had left Samsun four days earlier with its clergy and the last group from Alaçam had been sent by Kavak and Havza, and that converts were being settled in scattered places; Talat soon ordered that converts be deported all the same. At Merzifon, according to the missionary J. K. Marden, over 11,500 of 13,000 Armenians were deported and about 1,500 accepted Islam. Another missionary at Merzifon reported in December 1915 that 'thousands' of Armenians were hidden in the Greek mountain villages of her district alone, and the Patriarchate's compilation records that at Nicopolis (Şebinkarahisar) the Greek houses were set on fire to find Armenians hiding in them (p. 98; Akçam, p. 123, has Tokat). At the Trebizond court-martial a Justice Ministry inspector, Kenan Bey, said that he had seen the deportations at Samsun and that the kaymakam of Bafra had been killed (Akçam 2012, pp. 123, 196, 259, 263, 292-296).", "people": ["Necmi Bey", "William Peter", "Kuckhoff", "Kenan Bey", "Frances C. Gage", "J. K. Marden"], "sources_primary": ["[Ottoman document] Talat's register of deported Armenians, in M. Bardakçı, Talat Paşa'nın Evrak-ı Metrukesi (Istanbul 2008), p. 77 (Akçam 2012, p. 259)", "[Ottoman census] 1914, Samsun: 27,058 Armenians, after Karpat, Osmanlı Nüfusu (Akçam 2012, p. 263)", "[US consular agent] William Peter, Samsun, reports of June and 10 July 1915 (Sarafian, ed., United States Official Records; Akçam 2012, pp. 292, 294)", "[German consul] Kuckhoff, Samsun, telegram of 25 June 1915 in his report of 4 July 1915, PA-AA R 14086 (Akçam 2012, p. 292)", "[Ottoman document] petition of Ölmezoğlu Ali Kemal and the muhtar Binyadoğlu Ahmed Niyazi, Ordu, 30 June 1915, BOA DH.EUM 2. Şube 8/61-a/2 (Akçam 2012, p. 295)", "[Armenian party report] Dashnaktsutiun, Balkan section, Sofia, 2 August 1915, forwarded by Morgenthau, NA RG 59 867.4016/122 (Akçam 2012, p. 294)", "[Ottoman document] Necmi Bey, district governor of Canik, cipher of 9 July 1915, BOA DH.EUM 2. Şube 10/78/1 (Akçam 2012, p. 295)", "[Ottoman document] Security Directorate circular of 1 July 1915 (converts to be deported all the same), BOA DH.ŞFR 54/254 (Akçam 2012, p. 296)", "[American missionary] J. K. Marden, statement forwarded from Copenhagen, 3 July 1916, NA RG 59 867.4016/292 (Akçam 2012, p. 293)", "[American missionary] Frances C. Gage, Merzifon, report of 22 December 1915, NA RG 59 867.4016/252 (Akçam 2012, p. 123)", "[Patriarchate compilation] Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (Constantinople 1919), p. 98: Nicopolis (Şebinkarahisar), undated", "[Ottoman court record] Kenan Bey's testimony at the Trebizond court-martial, 11 May 1919 (Akçam 2012, p. 196)"], "sources_modern": ["Akçam, The Young Turks' Crime against Humanity (Princeton UP, 2012), pp. 123, 196, 259, 263, 292-296 [Turkish-born scholar abroad]"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Talat's register counts those removed, not the dead. The Ordu and Giresun conversion figures come from an Armenian party report forwarded by the US ambassador Morgenthau. Akçam quotes Kenan Bey from the trial reports without the kaymakam's name or the date of the killing, in a passage on officials removed for resisting the killings. The missionary's 'thousands' is one estimate for her district. The Nicopolis report is the Patriarchate's (p. 98); Akçam (p. 123) places it at Tokat, but Tokat appears on that page only as where deportees arrived. Greek villagers in the Trebizond hills also fed hidden Armenians (Chrysanthos; H6-011).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Talat's register (Bardakçı); Necmi Bey's cipher; the Ordu petition; the circular of 1 July 1915; Kenan Bey at the court-martial", "Neutral sources: US and German consuls; American missionaries; Akçam 2012", "Armenian sources: Dashnaktsutiun report, August 1915", "Greek sources: Patriarchate compilation (Tokat)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "Armenian deportations completed by early July 1915"}, {"name": "Alaçam", "lat": 41.61, "lon": 35.6, "role": "last convoy, by Kavak and Havza"}, {"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.567, "lon": 35.906, "role": "kaymakam killed (Kenan Bey)"}, {"name": "Ordu", "lat": 40.984, "lon": 37.879, "role": "conversions"}, {"name": "Fatsa", "lat": 41.03, "lon": 37.5, "role": "petitions of converts"}, {"name": "Giresun", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.389, "role": "conversions"}, {"name": "Merzifon", "lat": 40.873, "lon": 35.463, "role": "deportations; Armenians hidden in Greek villages"}, {"name": "Şebinkarahisar (Nicopolis)", "lat": 40.288, "lon": 38.424, "role": "Greek houses set on fire to find hidden Armenians (Patriarchate p. 98)"}]}, {"id": "H6-014", "date": "1916-01", "date_end": "1916-09", "date_text": "Winter – summer 1916", "precision": "month", "title": "Deportations of Greek villages behind the Ottoman lines in the Trebizond vilayet", "summary": "As the Ottomans fall back before the Russian offensive, the regional commander Kâmil Pasha orders the deportation of Greek villagers immediately behind his lines; they are driven out in midwinter and some freeze on the roads ('We must suffer, so must you'). In spring and summer the inhabitants of dozens of villages of the Trebizond vilayet are deported. A Greek Legation report says that on 15 April 1916 the people of sixteen villages of the Vazelon district were ordered to march to Argyroupolis; fearing the fate of the Armenians on that road, about 6,000 fled to the forests: 650 sheltered in the Vazelon monastery, where they found 130 refugees from Trebizond, and 1,200 hid in a cave near Kounaka until hunger forced them out, after which 26 young women and girls threw themselves into the river near Gephyra. Other Greek reports say women were taken to Vazelon and killed there. An Ottoman cable to Trebizond of 15 May 1916 set the rule for the coast: since a 'benign and tolerant policy' was being followed toward Greece, the coastal Greeks were not all to be moved inland, only those in places judged unsafe for military reasons, and those moved were to be sent to Kastamonu and to districts of Sivas without Muslim refugees (Akçam 2012, pp. 106-107). An eyewitness who reached the monastery after the Russian advance found it wrecked, with ten unburied bodies, among them the priest Constantine of Thersa and a young woman of the same village (Brown & Ion 1918, pp. 45-46).", "people": ["Mahmut Kâmil Pasha", "Mahmud Kâmil Pasha", "Chrysanthos, Metropolitan of Trebizond"], "sources_primary": ["An American missionary to 'friends', Erzurum, 26 Feb 1916, Houghton ABC 16.9.7 (Kâmil Pasha's order; via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 50)", "Greek Legation, Petrograd, report no. 1570, 30 Aug 1916 (Ministerial Archives no. 9067), in Brown & Ion 1918, pp. 45-46", "Greek Patriarchate, Persecution of the Greeks (1919), pp. 110–111 (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 51)", "[American missionary] letter to 'friends', Erzurum, 26 February 1916, Houghton ABC 16.9.7, A467, Reel 716 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 50)", "[Patriarchate compilation] Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (Constantinople, 1919), p. 113, as cited by Travis, AHIF Policy Journal 8 (2017)", "[Ottoman document] Interior Ministry, General Directorate of Security, to the province of Trebizond, 15 May 1916, BOA DH.ŞFR 64/29 (Akçam 2012, pp. 106-107)", "[Patriarchate compilation] Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (1919), pp. 109-111 (Rodopolis)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 389", "Grigoryan, 'Mass Suicide during the Greek Genocide in the Ottoman Empire, 1913–1923', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide 1913–1923: New Perspectives (2019), p. 281", "Minasidis 2024, citing Grigoryan 2019", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 389", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (New York and Athens: Caratzas, 2011), p. 62", "Travis, 'On the Centenary of the Greek Genocide', AHIF Policy Journal 8 (Spring 2017), p. 3 and n. 24 (the Metropolitan of Trabzon: 'more than 90%' of the Greeks from Rize and Tripoli did not survive; n. 24 = Patriarchate, Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (1919), p. 113)", "Akçam, The Young Turks' Crime against Humanity (Princeton UP, 2012), pp. 106-107 [Turkish-born scholar abroad]", "Brown & Ion (trans.), Persecutions of the Greeks in Turkey since the Beginning of the European War (New York: Oxford UP for the American-Hellenic Society, 1918), pp. 45-46 and p. 66 (the table: sixteen villages of the Vazelon district, 6,000 people) [Greek government documents in translation]"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Kâmil Pasha's order rests on the letter of an American missionary in Erzurum (Morris & Ze'evi), which does not name Rize or Trebizond. The Vazelon, Kounaka and Gephyra details rest on Greek reports only. The Legation report (Brown & Ion 1918, p. 45) has 130 refugees from Trebizond at Vazelon and 1,200 people in the cave at Kounaka (probably Kunaka, today Bağışlı in Maçka; Nişanyan). The Patriarchate's account of Rodopolis (p. 109) has 200 people in the cave at 'Kaneka', and the 26 women drowning themselves 'while passing over a bridge'; since γέφυρα is Greek for bridge, 'Gephyra' may not be a village. It dates the emptying of Vazelon (its monks, 780 refugees and 29 Armenians) to 22 April 1916 and the pillage of Soumela to 19 April (p. 110), and, on the word of Metropolitan Cyril (12 November 1918), gives 487 people hiding in the mountains and caves of Rodopolis massacred (p. 111). The Ottoman cable of 15 May 1916 agrees with what Talat told the German ambassador Radowitz in October 1916, that a removal of all the coastal Greeks had been planned and stopped (H7-054).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: American missionary letter, Erzurum, 26 Feb 1916 (via Morris & Ze'evi 2019, p. 389)", "Greek sources: Greek Legation report of 30 Aug 1916; Greek Patriarchate 1919; Grigoryan 2019 (transmitter); Minasidis 2024", "Greek sources: Metropolitan of Trebizond (Patriarchate compilation, 1919, via Travis 2017)", "Neutral sources: Morris & Ze'evi 2019 (Kâmil Pasha's order)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Vazelon monastery", "lat": 40.766, "lon": 39.628, "role": "atrocities reported, 1916"}, {"name": "Maçka (Matsouka)", "lat": 40.813, "lon": 39.613, "role": "Matsouka district villages"}, {"name": "Erzurum", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.266, "role": "taken by Russia, 16 Feb 1916; source of the missionary letter"}, {"name": "Rize", "lat": 41.025, "lon": 40.517, "role": "taken by Russia, March 1916; deportees from"}, {"name": "Trabzon (Trapezous)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "taken by Russia, 18 April 1916"}, {"name": "Tirebolu (Tripolis)", "lat": 41.006, "lon": 38.815, "role": "deportees from"}, {"name": "Görele (Elevi)", "lat": 41.031, "lon": 39.003, "role": "deportees from (Hofmann: Elevi and Tripolis area)"}, {"name": "Kounakia (Konaka/Kunaka; today Bağışlı, Maçka)", "lat": 40.7206, "lon": 39.5209, "role": "cave nearby where 1,200 hid, 1916 (identification probable: Nişanyan, old name Konaka/Kunaka 1876)"}]}, {"id": "C3-038", "date": "1916-02", "date_end": "1916-03-22", "date_text": "February 1916 (Rize); March 1916 (Chaldia; report of 10 March, letter of 22 March)", "precision": "month", "title": "Chrysanthos' account: arrests of Greek men at Rize and in Chaldia, reversed on his appeals", "summary": "Chrysanthos writes that in February 1916, as the Russians neared Rize, the governor of Rize arrested in one day all the Greek men of the town aged 15–70 to send them to Trebizond; the metropolis took this 'ὡς ἀπαρχὴν γενικοῦ ἐκτοπισμοῦ τῶν Ἑλλήνων τοῦ Πόντου' (as the beginning of a general deportation of the Greeks of Pontus) and had them freed. In March 1916 the same happened 'in a harsher form' at Argyroupolis, Ardasa and Kromni; all were released after appeals to the vali and telegrams to Vehip Pasha, commander of the Third Army.", "people": ["Chrysanthos", "Cemal Azmi", "Vehip Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.753–754 (account of a participant)", "Archive of Metropolitan Chrysanthos, nos. 40–43 (report of 10 March 1916; letter of 22 March 1916), as cited by him"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, pp.753–754"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Rizaion (Rize)", "lat": 41.0208, "lon": 40.5219, "role": "Greek men aged 15–70 arrested"}, {"name": "Argyroupolis (Gümüşhane, old town)", "lat": 40.4598, "lon": 39.4778, "role": "arrests"}, {"name": "Ardasa (Torul)", "lat": 40.55657, "lon": 39.29246, "role": "arrests"}, {"name": "Kromni (Kurum)", "lat": 40.562, "lon": 39.5923, "role": "arrests"}]}, {"id": "H6-015", "date": "1916-02-05", "date_end": "1916-03-09", "date_text": "5 Feb – 9 Mar 1916", "precision": "day", "title": "The Russian coastal offensive: Arhavi to the İyidere", "summary": "General Lyakhov's coastal detachment, supported by the Black Sea Fleet, attacks the Ottoman line at Arhavi on 5 February 1916, and the Ottomans order a withdrawal on 6 February. The Russians reach the Viçe (Vitze, Fındıklı) line on 7 February; the line there breaks on 15-16 February. They land behind the Ottoman positions at Atina (Pazar) on 4 March and Mapavri (Çayeli) on 5 March. Rize is taken on 6 March (Ottoman documents; 8 March in Russian-based accounts), and the front reaches the İyidere (Kalopotamos), the Trebizond-Lazistan boundary, on 9 March.", "people": ["Nikolai Yudenich", "V. P. Lyakhov", "Vehip Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["Ottoman 3rd Army records used by Bilgin 2022 (ATASE BDH-5262-23-011; BDH-2909-450-036; BDH-5263-28-076)", "Russian staff accounts used by Allen & Muratoff"], "sources_modern": ["Bilgin, Karadeniz Araştırmaları Enstitüsü Dergisi 8/16 (2022), 641–655", "Allen & Muratoff 1953"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Rize fell on 6 March by the Ottoman military archives (Bilgin; the Ottoman reports are dated 7 March) or on 8 March (Fevzi Çakmak); Bilgin (n. 68) takes the difference for a calendar slip. Enver's telegram puts the landing at Atina on 5 March, not 4 March. Mapavri fell on 5 March by the Ottoman documents, on 6 or 7 March in other accounts.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: ATASE records used by Bilgin 2022", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Bilgin 2022 (dates)", "Neutral sources: Allen & Muratoff 1953"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Arhavi", "lat": 41.351, "lon": 41.305, "role": "waypoint 1: Ottoman line broken, 5–6 Feb 1916"}, {"name": "Fındıklı (Vitze)", "lat": 41.272, "lon": 41.142, "role": "waypoint 2: 15–16 Feb 1916"}, {"name": "Pazar (Atina)", "lat": 41.179, "lon": 40.885, "role": "waypoint 3: Russian landing, 4 Mar 1916"}, {"name": "Çayeli (Mapavri)", "lat": 41.09, "lon": 40.729, "role": "waypoint 4: Russian landing, 5 Mar 1916"}, {"name": "Rize", "lat": 41.025, "lon": 40.517, "role": "waypoint 5: occupied 6 Mar 1916"}, {"name": "İyidere (Kalopotamos) river mouth", "lat": 41.01, "lon": 40.36, "role": "waypoint 6: front line, 9 Mar 1916"}]}, {"id": "H6-016", "date": "1916-02-16", "date_text": "16 February 1916", "precision": "day", "title": "Russians take Erzurum", "summary": "Yudenich's Caucasus Army storms the Erzurum forts. The fall of Erzurum makes Trebizond indefensible from the landward side, and the vali Cemal Azmi begins to plan the evacuation of the city.", "people": ["Nikolai Yudenich"], "sources_modern": ["Allen & Muratoff 1953", "Çakıcı, KAREN 4/6 (2018), 331–345"], "confidence": "high", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Allen & Muratoff 1953", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Çakıcı 2018 (date)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Erzurum", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.268, "role": "taken by Russia"}]}, {"id": "H6-017", "date": "1916-04", "date_end": "1916-08", "date_text": "Spring – summer 1916", "precision": "month", "title": "Muslim flight from the Russian-occupied zone", "summary": "Tens of thousands of Muslims of Lazistan, Trebizond and Gümüşhane flee west ahead of the Russian army, by sea and over the mountains, to Giresun, Ordu, Samsun, Sinop and further inland (Merzifon, Çorum, Ankara, Konya, Niğde). Chrysanthos, who stayed in Trebizond, confirms that much of the city's Muslim population left (H6-018).", "sources_primary": ["Chrysanthos, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (1933) (the flight from Trebizond)", "Ottoman refugee (muhacir) records used by Öksüz & Usta 2008 (destinations)"], "sources_modern": ["Çakıcı 2018 (destinations)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The death tolls and population figures for the flight come from Turkish works (Kaya 2002; Öksüz & Usta 2008; Erdeha 1975) and from McCarthy (1998), all as cited by Çakıcı (2018), and are given under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H6-T02). We have not found an independent count of the deaths.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933 (the flight from Trebizond)", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Ottoman refugee records via Öksüz & Usta 2008 and Çakıcı 2018 (destinations)", "Neutral sources"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "origin"}, {"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.39, "role": "refugee destination"}, {"name": "Ordu (Kotyora)", "lat": 40.984, "lon": 37.879, "role": "refugee destination"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "refugee destination"}, {"name": "Merzifon", "lat": 40.873, "lon": 35.463, "role": "refugee destination"}, {"name": "Konya", "lat": 37.872, "lon": 32.485, "role": "refugee destination"}]}, {"id": "H6-018", "date": "1916-04-15", "date_end": "1916-04-18", "date_text": "15–18 April 1916 (the Russians enter on the evening of 18 April)", "precision": "day", "title": "Trebizond handed to Chrysanthos; the Russians enter", "summary": "The Ottoman troops withdraw from Trebizond (night of 15/16 April, after Walton). On the night of Palm Sunday, 3/16 April, the vali Cemal Azmi hands the city by a written order to a provisional government under Metropolitan Chrysanthos, who receives it at the vali's villa at Kryoneri, and leaves around midnight; much of the Muslim population flees west by sea and land. Russian troops enter on the evening of 18 April 1916 (5 April Old Style); Chrysanthos receives them, and the Russians keep him as the head of the civil administration of the city.", "people": ["Chrysanthos (Philippidis), Metropolitan of Trebizond", "Cemal Azmi", "V. P. Lyakhov"], "sources_primary": ["Chrysanthos, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5, 1933)", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.762–764 (account of a participant); order of the vali, 3/16 April 1916 (facsimile, fig. 114)"], "sources_modern": ["Çakıcı 2018 (18 April)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "By Walton's account the troops left on the night of 15/16 April (1984, pp. 1306–13, known to us only as cited on Wikipedia); Chrysanthos has the vali hand over and leave on the night of Palm Sunday, 3/16 April. Both may be right if the troops left a night before the vali. The Russians entered on 18 April New Style, 5 April Old Style (Çakıcı; Allen & Muratoff). Hofmann's '16 April' is the evacuation, not the entry.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933", "Neutral sources: Walton 1984 (as cited on Wikipedia); Allen & Muratoff", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Çakıcı 2018 (date)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "evacuated 15/16 April; Russian entry 18 April 1916"}, {"name": "Akçaabat (Platana)", "lat": 41.021, "lon": 39.571, "role": "Ottoman retreat road west"}]}, {"id": "H6-019", "date": "1916-04-18", "date_end": "1918-02-23", "date_text": "April 1916 – February 1918", "precision": "month", "title": "The Russian occupation and Chrysanthos' administration", "summary": "Under the Russian military government Chrysanthos runs the civil affairs of Trebizond. Greek accounts say he helped Muslims who stayed or returned to recover their property and institutions, and organised relief across communities; about 500 Armenian survivors and the monks of Kaymaklı return. Greek schools and the press revive. A Catholic missionary wrote in March 1917 that the Catholics enjoyed liberty and gentle treatment under the occupation. In a letter in Greek, signed by him and dated 31 January 1918 by Greek sources, the Ottoman commander Vehip Pasha thanked Chrysanthos for his 'wholly paternal and willing care and protection' of 'the Ottoman element' since the Russian entry (Chrysanthos prints it in facsimile). Chrysanthos's own account (pp. 764-766): he protected Turks against Armenian volunteers 'thirsting for revenge', resettled 'tens of thousands' of Muslim refugees in their villages of Rize, Of, Sourmena, Gemura, Matsouka and Platana and fed 'other tens of thousands' for two years, and set up mixed Greek-Muslim courts and a local guard.", "people": ["Chrysanthos", "Nikolai Yudenich", "Chrysanthos, Metropolitan of Trebizond"], "sources_primary": ["Chrysanthos 1933", "Greek Patriarchate, Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914–1918 (1919)", "Vehip Pasha to Chrysanthos, 31 Jan 1918, as quoted in Le Martyre du Pont-Euxin et l'opinion publique internationale (Geneva 1922; Greek advocacy compilation, preface by A. Wuarin), p. 9, after Chrysanthos' memorandum 'La Question du Pont-Euxin' to the Peace Conference, 2 May 1919", "F. Lorenzo da Montemarciano to the General Superintendent of the Capuchins, Trebizond, 15 March 1917 (AGC H 97), via Kyriakides (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias 2026; Greek ed. 2015), p. 57", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.678; p.748; pp.764–766 (account of a participant)", "Vehip Pasha to Chrysanthos, letter in Greek signed by Vehip, facsimile in Chrysanthos 1933, fig. 117 (116) (four pages, no visible date), transcribed pp. 767-769: 'ἡ ὅλως πατρικὴ καὶ πρόφρων μέριμνα καὶ προστασία... ἀπέναντι τοῦ ὀθωμανικοῦ στοιχείου'"], "sources_modern": ["Kyriakides (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias 2026; Greek ed. 2015), pp. 57, 61 (citing Chrysanthos 1933, pp. 764–772)", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 389 (Trabzon taken 18 April 1916)", "Erickson 2001, p. 183 (as cited on Wikipedia)", "Kyriakidis (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias Press, 2026), p. 57 (Catholic testimony that conditions for Christians in Trebizond improved under Russian occupation: Lorenzo da Montemarciano to the General Superintendent, 15 March 1917)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Vehip Pasha's letter, which Chrysanthos prints in facsimile (fig. 116), carries no visible date; '31 January 1918' comes from Chrysanthos' memorandum to the Peace Conference (1919) and Le Martyre du Pont-Euxin (1922, p. 9), whose 'noble... protection' of 'the Turkish element' loosens the Greek 'wholly paternal and willing care and protection' of 'the Ottoman element'. The Catholic letter of 1917 confirms the good conditions for Catholics, not Chrysanthos' role. The 'tens of thousands' are Chrysanthos' phrases, not counts. Turkish historians' accounts of Greek and Armenian bands burning Turkish houses in the occupied countryside are given under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H6-T01); we found no non-Turkish source for them.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933; Greek Patriarchate 1919", "Neutral sources", "Greek sources: Martyre du Pont-Euxin 1922, p. 9 (Vehip's letter, quoted from Chrysanthos' memorandum of 1919)", "Neutral sources: Capuchin letter of 15 March 1917 (via Kyriakides & Nikoloudis 2026, p. 57)", "Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933", "Neutral sources: Morris & Ze'evi 2019; Erickson 2001; Capuchin mission records (via Kyriakidis 2026)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "seat of the occupation"}, {"name": "Kaymaklı monastery, Trebizond", "lat": 40.985, "lon": 39.745, "role": "Armenian monks return, 1916"}, {"name": "Rize", "lat": 41.025, "lon": 40.517, "role": "occupied zone"}, {"name": "Gümüşhane", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.481, "role": "occupied zone"}, {"name": "Trabzon (Trapezous)", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "Russian-held"}, {"name": "Harşit river mouth (front line 1916-18, approx.)", "lat": 41.03, "lon": 38.87, "role": "approximate front line"}]}, {"id": "H6-020", "date": "1916-04-26", "date_text": "26 April 1916", "precision": "day", "title": "Sazonov–Paléologue agreement: Trebizond promised to Russia", "summary": "In the Franco-Russian exchange that completed the Sykes–Picot settlement, Russia is promised Erzurum, Trebizond, Van and Bitlis, with a boundary on the Black Sea west of Trebizond to be fixed later. The Russian annexation plans, not Greek autonomy, framed the occupation.", "people": ["Sergei Sazonov", "Maurice Paléologue"], "sources_primary": ["Sazonov–Paléologue notes, April 1916", "Russian Ministry of Foreign Affairs, 'Memorandum... on the Question of Asia Minor', 21 Feb / 6 Mar 1917, published by the Soviet government and transmitted by Ambassador Francis, Petrograd, 5 Dec 1917: FRUS 1917, Supplement 2, vol. I, doc. 416, enclosure 6 ('Russia acquires the provinces of Erzerum, Trebizond, Van and Bitlis'; the coast boundary at 'a point west of Trebizond to be determined later'; agreement reached 'during the spring of 1916')"], "sources_modern": ["McMeekin 2015", "Hovannisian, The Republic of Armenia vol. 1"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The terms are in the Russian Foreign Ministry memorandum of 21 February / 6 March 1917, published by the Soviets (FRUS 1917, Supplement 2, vol. I, doc. 416, enclosure 6): Russia acquires Erzurum, Trebizond, Van and Bitlis, with the coast boundary at 'a point west of Trebizond to be determined later', agreed 'during the spring of 1916'. The day, 26 April 1916, is that of the secondary literature (Earle 1923, p. 292; Hovannisian 1968), known to us as cited on Wikipedia.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Sazonov-Paléologue notes; McMeekin 2015", "Armenian sources: Hovannisian vol. 1"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Petrograd", "lat": 59.939, "lon": 30.316, "role": "where the notes were exchanged"}, {"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "promised to Russia"}]}, {"id": "H6-022", "date": "1916-07", "date_end": "1918-02", "date_text": "July 1916 – February 1918", "precision": "month", "title": "The front settles on the Harşit; Gümüşhane, Bayburt and Erzincan fall", "summary": "In July 1916 the Russians take Gümüşhane, Bayburt and Erzincan (25 July), and the coastal force reaches the Harşit river at Tirebolu. The Ottoman Third Army holds the west bank of the Harşit, its last defensive line, until the Russian army dissolves. Tirebolu and its villages sit in the front zone, which shaped the deportations there.", "people": ["Nikolai Yudenich", "Vehip Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["Ottoman 3rd Army records (ATASE) used by Yazıcı 2020"], "sources_modern": ["Yazıcı, Birinci Dünya Savaşı'nda Harşit Savunması (MA thesis, Giresun University, 2020)", "Allen & Muratoff 1953"], "confidence": "high", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: ATASE records used by Yazıcı 2020", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Yazıcı 2020", "Neutral sources: Allen & Muratoff 1953"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Harşit river mouth, Tirebolu", "lat": 41.02, "lon": 38.85, "role": "front line waypoint 1 (coast), July 1916 – Feb 1918"}, {"name": "Doğankent (Harşit valley)", "lat": 40.806, "lon": 38.917, "role": "front line waypoint 2 (approximate)"}, {"name": "Kürtün", "lat": 40.7, "lon": 39.1, "role": "front line waypoint 3 (approximate)"}, {"name": "Gümüşhane", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.481, "role": "Russian, mid-July 1916"}, {"name": "Bayburt", "lat": 40.255, "lon": 40.225, "role": "Russian, mid-July 1916"}, {"name": "Kelkit", "lat": 40.128, "lon": 39.437, "role": "front line waypoint 4 (Russian until 7 Feb 1918)"}, {"name": "Erzincan", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 39.5, "role": "front line waypoint 5: Russian from 25 July 1916"}, {"name": "Kemah", "lat": 39.602, "lon": 39.033, "role": "front line waypoint 6 (Erzincan–Kemah passage)"}]}, {"id": "H6-023", "date": "1916-07", "date_end": "1917", "date_text": "July 1916; court-martial later in 1916", "precision": "month", "title": "Topal Osman's arrests in Giresun", "summary": "According to Sigalas, Topal Osman, the Giresun band leader of the Armenian deportations, arrested Orthodox merchants in Giresun as 'spies'; one, Ioannis Deligiorgis, died in custody. Unusually, after the vali Cemal Azmi and the mutasarrıf of Giresun complained that he was interfering in government affairs, Osman was summoned before the court-martial at Sivas and held under guard for a time (Ottoman army records). A Greek metropolitan's letter of 15 December 1916 describes a raid by 'Osman Aga', an agent of the vali Cemal Azmi, on the village of Prossori 'about a month' before the letter, so about November 1916: his men plundered the houses, raped the women, 'murdered four young Greeks, and beat the parish priest to death', then forced the peasants to sign a document certifying that the murderers were Armenians. The same letter, by the Metropolitan of Chaldia, writing from Kerasous, says that the merchant Iordanni Sourmeli was seized at night by members of the CUP, and that the rich merchant Ioanni Deliyorghi was taken by a band of about 150, accused on anonymous letters of signalling to Russian ships, and died in custody at the court-martial of Tirebolu; the kaymakam Rechid Bey protested and was replaced by Nihad Bey (Patriarchate 1919, pp. 103-104).", "people": ["Topal Osman (Osman Ağa Feridunoğlu)", "Ioannis Deligiorgis"], "sources_primary": ["Greek Patriarchate, Persecution of the Greeks (1919), pp. 100–105, quoting a metropolitan's letter of 15 Dec 1916 (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 53)", "[Patriarchate compilation] Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (1919), pp. 102-107: letter of the Metropolitan of Chaldia, Kerasous, dated 15 December 1916 (printed '1910')"], "sources_modern": ["Sigalas 2026, '1914-1918-online' (as cited on Wikipedia)", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 390", "Çetinoğlu & Cibran, The Pontos Question (English summary, January 2007) [Turkish writers], section 'Western Pontos', quoting Balcıoğlu 2001, p. 178, after ATASE folder 1888, doc. 362, files 41 and 43 (complaints of Cemal Azmi and the mutasarrıf of Giresun; Sivas court-martial; date printed '25 August 1936')"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The Prossori raid and the arrests of the merchants rest on the Metropolitan of Chaldia's letter, printed by the Patriarchate (1919, pp. 102–107) and also in the Greek government's collection (Brown & Ion 1918, pp. 17–18, 'a report of 1917 from Kerasounda'), so the two are one witness; Morris & Ze'evi (p. 390) repeat it. The letter does not say that Osman arrested the merchants; it names him afterwards as the vali's agent in the villages (pp. 104–105). Prossori is probably the Prassari of the book's list of villages (p. 107). That Osman was behind the Giresun arrests is Sigalas' account, known to us only as cited on Wikipedia. The court-martial is confirmed by Ottoman army records (ATASE folder 1888, doc. 362, files 41 and 43), cited by Balcıoğlu (2001, p. 178) and quoted by the Turkish writers Çetinoğlu & Cibran (2007); they give the reason as his interference in government affairs, not the arrests of Greeks, and print the date as '25 August 1936', evidently a misprint, perhaps for 25 August 1332 (7 September 1916).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Greek Patriarchate 1919 (metropolitan's letter of 15 Dec 1916); Sigalas 2026 (1914-1918-online)", "Neutral sources: Morris & Ze'evi 2019, p. 390 (transmitter)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.39, "role": "arrests, July 1916"}]}, {"id": "C3-041", "date": "1916-07-30", "date_text": "17/30 July 1916", "precision": "day", "title": "Chrysanthos' account: Grand Duke Nicholas asks him to sound out the Turks on peace", "summary": "Chrysanthos writes that Grand Duke Nicholas Nikolaevich, visiting Trebizond on 17/30 July 1916, confided his fear of revolution and his worry about an ally's gains in Mesopotamia, and asked him to sound the Turkish government confidentially on peace talks; he did so, without result.", "people": ["Chrysanthos", "Grand Duke Nicholas Nikolaevich"], "sources_primary": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.766 (account of a participant)"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "Known only from Chrysanthos.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, p.766"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "Grand Duke's visit"}]}, {"id": "H6-024", "date": "1916-11-16", "date_end": "1917-03", "date_text": "16 Nov 1916 – March 1917", "precision": "day", "title": "Deportation of the Greeks of Tripolis (Tirebolu) and Elevi", "summary": "With the front on the Harşit, the Greeks of the Tirebolu and Elevi (Görele) districts are marched inland in winter. The route ran south over the Pontic range to Şebinkarahisar (Nikopolis), Koyulhisar and Suşehri; survivors were scattered between Karahisar and Tokat (Reşadiye, Niksar, Erbaa, Amasya) and held there until August 1918. The Greek Legation's table of January 1917 lists 3,000 deported from Tripoli itself and 29 villages of its sub-district (from 157 to 1,650 people each), 800 from Elevi and 580 from Satou-Maden (Brown & Ion 1918, p. 67); the figure for each village is given in the Villages section. Metropolitan Chrysanthos wrote on 12 October 1918 that the Christians left in Elevi and Tripoli were driven to Sivas in midwinter over snow-covered mountains: of 1,250 people of Elevi 150 survived, of 2,300 of Tripoli about 200, and of 30,000 Christians of the Tripoli district 1,500 to 2,000 (Patriarchate 1919, p. 113). The Patriarchate's account of Chaldia gives 35,000 expelled from the Tripoli and Kerasous regions by the end of 1916, with 2,000 Tripoli refugees at the Aksu refused bread by the acting governor Nihad Bey (p. 107).", "sources_primary": ["Valavanis, Σύγχρονος γενική ιστορία του Πόντου (1925)", "Sakkas (route account)", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.765 (account of a participant)", "[Greek Legation table] deported up to January 1917: Tripoli 3,000 and 29 villages of the Tripoli sub-district; Elevi 800 (Brown & Ion 1918, p. 67)", "[Patriarchate compilation] Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (1919), pp. 107, 113 (Chrysanthos's letter of 12 Oct 1918)"], "sources_modern": ["IMOPH series 'Η γενοκτονία των Ελλήνων του Πόντου 1916–1923', part D (2017)", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), p. 62 (16,750 inhabitants of Elevi and Tripolis, of whom 550 survived; no source given on the page)", "Kelly, 'Shared Intent in a Collapsing Empire', in Akçam, Kyriakidis et al. (eds), The Genocide of the Christian Populations in the Ottoman Empire and its Aftermath (Routledge 2023), p. 239, after Fotiadis, The Genocide of the Pontian Greeks (2019), pp. 182-183 (13,000 Greeks of Tripolis set out for Birk; 800 remained three months later)", "Brown & Ion (trans.), Persecutions of the Greeks in Turkey since the Beginning of the European War (New York: Oxford UP for the American-Hellenic Society, 1918), p. 67 [Greek government documents in translation]"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The figures conflict. Valavanis gives 15,195 Greeks before and about 12,000 after, a loss of a fifth; Hofmann (2011, p. 62) gives 16,750, of whom only 550 survived, without a footnote; Kelly (2023, p. 239, after Fotiadis 2019, pp. 182–183) has 13,000 Greeks of Tripolis set out for Birk, 'a village in Armenia whose residents had been slaughtered', and 800 left three months later. Chrysanthos' letter of 1918 implies a loss of more than nine-tenths, as Travis reckons it (2017, p. 3, n. 24); it was printed in a compilation prepared for the peace settlement. Tamzara is located (Nişanyan); Birk was not found. The Patriarchate's book prints the writer of the letter as 'Chryssostomos', an error for Chrysanthos.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Valavanis 1925; Sakkas (route account); IMOPH series (2017)", "Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Tirebolu (Tripolis)", "lat": 41.006, "lon": 38.815, "role": "waypoint 1: origin"}, {"name": "Espiye", "lat": 40.948, "lon": 38.708, "role": "waypoint 2"}, {"name": "Şebinkarahisar (Nikopolis, Karahisar)", "lat": 40.288, "lon": 38.424, "role": "waypoint 3"}, {"name": "Koyulhisar", "lat": 40.3, "lon": 37.828, "role": "waypoint 4"}, {"name": "Suşehri", "lat": 40.165, "lon": 38.087, "role": "waypoint 5"}, {"name": "Reşadiye", "lat": 40.393, "lon": 37.343, "role": "dispersal area"}, {"name": "Niksar (Neocaesarea)", "lat": 40.592, "lon": 36.952, "role": "dispersal area"}, {"name": "Erbaa", "lat": 40.669, "lon": 36.568, "role": "dispersal area"}, {"name": "Tokat", "lat": 40.314, "lon": 36.554, "role": "dispersal area"}, {"name": "Amasya (Amaseia)", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "dispersal area"}, {"name": "Tamzara (Şebinkarahisar)", "lat": 40.3285, "lon": 38.435, "role": "intermediate station (Nişanyan)"}]}, {"id": "PT-001", "date": "1916-11", "date_end": "1917-07", "date_text": "November 1916 – July 1917", "precision": "month", "title": "The Patriarchate's record: villages of the diocese of Kolonia expelled at a few hours' notice", "summary": "The Patriarchate's compilation records that in November 1916, on the order of a governor it calls Assaf, the Greeks of Valdjana and Troupsi were expelled at three hours' notice and driven to Tokat, where the men were separated and dispersed among Turkish villages; a widow was killed in front of her six children and a man who protested was cut to pieces. The villages of the Alucra region were emptied at the same time, their people fleeing to Sivas, Çankırı and Kastamonu. In July 1917 Kadıköy and six other villages of the Refahiye region were given two days to leave and dispersed in the Sivas villages, and four villages of the Abeş region were expelled; eleven more were partly emptied. Villages around Suşehri and Mesudiye were not evacuated but were terrorised by bands raised by the troop commander Feizi Bey. Allowed home in 1917, the Valdjana and Troupsi people were attacked by brigands and fled again (Patriarchate 1919, pp. 97-99). Each village's fate is given in the Villages section.", "sources_primary": ["[Patriarchate compilation] Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (Constantinople 1919), pp. 97-99"], "sources_modern": ["Greek Patriarchate, Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (Constantinople, 1919), English edition, Part III (Pontus) [Patriarchate compilation, Greek, 1919, for the peace settlement]"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "This rests on one Greek compilation, in which some dates are misprinted (the arrival at Tokat is dated '26th December, 1918'). We found no other source on these villages.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Patriarchate compilation (1919)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Şebinkarahisar", "lat": 40.288, "lon": 38.424, "role": "see of the diocese (Nicopolis)"}, {"name": "Tokat", "lat": 40.314, "lon": 36.554, "role": "deportees from Valdjana and Troupsi"}, {"name": "Alucra", "lat": 40.318, "lon": 38.765, "role": "region emptied"}, {"name": "Refahiye", "lat": 39.901, "lon": 38.77, "role": "region: villages expelled July 1917"}, {"name": "Suşehri", "lat": 40.164, "lon": 38.084, "role": "not evacuated; bands"}]}, {"id": "PT-002", "date": "1916-02", "date_end": "1916-07-08", "date_text": "February – 8 July 1916", "precision": "month", "title": "The Patriarchate's record: Chaldia in 1916, the villages of Torul and Argyroupolis plundered, Cheriana and Kelkit deported", "summary": "After the fall of Erzurum, Muslim refugees from Argyroupolis and Ardasa, joined by others from Trebizond, Yomra and Platana, gathered at the Turkish village of Monastir near Ardasa and plundered the Christian villages around it. The Patriarchate's compilation names more than thirty villages of the Torul and Argyroupolis region whose people fled to the mountains while their houses and churches were plundered and burnt, with killings in many (sixty at Simikli, twenty at Desmena, fifteen each at Sarandar and Gargaena). Before Ardasa fell to the Russians on 8 July 1916, the Christian houses and shops there were burnt, and the remaining people of Kurtakous were deported to Sivas and Tokat; the rest of the region came under Russian protection. In the Cheriana and Kelkit region, all but 200 of the people of sixteen villages were deported to Sivas during the Russian advance, and their houses, churches and schools plundered and burnt (Patriarchate 1919, pp. 100-101). Each village's fate is given in the Villages section.", "sources_primary": ["[Patriarchate compilation] Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (Constantinople 1919), pp. 100-101"], "sources_modern": ["Greek Patriarchate, Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (Constantinople, 1919), English edition, Part III (Pontus) [Patriarchate compilation, Greek, 1919, for the peace settlement]"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "This rests on one Greek compilation; the Black Book (1922) prints the same village lists, so it is not an independent witness.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Patriarchate compilation (1919)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Torul (Ardasa)", "lat": 40.556, "lon": 39.293, "role": "fell to the Russians 8 July 1916"}, {"name": "Gümüşhane (Argyroupolis)", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.481, "role": "region"}, {"name": "Şiran (Cheriana)", "lat": 40.19, "lon": 39.122, "role": "sixteen villages deported to Sivas"}, {"name": "Kelkit", "lat": 40.125, "lon": 39.437, "role": "region"}, {"name": "Sivas", "lat": 39.748, "lon": 37.017, "role": "destination"}]}, {"id": "PT-004", "date": "1914", "date_end": "1918-11-08", "date_text": "1914–1918 (priests' report of 8 November 1918)", "precision": "year", "title": "The Stavriote priests' report: the Stavriotes of Akdağmadeni registered as Muslims during the war", "summary": "A report of the Stavriote priests of Akdağmadeni, dated 8 November 1918, says that during the war the Stavriotes whose Christian registration had not been recognised were conscripted as Muslims, and that about 200 families let themselves be registered as Muslims to receive relief; even a priest, Joanni, was entered as a Muslim (Patriarchate 1919, pp. 117-118). It continues the story of the crypto-Christians' registration under Christian names in 1909-10 (H5-051).", "sources_primary": ["[Patriarchate compilation] Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (Constantinople 1919), pp. 117-118"], "sources_modern": ["Greek Patriarchate, Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (Constantinople, 1919), English edition, Part III (Pontus) [Patriarchate compilation, Greek, 1919, for the peace settlement]"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "This rests on one report, printed by the Patriarchate; we found no Ottoman record of the registrations.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Patriarchate compilation (1919)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Akdağmadeni", "lat": 39.66, "lon": 35.884, "role": "Stavriote centre"}]}, {"id": "PT-005", "date": "1916-10", "date_end": "1917-09", "date_text": "October 1916 – September 1917", "precision": "month", "title": "The Patriarchate's record: the villages of Ordu and Fatsa deported, 1916–17", "summary": "The Patriarchate's compilation records that the Greek villages around Ordu were deported between October 1916 and September 1917, 'without any military reason' in its words, and names 24 of them; the town's Greeks had averted expulsion for a time by hauling 200 tons of supplies a month to Mesudiye for the vali Cemal Azmi, but were expelled from September 1917 to the Sivas and Kastamonu provinces. A letter of Metropolitan Polycarpos of 29 January 1917 says that the priests of Vona, Andouz and Elez-keuy died after beatings or prison. At Fatsa the Greeks were exiled from November 1916 to February 1917 to Samsun, Tokat and Zile, their houses demolished and seven villages emptied (Patriarchate 1919, pp. 125-127). Each village's fate is given in the Villages section.", "people": ["Polycarpos, Metropolitan of Neocaesarea"], "sources_primary": ["[Patriarchate compilation] Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (Constantinople 1919), pp. 125-127"], "sources_modern": ["Greek Patriarchate, Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (Constantinople, 1919), English edition, Part III (Pontus) [Patriarchate compilation, Greek, 1919, for the peace settlement]"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "This rests on one Greek compilation. Its Ordu dates differ from the missionary Crawford's letters (the men to be gone by 12 July 1917, the families a month later; H6-027) and from Chrysanthos' date for the shelling of Ordu (11 August 1917; C3-043).", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Patriarchate compilation (1919)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Ordu", "lat": 40.984, "lon": 37.879, "role": "villages deported Oct 1916 - Sep 1917; town from Sep 1917"}, {"name": "Fatsa", "lat": 41.03, "lon": 37.5, "role": "exiled Nov 1916 - Feb 1917"}, {"name": "Mesudiye", "lat": 40.457, "lon": 37.773, "role": "supplies hauled by the Ordu Greeks"}]}, {"id": "H6-026", "date": "1916-12", "date_text": "December 1916", "precision": "month", "title": "Enver orders Greeks removed from the coastal strip", "summary": "War Minister Enver orders the deportation of Greeks living within about 50 km of the Black Sea coast, 'for purely military reasons' in the order's words; German diplomats were told it would be carried out more mildly than the Armenians' deportation. Notables of Samsun, Bafra, Ordu, Tirebolu, Amasya and Çarşamba are deported, and about 200 Greeks are reported hanged for 'desertion'. Despite promises, the Greeks of Kerasous (Giresun) were not allowed to stay at Kulakkaya, 50 km inland, but were driven over the mountains towards Sivas in midwinter.", "people": ["Enver Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["[Ottoman document] Talat to Samsun, 11 January 1917, BOA DH.ŞFR 71/234: Enver says the Third Army has made the arrangements (Akçam 2012, p. 111); the December 1916 order itself is known from German reports (Hofmann 2011)", "British intelligence, 'Refet Pasha (Ali Refet Pasha)', undated, UKNA FO 371/6501, and Greek Patriarchate, Persecution of the Greeks, p. 120 (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 56)", "German diplomatic reports, Oct 1916 – Feb 1917 (Radowitz; Kühlmann), PA/AA, via Hofmann 2011, after Fotiadis, Der Genozid an den Pontosgriechen, pp. 131–132, 139"], "sources_modern": ["Akçam, The Young Turks' Crime against Humanity (Princeton UP, 2012), pp. 109-112 (the change of policy in autumn 1916; Talat's cables of September 1916; the Samsun cable of January 1917) [Turkish-born scholar abroad]", "Hofmann, Abriss (AGA)", "Topalidis, 'The relocation of Greeks from Pontos' (pontosworld)", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 390", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), pp. 59–60", "Morris & Ze'evi 2019, p. 385 ('the CUP leadership never adopted a policy of genocide or even of comprehensive ethnic cleansing vis-à-vis the Ottoman Greeks', but 'real persecution … orchestrated by Constantinople'); p. 490 ('centrally orchestrated efforts') [international scholars, Israel]", "Pallavicini, January 1918 (the deportations not dictated by military reasons), quoted in Agtzidis 2005, p. 155 [contemporary witness, Austro-Hungarian ambassador]", "Vehip Pasha's telegram of 20 November 1916 ('purely a military measure'), in Çilingir, p. 54 [Ottoman official, via a Turkish writer]"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The Ottoman security case is given under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H6-T03). Vehip Pasha's telegram of 20 November 1916 called the measure 'purely a military measure' (Çilingir, p. 54). Talat told the German ambassador Radowitz in October 1916 that a deportation of all the Greeks of the coast had been planned earlier that year and stopped by Enver so as not to provoke Greece (Hofmann 2011, p. 59). Akçam (2012, p. 109) dates the change of policy to the autumn of 1916 and gives three causes: the Entente's occupation of Lesbos, Chios and Samos, the Russian advance, and an expected Greek entry into the war; on 11 September 1916 Talat told provinces of the west to prepare, in secret, to move the Greeks of border regions inland at an order from Istanbul (pp. 109–110). Akçam quotes no December order of Enver; the Ottoman documents he gives for this phase are Talat's cables of January 1917 (H7-011). Most non-Turkish historians read the removals as demographic homogenisation with security as the pretext (Minasidis 2024), and Pallavicini wrote in January 1918 that the deportations were not dictated by military reasons (quoted by Agtzidis 2005, p. 155). Morris & Ze'evi (p. 385) write that the CUP leadership 'never adopted a policy of genocide or even of comprehensive ethnic cleansing' toward the Ottoman Greeks, but that there was 'real persecution … orchestrated by Constantinople'. Minasidis notes that Vehip Pasha protected some communities near Giresun; German reports say that the Giresun Greeks were driven inland despite a promise to let them stay at Kulakkaya. The 200 hanged rest on 'Greek witnesses' and a British intelligence profile of Refet (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 390: 'apparently hanged'); the Patriarchate's compilation, which Morris & Ze'evi also cite, has no Greeks hanged anywhere in its Pontus part. In that book's English edition 'Amassia' (pp. 120–122) is Samsun (Amisos), so 'Amasya' in the list of towns is probably Samsun counted twice.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Talat's cable of 11 January 1917 reporting Enver's word (BOA DH.ŞFR 71/234, via Akçam 2012, p. 111)", "Neutral sources: Akçam 2012; Hofmann 2011 (German diplomatic reports); Morris & Ze'evi 2019, p. 390 (British intelligence file)", "Greek sources: Greek Patriarchate 1919; Topalidis; Minasidis 2024"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "notables deported"}, {"name": "Bafra (Pafra)", "lat": 41.567, "lon": 35.906, "role": "notables deported"}, {"name": "Çarşamba", "lat": 41.199, "lon": 36.722, "role": "notables deported"}, {"name": "Ordu", "lat": 40.984, "lon": 37.879, "role": "notables deported"}, {"name": "Tirebolu", "lat": 41.006, "lon": 38.815, "role": "notables deported"}, {"name": "Amasya", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "notables deported"}, {"name": "Kulakkaya (yayla south of Giresun)", "lat": 40.73, "lon": 38.42, "role": "promised place of internment for the Giresun Greeks (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H6-027", "date": "1916-12", "date_end": "1917-03", "date_text": "Winter 1916–17 (Kerasous); July – August 1917 (Ordu)", "precision": "month", "title": "Deportations from the Kerasous and Ordu hinterland", "summary": "Villages of the Kerasous (Giresun) hinterland, such as Panagia at Kuşkaya, are marched south over the mountains in winter towards Şebinkarahisar, Zara and Tokat, escorted by paramilitaries who had taken part in the Armenian deportations. By 13 January 1917 the American relief treasurer in Constantinople reported that the Greeks had been expelled from Kerasous. In July 1917, an order of the army headquarters at Suşehri, reported by the American missionary Lyndon Crawford, read: 'By 12 July let no Greek man over 16 and under 50 be found in Ordou. Send all such on into the interior. As for the families, we will send further orders later'; a month later the women and children were being told to 'move on' to places without food. One Kerasous survivor's column of 660 is said to have ended with 10–15 survivors. Chrysanthos (p. 765) describes the winter deportation of the Greeks of Turkish-held Pontus over snow-covered mountains towards Sivas, 'so that they would die on the road of cold, hardship and hunger', without places or figures. Greek Legation reports give the scale. On 7 February 1917: 38 villages of the coast emptied within 24 hours, 23,000 people, driven off under escort without food, clothes or baggage, despite the assurances of the Third Army's commander (Brown & Ion 1918, p. 47). The Legation's table to January 1917 lists 22,356 deported from the Kerasous district, village by village (p. 67). A report of 21 April 1917 gives 88 villages evacuated and burned from December 1916 to February 1917, 'on account of three hundred deserters', and about 30,000 people taken to the Ankara district, a quarter of whom died on the road (p. 13).", "sources_primary": ["Memoir of Charalampos Eleftheriadis (Kuşkaya)", "W. W. Peet to J. L. Barton, 13 Jan 1917, ABCFM 16.9.3 vol. 48 (via Kyriakidis 2023, p. 47)", "Lyndon S. Crawford to J. L. Barton, 24 July 1917, and to Miss Lamson, 27 Aug 1917, ABCFM 16.9.3 vol. 41 (via Kyriakidis 2023, p. 48)", "British Acting High Commissioner to Curzon, 15 Aug 1919, FO 371/4158 (Ordu Greeks expelled, half the town destroyed; via Kyriakidis 2023, n. 54, after Fotiadis vol. 13, p. 116)", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.765 (account of a participant)", "[Greek government documents, translated] Brown & Ion 1918, p. 13 (report of 21 April 1917: 88 villages evacuated and burned, Dec 1916 – Feb 1917, about 30,000 people taken to the Ankara district, a quarter dead on the road); p. 47 (Greek Legation report no. 119, 7 Feb 1917: 38 coastal villages, 23,000 people); p. 53 (all the Greeks outside Kerasous sent towards Koyulhisar and Hamidiye); p. 67 (table: 22,356 deported to January 1917, village by village)", "[Patriarchate compilation] Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (1919), pp. 102-108, 125-126"], "sources_modern": ["Minasidis 2024", "Kyriakidis, 'Testimonies of American Charitable and Missionary Organizations on the Genocide of the Pontic Greeks', in Akçam, Kyriakidis et al. (eds), The Genocide of the Christian Populations in the Ottoman Empire and its Aftermath (Routledge 2023), pp. 47–48", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), p. 60 (Giresun Greeks driven towards Sivas in midwinter)", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (New York and Athens: Caratzas, 2011), p. 60 (after Fotiadis, Der Genozid an den Pontosgriechen, p. 139)", "Akçam, The Young Turks' Crime against Humanity (Princeton UP, 2012), pp. 112-113 (the evacuation of the Giresun and Amasya regions followed the Samsun deportations, and Istanbul knew of the looting; for Giresun he refers to the Patriarchate's Persecution of the Greeks, pp. 103-107; a cable to Trebizond of 30 July 1917, BOA DH.ŞFR 78/154, is cited for some populations largely left in place) [Turkish-born scholar abroad]"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The Kerasous deportation of the winter of 1916–17 and the Ordu men's deportation of July 1917 are attested by American missionary letters (Peet; Crawford) and by German reports (in Hofmann), which confirm the forced march towards Sivas but give no numbers. The three Greek figures for the Kerasous district, 23,000 (38 coastal villages, 7 February 1917), 22,356 (table to January 1917) and about 30,000 (88 villages, 21 April 1917), are counts at different dates from Greek consular reports, published in wartime to rouse opinion; they are the reporters' estimates. The survivor's figures (a column of 660 reduced to 10–15) come from one memoir. The Patriarchate's compilation says that the expulsion of the Kerasous town families (60, then 50 more, posted on 1 January 1917) was averted after the Metropolitan appealed to Suşehri, while Bulancak was emptied in two hours (p. 108); Peet has the Greeks 'expelled from Kerasous'. For Ordu it gives the villages deported between October 1916 and September 1917 (24 named) and the town's expulsion from September 1917, after a Russian shelling on '19th August' when about 2,500 left by sea for Trebizond (pp. 125–126); Chrysanthos has 11 August and about 3,000 (C3-043).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: memoir of Charalampos Eleftheriadis; Minasidis 2024", "Neutral sources: Peet and Crawford letters (ABCFM, via Kyriakidis 2023); German reports (via Hofmann 2011); British Acting High Commissioner, 15 Aug 1919", "Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933", "Greek sources: Greek government documents (Brown & Ion 1918, p. 13, report of 21 April 1917); Fotiadis (edition)", "Neutral sources: German diplomatic reports (via Hofmann)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.39, "role": "origin district"}, {"name": "Ordu (Kotyora)", "lat": 40.984, "lon": 37.879, "role": "origin district"}, {"name": "Şebinkarahisar", "lat": 40.288, "lon": 38.424, "role": "route"}, {"name": "Zara", "lat": 39.896, "lon": 37.752, "role": "destination"}, {"name": "Tokat", "lat": 40.314, "lon": 36.554, "role": "destination"}, {"name": "Suşehri (army headquarters, 1917)", "lat": 40.165, "lon": 38.087, "role": "headquarters that issued the Ordu order of July 1917"}, {"name": "Giresun (Kerasous)", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.39, "role": "district of origin; 88 villages burned"}, {"name": "Ankara (Angora)", "lat": 39.933, "lon": 32.86, "role": "destination district"}]}, {"id": "H6-028", "date": "1916-12-11", "date_end": "1917-01-18", "date_text": "11 Dec 1916 – 18 Jan 1917", "precision": "day", "title": "Burning and deportation in the Samsun and Bafra districts", "summary": "On 11 December five Greek villages of the Samsun district are looted, burned and their people deported. The Austro-Hungarian ambassador Pallavicini forwarded reports that 'entire villages including schools and the churches are set on fire' (entry for 14 December), that 'in the district of Samsun they burnt eleven villages' (17 December), and that by 31 December about 18 villages had been burnt completely and 15 partly, with 'around 60 women' raped; 28 villages had been burned by 15 January. The village of Kadıköy is marched south on 28 December. Mass expulsions from Samsun town follow on 10-13 January 1917 and from Bafra on 17-18 January. Deportees go by Kavak and Havza to the provinces of Ankara and Sivas: about 20,000 (Greek Legation) or 30,000 (Karavangelis). An Ottoman report gives 13,406 Greeks sent inland from Samsun (H7-053). The Greek Legation's reports from Samsun (Brown & Ion 1918, pp. 48-55) add: 28 more villages burnt in the week from 15 January; eight Bafra villages that grew the best tobacco burnt and twenty more later; Turkish refugees settled in the houses of eight Samsun villages and promised title deeds; the tobacco of villages emptied but not burnt (Aylaskoy, Andreanta, Tekkeköy, Kadıköy) auctioned by the government; all the men of Bafra sent to Boyabat; the richest men of Bafra to Kastamonu, and the merchants of Samsun and Çarşamba to villages around Çorum, Sungurlu, Mecitözü and İskilip. An Interior Ministry official called 'Vehaedin' drew up the lists of those to be deported, which Rafet carried out; he is most likely Bahaeddin Şakir, whom Akçam places in the region from December 1916 (H7-011). The Legation's list of the villages of the Samsun district emptied by February 1917, all those of the coast as far as Alaçam, (Brown & Ion, pp. 68–69), is given in the Villages section.", "people": ["Rafet Bey", "Johann von Pallavicini"], "sources_primary": ["Pallavicini to Vienna, HHStA PA XII (Türkei), Liasse 467 LIV, Zl. 97/pol., Constantinople, 2 Jan 1917 (entries of 14, 17 and 31 Dec 1916; a covering date printed '19.1.1916', probably 1917), and Nr. 6/P., 20 Jan 1917, as cited by greek-genocide.net, 'The Samsun Deportations', nn. 4-5", "Greek Legation, Constantinople, communication no. 548 (Ministerial Archives no. 5079), forwarding four reports from Samsun dated 14 and 29 January and 7 and 29 [sic] February 1917, in Brown & Ion 1918, pp. 48-55; the arrests began on 27 Dec 1916", "Germanos Karavangelis, letter of 29 Dec 1918", "Refet's report, BOA DH.ŞFR 548/1 (12 March 1333 = 1917), via Biber (TÜBA) ch. 13, p. 430 n. 24 (13,406)", "Austrian Foreign Office report on Aylaskoy and Kadıköy (3,000–4,000 driven inland at night), via Hofmann 2011, p. 60, after Fotiadis, p. 142", "Lt Earl Zimmer, USS Olympia, to CO, 8 Sept 1919 (Bafra hinterland deportees; Germanos' statement), via Morris & Ze'evi, nn. 57, 62", "Ralph Chesbrough, report on Samsun, 8 Aug 1919, USNA RG 59 (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 59)", "[Austrian diplomatic] Pallavicini, HHStA PA XII (Türkei), Liasse 467 LIV, No. 97/pol., Constantinople, 19.12.1916 and 2.1.1917 (Fotiadis vol. 13 (2004), p. 20, via Shirinian 2017, p. 72 n. 167); a copy is in PA-AA, Türkei No. 168, Bd. 15 f. Bd. 16, No. 61/pol., A. 4699, Berlin, 9.2.1917 (ibid.)", "[British intelligence] 'Refet Pasha (Ali Refet Pasha)', undated, FO 371/6501, and Patriarchate, 'Persecution of the Greeks', p. 120 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 56)", "[US Navy] Lt Earl Zimmer, USS Olympia, to CO, 8 Sept 1919, USNA RG 59, 867.00, Roll 7 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 57)", "[Ottoman document] Talat to Canik, 26 February 1917, BOA DH.ŞFR 73/79: an inquiry into which villages had been burnt and destroyed 'in the course of bandit raids' (Akçam 2012, p. 112, n. 55)", "[Patriarchate compilation] Persecution of the Greeks in Turkey 1914-1918 (1919), pp. 120-122 (the Metropolitan's Samsun reports, printed 'Amassia'; letters of 27 Feb and 15 March 1917)"], "sources_modern": ["greek-genocide.net, 'The Samsun Deportations'", "Minasidis 2024 (Kadıköy)", "Akçam, The Young Turks' Crime against Humanity (Princeton UP, 2012), p. 112 (Bahaeddin Şakir in the Samsun region from December 1916; by the end of the month at least 18 villages wholly and 15 partly emptied; in January 1917 some 80 notables of Samsun arrested and 4,000 people deported to Havza, then Çorum, and settled in the houses of deported Armenians; Karavangelis, in letters to the Patriarchate, about 30,000 deported to the province of Ankara; for the period he refers to Yerasimos, Milliyetler ve Sınırlar, 1994, pp. 351-427) and p. 113 (the bishop of Amasya, March 1917: two-thirds of those deported from Amasya to Ankara massacred, after the Patriarchate's Persecution of the Greeks, pp. 120-122) [Turkish-born scholar abroad]", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 390–391", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), p. 60", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 390", "Shirinian, 'The Background to the Late Ottoman Genocides', in Shirinian (ed.), Genocide in the Ottoman Empire: Armenians, Assyrians, and Greeks, 1913-1923 (Berghahn, 2017), p. 52", "Çetinoğlu, 'T.C.'nin soykırımlar geleneği ve Pontos soykırımı' [Turkish writer], p. 15 (Turkish translation of the same entries)", "Brown & Ion (trans.), Persecutions of the Greeks in Turkey since the Beginning of the European War (New York: Oxford UP for the American-Hellenic Society, 1918), pp. 48-55, 68-69 [Greek government documents in translation]"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The numbers: about 20,000 deported by the Greek Legation's rough estimate (Brown & Ion 1918, p. 52), and 30,000 from the Samsun district by February 1917, a quarter of whom were said to have died already (pp. 55, 68); 30,000 by Karavangelis (cited by Akçam); and 13,406 sent inland from Samsun by the Ottoman report of Refet, which is a floor. The Austrian report puts the people of Aylaskoy and Kadıköy alone at 3,000–4,000 (Hofmann 2011, p. 60). Akçam (2012, p. 112) gives the same counts for the end of December 1916 as Pallavicini (18 villages wholly and 15 partly), but as evacuated where Pallavicini has burnt; Talat's own cable of 26 February 1917 asked Canik which villages had so far been burnt and destroyed 'in bandit raids', so the burning was known in Istanbul. The '28 villages burnt by 15 January' may come from the Legation report's '28 other villages burnt within a week from January 15th' (p. 51). The Patriarchate's letters from Samsun are the same reports as the Greek Legation's, so the two are one witness; its letter of 15 March 1917 says that two-thirds of those expelled had died in the Turkish villages of the Ankara province from want and neglect (p. 122), which Akçam renders as 'massacred'. Its 'February 1916' and 'February 1915' for the Bafra and Samsun burnings (p. 122) are the events of early 1917. The book names Refet's partner as 'Behaeddine Effendi', a special delegate of the Interior Minister from Constantinople (p. 121), which supports reading the Legation's 'Vehaedin' as Bahaeddin Şakir; neither gives his full name. A US consul's post-war inquiry suggested that about 5,000 Greeks and as many Armenians had been 'eliminated' from Samsun by massacre, expulsion and flight, and noted that Samsun's Turks had also suffered heavy losses in the war, in combat and from disease (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 391). Metropolitan Germanos told US officers that 'the greatest cause' of the Bafra deportations was 'religion' (p. 391). Karavangelis' claim that only a tenth to a twentieth of 160,000 Pontic deportees survived is an advocacy figure.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Pallavicini (Austro-Hungarian embassy reports); Akçam 2012", "Greek sources: Greek Legation report 1916; Karavangelis 1918; Minasidis 2024; greek-genocide.net", "Ottoman state records: Refet's report, BOA DH.ŞFR 548/1 (13,406)", "Neutral sources: Pallavicini (Austro-Hungarian embassy, HHStA; copy in PA-AA); British intelligence; US Navy (Zimmer)", "Greek sources: Fotiadis (edition); Patriarchate; Greek witnesses (via Morris & Ze'evi)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "waypoint 1: origin (town and villages)"}, {"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.567, "lon": 35.906, "role": "origin, 17–18 Jan 1917"}, {"name": "Kavak", "lat": 41.079, "lon": 36.044, "role": "waypoint 2: first night stop"}, {"name": "Havza", "lat": 40.97, "lon": 35.662, "role": "waypoint 3"}, {"name": "Amasya", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "waypoint 4"}, {"name": "Çorum", "lat": 40.549, "lon": 34.954, "role": "destination area (Ankara province)"}, {"name": "Sivas", "lat": 39.748, "lon": 37.017, "role": "destination area"}, {"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "notables deported"}, {"name": "Çarşamba", "lat": 41.199, "lon": 36.723, "role": "notables deported; villages burned"}, {"name": "Ordu (Kotyora)", "lat": 40.984, "lon": 37.879, "role": "notables deported"}, {"name": "Amasya (Amaseia)", "lat": 40.654, "lon": 35.833, "role": "notables deported"}]}, {"id": "H6-029", "date": "1917-01-31", "date_text": "January – February 1917", "precision": "month", "title": "German and Austrian protests", "summary": "The Ottoman Empire's allies, Germany and Austria-Hungary, protest the Pontic deportations. In early February 1917 German diplomats, informed by Austrian reports, concluded that the 'ban' on the Greeks of Samsun, carried out on the pretext of pursuing Greek bands, was a 'large scale' persecution; ambassador Kühlmann (16 February 1917) expected the new grand vizier Talat to end 'the locally committed persecution of Greeks'. Pallavicini raised the matter with the Porte. A text dated 31 January 1917, quoted by Midlarsky as Chancellor Bethmann Hollweg's and by Greek and Turkish writers as an Austrian Foreign Ministry document sent to Berlin, says the Turks 'plan to eliminate the Greek element as enemies of the state, as they did earlier with the Armenians'. The deportations slow in 1917 but those deported are not returned until after the armistice.", "people": ["Theobald von Bethmann Hollweg", "Richard von Kühlmann", "Johann von Pallavicini"], "sources_primary": ["Bethmann Hollweg, statement of 31 Jan 1917 (as quoted by Midlarsky 2005)", "Kühlmann to Berlin, 16 Feb 1917, PA/AA (via Hofmann 2011, p. 61)", "German diplomatic reports of early Feb 1917, after Austrian sources (via Hofmann 2011, p. 60, after Fotiadis, p. 142)"], "sources_modern": ["Midlarsky, The Killing Trap (2005), pp. 342f.", "Hofmann, Abriss (AGA)", "Bjørnlund 2011", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), pp. 59–61", "Kelly, in Akçam, Kyriakidis et al. (eds) (Routledge 2023), p. 239 (the same text as the German chancellor's, 1917, after Midlarsky 2005, pp. 342-343)", "Hofmann, Kurzer Abriss (aga-online.org), n. 2 (the German wording 'Es deutet alles darauf hin, dass die Türken das griechische Element als Staatsfeind beseitigen wollen', quoted from Midlarsky, p. 342f.)", "Çetinoğlu, 'Germanos'tan Konstantinides'e Tarihi Mektup...' [Turkish writer], p. 9 and n. 31 (the same text as a document the Austrian Foreign Ministry sent to Berlin, after Frangoulis Frangos, Hangi Türkiye, Hangi Türkler (Athens 2012), p. 118); Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği (2016) [Turkish writer], pp. 56-57, after Çetinoğlu"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The German protests and assessments are documented by Hofmann (2011, pp. 59–61) from the German Foreign Office archive, as published by Fotiadis. The sentence dated 31 January 1917 has been given to different authors: Midlarsky quotes it as Chancellor Bethmann Hollweg's, and Hofmann's web essay and Kelly (2023, p. 239) cite only Midlarsky; Çetinoğlu (after Frangos 2012, p. 118) and Çilingir (pp. 56–57) quote it as a document of the Austrian Foreign Ministry sent to Berlin. Since German diplomats were using Austrian information in early February 1917 (Hofmann 2011, p. 60), it is probably an Austrian text forwarded to Berlin and later attributed to the chancellor; we have not found the original. It should not be confused with Talat's remark to an Austrian agent in January 1916 (H7-054).", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: German diplomatic reports (via Hofmann 2011); Bethmann Hollweg (as quoted by Midlarsky 2005); Bjørnlund 2011"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "embassies"}]}, {"id": "C3-043", "date": "1917-03", "date_end": "1917-08-11", "date_text": "1917; Ordu evacuated 11 August 1917", "precision": "month", "title": "1917: the soldiers' soviet, the Youth Union and the evacuation of the Ordu Greeks", "summary": "After the February revolution, Chrysanthos writes, the Russian army deposed its officers, and he accepted a seat on the Trebizond soldiers' soviet 'ἵνα διαφωτίζῃ' (to enlighten it). A 'Ἐθνικὴ Ἕνωσις τῶν Νέων' (National Union of Youth) was founded in Trebizond and Rize. On 11 August 1917 the Bolshevik fleet bombarded Turkish-held Ordu; the Greek population rushed into the sea towards the ships, and the Russians took '3.000 περίπου ψυχὰς' (about 3,000 souls) to Trebizond, where they were housed within three hours.", "people": ["Chrysanthos"], "sources_primary": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.749–750; p.766 (account of a participant)", "Λογοδοσία of the refugee committee, 23 January 1919, pp.15–16, as cited by Chrysanthos"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The Patriarchate's compilation dates the shelling of Ordu to '19th August' and gives about 2,500 taken to Trebizond (p. 125).", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, pp.749–750; p.766"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "soviet; refugees housed"}, {"name": "Rizaion (Rize)", "lat": 41.0208, "lon": 40.5219, "role": "Youth Union"}, {"name": "Kotyora (Ordu)", "lat": 40.97782, "lon": 37.89047, "role": "bombarded; c. 3,000 Greeks evacuated"}]}, {"id": "C3-044", "date": "1917-11", "date_text": "November 1917", "precision": "month", "title": "Chrysanthos' account: about 300 notables of Of thank him at Yiga", "summary": "Chrysanthos writes that in November 1917, at the village of Yiga in Of, 'περὶ τοὺς 300 μπέϊδες, ἰμάμιδες καὶ χοτζάδες' (about 300 beys, imams and hocas), led by the Muslim mayor of Of, came to thank him 'διὰ τὴν παρασχεθεῖσαν αὐτοῖς … προστασίαν' (for the protection given to them) during the occupation. They were descendants of the first converts and greeted him 'ὡς ἴδιον αὐτῶν ποιμενάρχην' (as their own chief pastor).", "people": ["Chrysanthos"], "sources_primary": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.709 (account of a participant)"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, p.709"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Yiga (Yarlı), Of", "lat": 40.9026, "lon": 40.3439, "role": "meeting with c. 300 Muslim notables"}, {"name": "Of", "lat": 40.946, "lon": 40.27, "role": "Muslim mayor of Of"}]}, {"id": "C3-045", "date": "1917-12", "date_end": "1918-02-23", "date_text": "December 1917 – February 1918", "precision": "month", "title": "Chrysanthos' account: the çete campaign against the Greek villages, self-defence, and the flight of half the Greeks to Russia", "summary": "Chrysanthos writes that from December 1917 Turkish leaders 'ἐξαπέλυσαν στίφη «τσετέδων»' (let loose bands of çetes) on the unguarded countryside 'ἵνα τρομοκρατηθῇ ὁ χριστιανικὸς κόσμος καὶ μεταναστεύσῃ εἰς Ῥωσίαν' (to terrorise the Christians into emigrating to Russia). The Youth Union armed villagers with weapons left by the Bolshevik authorities, about 80 young men held Boz Tepe, and a district defence (Trebizond, Sourmena, Matsouka, Santa, Mesochaldion) repelled 'thousands' of çetes led by Turkish officers 'for whole months'. The village of Uz was destroyed and its people came to Trebizond naked. In the end 'κατωρθώθη νὰ περισωθῇ τὸ ἥμισυ τοῦ Ἑλληνικοῦ πληθυσμοῦ τῆς Τραπεζουσίας χώρας, τοῦ ἑτέρου ἡμίσεος μεταναστεύσαντος εἰς Ῥωσίαν' (half the Greek population of the Trebizond region was saved, the other half having emigrated to Russia).", "people": ["Chrysanthos"], "sources_primary": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.745–751; p.767 (account of a participant)", "Λογοδοσία of the refugee committee, 23 January 1919, pp.10–13, as cited by Chrysanthos"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Chrysanthos gives no number; half of his own count of 1913–14 would be about 23,000–25,000 people, a reckoning of ours, not his.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, pp.745–751; p.767"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Mithrios hill (Boz Tepe), Trebizond (approx.)", "lat": 41.0, "lon": 39.735, "role": "c. 80 young men held the hill"}, {"name": "Sourmena (Sürmene)", "lat": 40.91046, "lon": 40.11405, "role": "district defence"}, {"name": "Matsouka (Maçka)", "lat": 40.8111, "lon": 39.6132, "role": "district defence"}, {"name": "Santa (Dumanlı)", "lat": 40.66888, "lon": 39.80164, "role": "defended for months"}, {"name": "Uz (Özdil), Gemura", "lat": 40.83336, "lon": 39.82453, "role": "village destroyed by çetes"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "refugees; embarkation for Russia"}]}, {"id": "H6-030", "date": "1917-12-05", "date_end": "1918-01", "date_text": "5 Dec 1917 – Jan 1918", "precision": "day", "title": "Erzincan armistice; the Russian army dissolves", "summary": "After the revolutions of 1917 the Russian Caucasus Army falls apart. The armistice of Erzincan ends fighting on 18 December 1917 (5 December Old Style), and Russian soldiers begin leaving Trebizond in late December. On 11/24 December the French military representative in Tiflis, Colonel Chardigny, with the approval of his British and American colleagues, asked Chrysanthos to help the Russian General Kolosowski raise a Greek volunteer regiment at Trebizond, part of a Greek division under General Ananias, for order in the city and defence against the Turks; Chrysanthos was cautious, fearing to inflame the Muslims. Turkish armed bands grow bolder (US consul, late January 1918). Chrysanthos appeals to Vehip Pasha about attacks on Christians and distributes arms to Greek villages for self-defence; Santa is among the districts saved.", "people": ["Chrysanthos", "Vehip Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["Greek Patriarchate 1919", "US consular reports from Trebizond, Jan 1918", "Colonel Chardigny to Chrysanthos, Tiflis, 11/24 Dec 1917, printed in Le Martyre du Pont-Euxin et l'opinion publique internationale (Geneva 1922; Greek advocacy compilation, preface by A. Wuarin), pp. 12–13", "Chrysanthos to Chardigny, Trebizond, 24 Dec 1917, Greek Foreign Ministry archive (via Georganopoulos 2023, p. 74 n. 11)"], "sources_modern": ["Topalidis, 'Santa, Pontos after 1914'", "Georganopoulos, 'The Evidence of the French Commission in Pontus on the Anti-Hellenic Persecutions … (1919–1920)', in Akçam, Kyriakidis et al. (eds) (Routledge 2023), p. 74"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The armistice is dated 5 December 1917 in the Russian (Old Style) calendar, the date some English works give, and 18 December in the New Style used in this chapter. The Allied plan for a Greek regiment is documented by a French officer's letter printed in a Greek advocacy pamphlet; that the regiment was not formed rests on Georganopoulos, after Chrysanthos' reply. The arming of the villages for self-defence rests on Greek sources.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Greek Patriarchate 1919; Topalidis", "Neutral sources: US consular reports from Trebizond, Jan 1918", "Neutral sources: Chardigny's letter, 11/24 Dec 1917 (printed in the Martyre, 1922)", "Greek sources: Chrysanthos' reply of 24 Dec 1917 (via Georganopoulos 2023)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Erzincan", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 39.5, "role": "armistice signed"}, {"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "Russian departure begins, late Dec 1917"}, {"name": "Santa (Dumanlı)", "lat": 40.6, "lon": 39.7, "role": "armed for self-defence"}, {"name": "Tiflis", "lat": 41.716, "lon": 44.783, "role": "Chardigny's letter of 11/24 Dec 1917"}]}, {"id": "H6-031", "date": "1918-02", "date_end": "1918-07", "date_text": "Feb – July 1918", "precision": "month", "title": "Armenian irregulars' killings of Muslims during the Russian withdrawal (context)", "summary": "As the Russian army melts away, Armenian irregular units left holding the front kill Muslim villagers around Erzincan and Erzurum. Ottoman commanders publicised these killings and they were used to justify the Ottoman advance. This is outside the core region but shaped Muslim fears in Pontus.", "people": ["Kâzım Karabekir"], "sources_primary": ["Ottoman 3rd Army reports"], "sources_modern": ["McMeekin 2015", "Hovannisian, Armenia on the Road to Independence (1967)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The numbers given in Turkish publications are high, and we found no independent count. Armenian historians (Hovannisian 1967) set the killings in the context of 1915.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Ottoman 3rd Army reports", "Neutral sources: McMeekin 2015", "Armenian sources: Hovannisian 1967"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Erzincan", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 39.5, "role": "killings of Muslims, early 1918"}, {"name": "Erzurum", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.268, "role": "killings of Muslims, Feb–Mar 1918"}]}, {"id": "H6-032", "date": "1918-02", "date_end": "1918-12", "date_text": "Early 1918 onward", "precision": "month", "title": "Pontic Greeks leave with the Russian army for the Caucasus and Russia", "summary": "Fearing reprisals, Greeks of eastern Pontus follow the retreating Russians to Batum, Sukhumi, Novorossiysk, the Kuban, Rostov and the Azov towns, adding to the older Pontic communities there. The Greeks of Kars and Ardahan follow in 1918–20.", "sources_primary": ["Kwiatkowski reports, 1918 (via Photiadis 1987)", "F. Lorenzo da Montemarciano, Memorie della Missione di Trebisonda, p. 194r (via Kyriakides & Nikoloudis 2026, p. 63)"], "sources_modern": ["Xanthopoulou-Kyriakou 1991", "Karpozilos 1999", "Topalidis, 'The relocation of Greeks from Pontos'"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Estimates range from about 30,000 (Kwiatkowski, as cited by Fotiadis) to about 80,000 (Xanthopoulou-Kyriakou 1991). Chrysanthos says that half the Greeks of the Trebizond region left for Russia in 1918 (C3-045); against his count of 1913–14 that would be about 23,000–25,000, a reckoning of ours, not his figure.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Kwiatkowski reports, 1918 (via Photiadis 1987)", "Greek sources: Xanthopoulou-Kyriakou 1991; Karpozilos 1999; Topalidis", "Neutral sources: Capuchin diary of F. Lorenzo (many Greeks left Trebizond for Batum after the Russian withdrawal)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "origin (about 8,000 from the town, per Kwiatkowski)"}, {"name": "Batumi", "lat": 41.642, "lon": 41.634, "role": "destination"}, {"name": "Sukhumi", "lat": 43.003, "lon": 41.016, "role": "destination"}, {"name": "Novorossiysk", "lat": 44.723, "lon": 37.769, "role": "destination"}, {"name": "Rostov-on-Don", "lat": 47.236, "lon": 39.714, "role": "destination"}, {"name": "Kars", "lat": 40.602, "lon": 43.097, "role": "origin of Kars Greeks, 1918–20"}]}, {"id": "H6-033", "date": "1918-02-07", "date_end": "1918-03-12", "date_text": "7 Feb – 12 Mar 1918", "precision": "day", "title": "The Ottoman return to Pontus", "summary": "Vehip Pasha's Third Army advances into the vacuum: Kelkit (7 Feb), Erzincan (13 Feb), Bayburt (19 Feb), Tercan (22 Feb) and Trebizond (23 Feb 1918, by Kâzım Bey's letter and Chrysanthos; 24 Feb in Erickson), with troops also landed by sea; Erzurum follows on 12 March. An ammunition depot explodes at Trebizond with about 600 killed. The head of the Capuchin mission recorded that the city's commander announced orders from Constantinople to deport all the Greeks, men, women, old people and children, inland, and that Chrysanthos protested; Turkish irregular bands burned and killed in the villages around the city, and many Greeks left for Batum. Greek sources say Chrysanthos' negotiations and his record towards Muslims spared the Greeks of the city from reprisals. The divisional commander Kâzım Bey's letter of 22 February (Rumi 22/2/1334), printed in facsimile by Chrysanthos, announced that the troops would occupy the city by daylight on 23/2/1334 ('10 Şubat 1918' in the Julian style); Chrysanthos drove to Kâzım's headquarters at Kryoneri and brought him into the city, where Russian troops rendered honours (Chrysanthos, a participant).", "people": ["Vehip Pasha", "Chrysanthos"], "sources_primary": ["F. Lorenzo da Montemarciano, Memorie della Missione di Trebisonda, pp. 190v, 194r, 223v–224r, 225v–226v (via Kyriakides & Nikoloudis 2026, pp. 62–63)", "Greek Patriarchate, Persecution of the Greeks, pp. 112–113, letter of Chrysostomos, 12 Oct 1918 (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 55)", "Kâzım Bey to Chrysanthos, 22/2/1334 (22 February 1918), facsimile in Chrysanthos 1933, fig. 118, translated pp. 769-770", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.767–771 (account of a participant)"], "sources_modern": ["Erickson 2001, p. 183 (Trebizond 24 Feb, Erzincan 13 Feb 1918) (as cited on Wikipedia)", "Kyriakides (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias 2026; Greek ed. 2015), pp. 61–63 (Chrysanthos' policy acknowledged by the Turkish authorities, after Chrysanthos 1933, pp. 767–772)", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 390 ('when … they reconquered Trabzon vilayet, they continued expelling Greeks systematically')"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "That the Greeks of Trebizond were spared because of Chrysanthos rests on Greek accounts (Chrysanthos 1933, pp. 767–772; Vehip's letter, as printed in 1919–22). The Capuchin diary shows that a deportation of all the Greeks was ordered after the Ottoman return, but not how far it was carried out. Morris & Ze'evi (p. 390), after a Greek metropolitan's letter of October 1918, say that the Ottomans 'continued expelling Greeks systematically' in the reconquered vilayet; the letter is printed in the Patriarchate's compilation under 'Chryssostomos, the Metropolitan of Trebizonde' (p. 112), an error for Chrysanthos. The entry into Trebizond is dated 23 February 1918 by Kâzım's letter and Chrysanthos' narrative, 24 February by Erickson (2001, p. 183).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: McMeekin 2015; Erickson 2001", "Greek sources: Greek accounts of Chrysanthos' role", "Neutral sources: Capuchin diary of F. Lorenzo (deportation order; irregular bands; departures for Batum)", "Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933 (via Kyriakides & Nikoloudis 2026, p. 61); metropolitan's letter of 12 Oct 1918 (via Morris & Ze'evi 2019, p. 390)", "Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kelkit", "lat": 40.128, "lon": 39.437, "role": "waypoint 1: retaken 7 Feb 1918"}, {"name": "Erzincan", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 39.5, "role": "waypoint 2: retaken 13 Feb 1918"}, {"name": "Bayburt", "lat": 40.255, "lon": 40.225, "role": "waypoint 3: retaken 19 Feb 1918"}, {"name": "Tercan (Mamahatun)", "lat": 39.778, "lon": 40.383, "role": "retaken 22 Feb 1918"}, {"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "waypoint 4: retaken 23 Feb 1918 (24 Feb in Erickson)"}, {"name": "Erzurum", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.268, "role": "retaken 12 Mar 1918"}]}, {"id": "H6-034", "date": "1918-03-03", "date_end": "1918-06-04", "date_text": "3 Mar – 4 June 1918", "precision": "day", "title": "Brest-Litovsk, the Trebizond conference, Batum and Kars", "summary": "By the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk (3 March) Russia cedes Kars, Ardahan and Batum. Ottoman and Transcaucasian delegates meet at Trebizond from 14 March; talks fail and Ottoman troops take Batum (14 April) and Kars (25 April). The Treaty of Batum (4 June) closes the Ottoman–Transcaucasian war. In March 1918 Hüseyin Rauf Bey, Ali Rıza Bey, the vali and staff officers, in Trebizond for the talks with the Georgian prime minister, met at Chrysanthos's villa to discuss post-war cooperation of the peoples of the East; the metropolis's committee meanwhile fed and clothed returning Muslim refugees (Chrysanthos, pp. 771-772).", "sources_primary": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), p.748; pp.771–772 (account of a participant)"], "sources_modern": ["Hovannisian 1967"], "confidence": "high", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources", "Armenian sources: Hovannisian 1967", "Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Brest-Litovsk", "lat": 52.097, "lon": 23.734, "role": "treaty, 3 Mar 1918"}, {"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "peace conference from 14 Mar 1918"}, {"name": "Batumi", "lat": 41.642, "lon": 41.634, "role": "taken 14 Apr 1918; treaty 4 June 1918"}, {"name": "Kars", "lat": 40.602, "lon": 43.097, "role": "taken 25 Apr 1918"}]}, {"id": "H6-035", "date": "1918-07", "date_end": "1918-11", "date_text": "July – November 1918", "precision": "month", "title": "Pontic congresses in the diaspora", "summary": "A Pontic congress at Baku is said to have proclaimed Pontic independence and elected a seven-member council (July 1918); a larger Pontic conference meets in Paris in November. Emigré committees in Marseille, Batum and Constantinople begin the campaign that leads to the Paris memoranda.", "people": ["Konstantinos Konstantinidis"], "sources_modern": ["Agtzidis 2024 (as cited on Wikipedia)", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 383 (December 1918: Greeks in Batum reported to have announced a Pontic government in exile; 'If true, nothing came of it')", "Agtzidis, Έλληνες του Πόντου: Η γενοκτονία από τον τουρκικό εθνικισμό (Athens 2005), p. 185 of the electronic edition [Greek] (July 1918: a congress at Baku of representatives of the Greeks of south Russia, Transcaucasia and Pontus proclaimed the independence of Pontus and elected a seven-member council over all Pontic associations; November 1918: the Pontic conference in Paris)"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "The Baku 'proclamation' of July 1918 is reported by the Greek historian Agtzidis (2005); we have seen no contemporary document. Morris & Ze'evi (p. 383) report a similar announcement by Greeks in Batum in December 1918 and add that, if it happened, nothing came of it; the two may be versions of the same claim.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Agtzidis 2024 (as cited on Wikipedia)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Baku", "lat": 40.409, "lon": 49.867, "role": "congress, July 1918"}, {"name": "Paris", "lat": 48.857, "lon": 2.352, "role": "conference, Nov 1918"}, {"name": "Marseille", "lat": 43.296, "lon": 5.37, "role": "Pontic committee"}]}, {"id": "H6-036", "date": "1918-10-30", "date_text": "30 October 1918", "precision": "day", "title": "The armistice of Mudros", "summary": "Signed aboard HMS Agamemnon at Mudros, the armistice ends the Ottoman war. Surviving deportees begin to return to Pontus in 1918–19, mostly to burned or occupied homes. A British detachment is later posted at Samsun and British control officers at Trebizond.", "people": ["Rauf Bey (Orbay)", "Admiral Somerset Calthorpe"], "sources_primary": ["Armistice of Mudros, text"], "sources_modern": ["McMeekin 2015", "Kévorkian, Parachever un génocide: Mustafa Kemal et l'élimination des rescapés arméniens et grecs (Odile Jacob 2023), part III ch. 3, section 'Vilayet de Trébizonde', n. 43 (e-book, without print pages)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "A report of the Armenian Patriarchate's Information Bureau put the Greek population of the Trebizond vilayet at 353,533, of whom 231,579 had been deported during the war, with 80,000 deaths (cited by Kévorkian 2023, part III, ch. 3, n. 43). The figures are not confirmed elsewhere and probably came from Greek sources.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: armistice of Mudros; McMeekin 2015", "Armenian sources: Armenian Patriarchate report (via Kévorkian 2023, n. 43)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Mudros, Lemnos", "lat": 39.876, "lon": 25.272, "role": "armistice signed"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "returning deportees; British detachment 1919"}]}, {"id": "C3-048", "date": "1918-11", "date_end": "1919-07-19", "date_text": "Armistice period; Patriarch Zaven's letter of 19 July 1919", "precision": "month", "title": "Armenian children and property recovered; Zaven's thanks", "summary": "Chrysanthos writes that during the armistice the metropolis recovered Armenian children from Turkish homes and Armenian community property, and ran an Armenian orphanage for about a year. The Armenian Patriarch Zaven thanked it in a letter of 19 July 1919, which he prints.", "people": ["Chrysanthos", "Zaven Der Yeghiayan"], "sources_primary": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.760–761 (account of a participant)", "Letter of Patriarch Zaven, 19 July 1919, as printed by Chrysanthos"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, pp.760–761"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "Armenian orphanage run by the metropolis"}, {"name": "Constantinople (Istanbul)", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "Armenian Patriarchate"}]}, {"id": "H6-037", "date": "1919", "date_end": "1920-01", "date_text": "1919 (Topal Osman mayor of Giresun by June 1919)", "precision": "year", "title": "Topal Osman returns to power in Giresun; first nationalist killings", "summary": "In the first months after the armistice, Unionist networks arm Muslim villagers and bands. A US officer at Samsun reported in January 1919 that the authorities had armed the Turkish villagers of the Tsinik district in 'a carefully laid down plan … to eliminate the Greek and Armenian elements'. The French commissioner at Trebizond reported returning Greek refugees murdered as alleged revolutionaries (January), 600 returnees prevented from landing (late May), and organised persecution of Greeks at Kerasous, Merzifon and Bulancak (4 June); in September British officers reported that 'public security all along the Black Sea Coast was very bad'. Giresun's Defence of Rights committee mandates Topal Osman to act against what it called the Greek guerrilla groups; by mid-1919 he is mayor of Giresun, which the Ottoman investigating judge at Trebizond cited as proof that the government would not punish the massacrers of 1915. A Catholic missionary reported that his roughly 600 Laz were responsible for many murders in Giresun, and on 19 December 1919 the head of the Capuchin mission wrote that the Giresun nationalists had decided in council to 'get rid of the Christians' but had postponed it, keeping them as hostages until the Paris decisions were known. Returning Greek refugees near Merzifon formed bands to defend themselves and recover their property. In September 1919 an armed band attacks Santa women returning from Trebizond, and the guerrillas of Santa dig in for the winter.", "people": ["Topal Osman", "Efkleidis Kourtidis"], "sources_primary": ["Efraimidis 1972–74 (Santa)", "Lt N. W. Harty, Samsun, 25 Jan 1919, USNA RG 84 (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 99)", "French commissioner at Trebizond (Lépissier) to the High Commissioner, Jan, 2 June, 4 June and 5 June 1919, CADN Nantes, CPC/CFT/77 (via Georganopoulos 2023, nn. 25, 48, 50, 51)", "Armenian Patriarchate, Information Bureau (APC/PAJ, BIP), reports from Trebizond, Ordu and Giresun, Feb – Aug 1919 (via Kévorkian 2023, part III ch. 3, nn. 45–52, 60)", "Sétrag Karagueuzian (Ottoman investigating judge at Trebizond), letter of June 1919, APC/PAJ BIP (via Kévorkian 2023, part V ch. 3, nn. 42–43)", "Fra Cirillo Zohrabian, A servizio dei fratelli, pp. 344–346 (via Kyriakides & Nikoloudis 2026, pp. 66–67)", "F. Lorenzo to Propaganda Fide, Trebizond, 19 Dec 1919, Propaganda Fide, Nuova Serie vol. 753, p. 12r (via Kyriakides & Nikoloudis 2026, pp. 67–68)", "White (NER) to Ravndal, 20 June 1919, USNA RG 59 (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 86)"], "sources_modern": ["Sigalas 2026 ( (as cited on Wikipedia), 'Topal Osman')", "Topalidis, citing Fotiadis 2019", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 395, 398–399", "Georganopoulos, 'The Evidence of the French Commission in Pontus on the Anti-Hellenic Persecutions … (1919–1920)', in Akçam, Kyriakidis et al. (eds) (Routledge 2023), pp. 76–82", "Kévorkian, Parachever un génocide: Mustafa Kemal et l'élimination des rescapés arméniens et grecs (Odile Jacob 2023), part III ch. 3, section 'Vilayet de Trébizonde', nn. 45–60 (e-book, without print pages)", "Kyriakides (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias 2026; Greek ed. 2015), pp. 63–68", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), p. 71 n. 103 (Osman first met Kemal at Havza, 29 May 1919)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The Armenian Patriarchate's report dates Topal Osman's appointment as mayor to August 1919 (Kévorkian), but the Ottoman investigating judge at Trebizond, Karagueuzian, already wrote in June 1919 that he had been 'officially named mayor'. The reports of 1919 come from French, American and Catholic observers, from the Armenian Patriarchate and from Greek sources. The figure of 12,000 Pontic Greeks killed in 1919 is often quoted; we found no source for it.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Harty (US, Jan 1919); Lépissier and the French commission (1919); White (NER, June 1919); Catholic reports (Fr Cirillo; F. Lorenzo, 19 Dec 1919); Morris & Ze'evi 2019; Georganopoulos 2023 (transmitter)", "Armenian sources: Armenian Patriarchate reports (via Kévorkian 2023)", "Ottoman state records: investigating judge Karagueuzian's letter, June 1919", "Greek sources: Efraimidis 1972-74; Sigalas 2026; Topalidis citing Fotiadis 2019"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.39, "role": "Topal Osman mayor, 1919"}, {"name": "Santa (Dumanlı)", "lat": 40.6, "lon": 39.7, "role": "attack on returnees, Sept 1919"}, {"name": "Tsinik (Çarşamba–Terme hinterland of Samsun; approximate)", "lat": 41.15, "lon": 36.6, "role": "Turkish villagers armed, January 1919 (approximate)"}, {"name": "Bulancak", "lat": 40.938, "lon": 38.231, "role": "persecution reported by the French commissioner, 4 June 1919"}, {"name": "Merzifon", "lat": 40.873, "lon": 35.463, "role": "returning refugees form self-defence bands, 1919"}]}, {"id": "H6-038", "date": "1919-02", "date_text": "February 1919", "precision": "month", "title": "The Greek memorandum on Pontus, and the Turkish response in Trebizond", "summary": "A Greek memorandum to the Peace Conference (Oeconomos) asks for self-determination for Pontus and puts its pre-war Greek population at 700,000. In Trebizond, Muslims found the Trabzon Society for the Defence of National Rights in February 1919 to oppose any Pontic or Armenian claim; an Armenian Patriarchate report of 17 February 1919 describes Turkish notables implicated in the wartime massacres forming a 'pan-Turk committee' with branches in the other towns of the vilayet.", "people": ["Socrates Oeconomos"], "sources_primary": ["Oeconomos, memorandum, 1919", "Armenian Patriarchate, Information Bureau, 'La population arménienne du vilayet de Trébizonde', 17 Feb 1919 (via Kévorkian 2023, part III ch. 3, n. 48)", "[Greek diplomatic memorandum] Feb 1919 (not consulted)", "[Pontic leaders] Chrysanthos, Constantinides and Oeconomos to Lloyd George, 10 March 1920, FO 371/5192 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 3)", "[British minutes] Toynbee and others on a Pontic Greek memorandum of November 1918, FO 371/3419 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 3)"], "sources_modern": ["Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919–1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide 1913–1923: New Perspectives (2019), p. 10 (Trabzon Müdafaa-i Hukuk Cemiyeti founded February 1919)", "Kévorkian, Parachever un génocide: Mustafa Kemal et l'élimination des rescapés arméniens et grecs (Odile Jacob 2023), part III ch. 3, section 'Vilayet de Trébizonde', n. 48 (e-book, without print pages)", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 382 (Pontic claims of two million; Western diplomats' estimate of about 450,000)", "Le Martyre du Pont-Euxin et l'opinion publique internationale (Geneva 1922; Greek advocacy compilation, preface by A. Wuarin), p. 6 (over 800,000 Greeks; '700,000 according to the electoral agreement of 1912')", "greek-genocide.net, 'Pontus' (which itself calls 700,000 a territorial claim)", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), p. 382 and n. 3"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The 700,000 was a figure made for the case; the Greek census of 1910–12 counted 400,586 in the six metropolises. Other claims ran higher: over 800,000 Greeks in Pontus plus 250,000 refugees in Russia (Le Martyre du Pont-Euxin, 1922, p. 6), and some two million between Rize and Sinop (Pontic spokesmen). Western diplomats thought about 450,000 more likely (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 382, citing a Foreign Office minute by Toynbee of December 1918). The 850,000 of 1920 included about 250,000 in Russia and the Caucasus; we have not consulted the memorandum of 1919 and do not know whether its 700,000 did the same.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Oeconomos memorandum, 1919", "Neutral sources", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019, p. 10 (founding date)", "Armenian sources: Armenian Patriarchate report of 17 Feb 1919 (via Kévorkian 2023)", "Neutral sources: Morris & Ze'evi 2019, p. 382", "Greek sources: Greek memorandum, Feb 1919; Pontic leaders' letter, 1920", "Neutral sources: British minutes and Morris & Ze'evi"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Paris", "lat": 48.857, "lon": 2.352, "role": "memorandum"}, {"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "Trabzon Muhafaza-i Hukuk-u Milliye Cemiyeti"}]}, {"id": "H6-039", "date": "1919-03-26", "date_end": "1919-05-22", "date_text": "Spring 1919; verdict 22 May 1919", "precision": "day", "title": "The Trabzon court-martial", "summary": "An Ottoman court-martial in Constantinople tries the officials of the Trebizond vilayet for the Armenian deportations. Witnesses include the Giresun governor Arif Bey, who said Azmi ordered deportations 'towards Mosul by way of the Black Sea', meaning drowning. Cemal Azmi and the CUP secretary Nail Bey are sentenced to death in absentia. Azmi is shot in Berlin by an Armenian avenger on 17 April 1922.", "people": ["Cemal Azmi", "Nail Bey", "Arif Bey", "Arshavir Shirakian"], "sources_primary": ["Takvim-i Vekayi no. 3616 (6 Aug 1919), verdict", "Session reports, March–April 1919"], "sources_modern": ["Dadrian & Akçam, Judgment at Istanbul (2011)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Most accounts date the verdict to 22 May 1919; the published verdict carries the date 8 July 1919. The trial opened about 26 March.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: Takvim-i Vekayi 3616 (verdict); session reports, 1919 (testimony of Arif Bey)", "Neutral sources: Akçam (Dadrian & Akçam 2011)", "Armenian sources: Dadrian"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "court-martial"}, {"name": "Berlin", "lat": 52.52, "lon": 13.405, "role": "Azmi killed, 17 Apr 1922"}]}, {"id": "H6-040", "date": "1919-04-29", "date_end": "1919-05-16", "date_text": "29 April – 16 May 1919", "precision": "day", "title": "Chrysanthos at the Peace Conference", "summary": "Chrysanthos arrives in Paris on 29 April with an 18-page memorandum for an independent Pontic republic. On 16 May, with Venizelos' consent, he meets President Wilson and raises an American or Greek mandate for Pontus. The proposed state was to cover the sancaks of Trebizond, Samsun (Canik), Sinop and Amasya, later extended to Karahisar.", "people": ["Chrysanthos", "Woodrow Wilson", "Eleftherios Venizelos"], "sources_primary": ["Chrysanthos' memorandum, 1919", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.772–775; pp.778–779 (account of a participant)"], "sources_modern": ["Petsalis-Diomidis, Balkan Studies (1972)", "Erimtan 2008", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 383 (Chesbrough, Aug 1919: influential Greeks of Trebizond, and Chrysanthos, opposed to separatism)", "Georganopoulos, 'The Evidence of the French Commission in Pontus on the Anti-Hellenic Persecutions … (1919–1920)', in Akçam, Kyriakidis et al. (eds) (Routledge 2023), p. 77 (Lépissier, March 1919: Chrysanthos, Germanos, Lavrentios and Konstantinidis lead a movement for autonomy, then union with Greece)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Wilson's reported promise to vote for independence comes from Greek accounts. Chrysanthos' position is reported differently: the French commissioner Lépissier (March 1919) counted him among the leaders of a movement for autonomy and then union with Greece (Georganopoulos, p. 77), whereas the American Chesbrough, who toured Pontus in the summer of 1919, reported that many influential Greeks of Trebizond 'view this policy [of separatism] with disfavor' and that 'the local Greek Archbishop … was also opposed' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 383). Chrysanthos' own account of 1933 (pp. 772–775) presents his line in Paris as a Greek-Muslim administration of Pontus 'on equal terms' ('ἐν ἰσοπολιτείᾳ'), with a possible federation with Armenia, cites his memorandum La question du Pont-Euxin of 2 May 1919, and is silent on an independent republic and on Wilson. It was written fourteen years later and should be weighed against the documents of 1919.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos' memorandum, 1919; Petsalis-Diomidis 1972", "Neutral sources: Erimtan 2008", "Neutral sources: Chesbrough (US) via Morris & Ze'evi 2019, p. 383; Lépissier (French) via Georganopoulos 2023, p. 77", "Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Paris", "lat": 48.857, "lon": 2.352, "role": "Peace Conference"}, {"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "proposed capital"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "within the proposed state"}, {"name": "Sinop", "lat": 42.026, "lon": 35.151, "role": "within the proposed state"}, {"name": "Amasya", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "within the proposed state"}, {"name": "Şebinkarahisar", "lat": 40.288, "lon": 38.424, "role": "added in later versions"}]}, {"id": "H6-041", "date": "1919-05-15", "date_text": "15 May 1919", "precision": "day", "title": "The Greek landing at Smyrna (context)", "summary": "Greek troops land at Smyrna under Allied mandate, with killings of Turkish civilians on the first day. The landing begins the Greco-Turkish war of 1919–22 and turns Turkish opinion; from now on Pontic Greeks were treated as a fifth column of an enemy state.", "people": ["Eleftherios Venizelos"], "sources_primary": ["Inter-Allied Commission of Inquiry report (1919)"], "sources_modern": ["Smith, Ionian Vision (1973)"], "confidence": "high", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Inter-Allied Commission of Inquiry (1919); Smith 1973"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Smyrna", "lat": 38.419, "lon": 27.129, "role": "landing"}]}, {"id": "H6-042", "date": "1919-05-19", "date_end": "1919-09-11", "date_text": "19 May 1919 (Havza 28 May; Amasya 22 June; Erzurum 23 July – 7 Aug; Sivas 4–11 Sept)", "precision": "day", "title": "Mustafa Kemal lands at Samsun", "summary": "Mustafa Kemal arrives at Samsun aboard the Bandırma as inspector of the Ninth Army, officially to restore order. From Havza (circular of 28 May) and Amasya (circular of 22 June) he organises resistance, then the congresses of Erzurum and Sivas. In Turkey 19 May is the start of the National Struggle; in Greece it is the day of remembrance of the Pontic genocide.", "people": ["Mustafa Kemal (Atatürk)"], "sources_primary": ["Mustafa Kemal, Nutuk (1927)", "his reports of May 1919", "Mustafa Kemal, Nutuk (1927) (date of the landing)"], "sources_modern": ["Mango, Atatürk (1999)", "Law 2193/1994 (the Greek choice of 19 May)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Kemal's reports of late May 1919 of 'forty guerrilla' bands working for a 'Pontus state' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 384, after Shaw) are given under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H6-T04, H7-T03), with Morris & Ze'evi's finding that the agitation produced 'almost no acts of overt rebellion' (p. 383). The Turkish foreign ministry's objection to the Greek day of remembrance is given there too (H7-T09).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Mustafa Kemal, Nutuk (dates and itinerary)", "Neutral sources: Mango 1999", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Nutuk (date); the Turkish commemoration of 19 May", "Greek sources: Law 2193/1994"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "landing, 19 May 1919"}, {"name": "Havza", "lat": 40.97, "lon": 35.662, "role": "circular, 28 May 1919"}, {"name": "Amasya", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "circular, 22 June 1919"}, {"name": "Erzurum", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.268, "role": "congress, 23 July – 7 Aug 1919"}, {"name": "Sivas", "lat": 39.748, "lon": 37.017, "role": "congress, 4–11 Sept 1919"}, {"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "landing"}]}, {"id": "H6-043", "date": "1919-07", "date_end": "1921-02", "date_text": "1919–1921", "precision": "month", "title": "Repatriation of Caucasus Greeks to Greece", "summary": "In summer 1919 the Greek Ministry of Relief, under Nikos Kazantzakis, sends a mission to the Caucasus to bring Greeks to Macedonia. Between May 1920 and February 1921, by Topalidis' account, about 53,000 Pontic Greeks sail from Batum to Greece, three-quarters of them from Kars and Ardahan, which fell to the Turkish army in the war with Armenia (Kars, 30 Oct 1920).", "people": ["Nikos Kazantzakis", "Ioannis Stavridakis"], "sources_modern": ["Pratsinakis 2013 (PhD)", "Vergeti 1991", "Topalidis, 'The relocation of Greeks from Pontos'", "Georganopoulos, 'The Evidence of the French Commission in Pontus on the Anti-Hellenic Persecutions … (1919–1920)', in Akçam, Kyriakidis et al. (eds) (Routledge 2023), p. 81 and n. 71 (Greek Foreign Ministry document of 1 Aug 1919: briefing of the Ministry of Relief to Kazantzakis at Batum; the Greek government temporarily stopped repatriation from southern Russia to Pontus in July 1919)", "Agtzidis, Παρευξείνιος Διασπορά (2nd ed., Thessaloniki 2001), p. 165 [Greek] (the committee: Kazantzakis, Lt-Col Ir. Polemarchakis, I. Zervos; Kazantzakis's telegram to Venizelos: 500,000 Greeks in Russia, about 100,000 wishing to settle in Greece; relief for more than 70,000 refugees; after Greek Foreign Ministry files, 1919)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The Kazantzakis mission at Batum is attested by a Greek Foreign Ministry document of 1 August 1919 (Georganopoulos, n. 71). Agtzidis (2001, p. 165) names its members (Kazantzakis, Polemarchakis, Zervos), quotes Kazantzakis' estimate that about 100,000 Greeks of Transcaucasia wished to move to Greece, and says that the relief service fed more than 70,000 refugees. In July 1919 the Greek government, under British pressure, for a time stopped the return of refugees from southern Russia to Pontus (Georganopoulos, p. 81). The 53,000 who sailed in 1920–21 is Topalidis' figure; we have not found it in another source.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Pratsinakis 2013; Vergeti 1991; Topalidis"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Batumi", "lat": 41.642, "lon": 41.634, "role": "port of departure"}, {"name": "Kars", "lat": 40.602, "lon": 43.097, "role": "origin; taken by Turkish forces 30 Oct 1920"}, {"name": "Ardahan", "lat": 41.111, "lon": 42.702, "role": "origin"}, {"name": "Tiflis", "lat": 41.716, "lon": 44.783, "role": "Greek mission base"}, {"name": "Thessaloniki", "lat": 40.64, "lon": 22.944, "role": "destination"}]}, {"id": "C3-050", "date": "1919-09", "date_text": "September 1919", "precision": "month", "title": "Negotiations in Constantinople: ten terms for an autonomous Pontus under mandate", "summary": "Chrysanthos writes that in Constantinople in September 1919 he negotiated with the former vali Süleyman Necmi Bey, the Trebizond deputy Hafız Mehmed Bey, the former grand vizier İzzet Pasha and Kara Vasıf Bey (Kemal's representatives) 'περὶ τῆς ἐν ἰσοπολιτείᾳ Ἑλλήνων καὶ Μουσουλμάνων αὐτονομίας τοῦ Πόντου ὑπὸ ἐντολὴν (mandat) τῆς κοινωνίας τῶν ἐθνῶν' (on the autonomy of Pontus, with Greeks and Muslims equal, under a League of Nations mandate). He prints ten terms written in Turkish in Hafız Mehmed's hand (fig. 120): a mandate; free community institutions and courts; courts, councils, police and gendarmerie half Muslim and half Greek; Greek deputies, ministers and battalions; and Greek as an official language.", "people": ["Chrysanthos", "Süleyman Necmi Bey", "Hafız Mehmed Bey", "Ahmed İzzet Pasha", "Kara Vasıf Bey"], "sources_primary": ["Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.773–774 (account of a participant)", "Ten terms in Hafız Mehmed's hand (facsimile, fig. 120), as printed by Chrysanthos"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "Known from Chrysanthos and the document he reproduces.", "basis": "greek", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933, pp.773–774"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Constantinople (Istanbul)", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "negotiations"}, {"name": "Trapezous (Trabzon)", "lat": 41.0024, "lon": 39.7189, "role": "the region concerned"}]}, {"id": "H6-044", "date": "1919-12", "date_end": "1920-04", "date_text": "Dec 1919 – Apr 1920", "precision": "month", "title": "The Pontic–Armenian confederation plan", "summary": "Chrysanthos and the Greek consul Stavridakis negotiate in Tiflis and Yerevan; letters between Chrysanthos and the Armenian prime minister Khatisian (10–16 January 1920) agree in principle on a confederation, with an autonomous Pontus sharing foreign policy, customs and currency with Armenia. A military pact foresaw a Greek landing at Trebizond as the Armenians advanced on Erzurum. The Entente (February) and the San Remo conference (April 1920) refuse to give Trebizond to Armenia. Chrysanthos (pp. 775–776) also records a joint telegram of 16 January to the Greek and Armenian delegations in Paris, and British refusals (December 1919 - January 1920) of Pontic battalions at Batum and of a Greek landing at Trebizond.", "people": ["Chrysanthos", "Alexander Khatisian", "Ioannis Stavridakis", "Dimitrios Katheniotis"], "sources_primary": ["Chrysanthos–Khatisian correspondence, January 1920", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.775–776 (account of a participant)"], "sources_modern": ["Lampsidis 2002", "Bilgiç 2011", "Hovannisian, The Republic of Armenia vol. 3 (1996)"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Chrysanthos-Khatisian correspondence; Lampsidis 2002", "Armenian sources: Hovannisian vol. 3 (1996)", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Bilgiç 2011", "Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Tiflis", "lat": 41.716, "lon": 44.783, "role": "negotiations"}, {"name": "Yerevan", "lat": 40.177, "lon": 44.513, "role": "negotiations"}, {"name": "San Remo", "lat": 43.815, "lon": 7.776, "role": "conference, April 1920"}, {"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "contested port"}]}, {"id": "H6-045", "date": "1920-04-23", "date_end": "1920-08-10", "date_text": "23 Apr – 10 Aug 1920", "precision": "day", "title": "The Ankara assembly and the Treaty of Sèvres", "summary": "The Grand National Assembly opens in Ankara on 23 April 1920. The Treaty of Sèvres (10 August) leaves the Turkish–Armenian frontier in the vilayets of Erzurum, Trebizond, Van and Bitlis to arbitration (article 89); it says nothing about Pontus. In May Venizelos told the Greek parliament that an independent Pontus could not be realised.", "people": ["Mustafa Kemal", "Eleftherios Venizelos"], "sources_primary": ["Treaty of Sèvres, art. 89"], "sources_modern": ["Yerasimos 1989"], "confidence": "high", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Treaty of Sèvres, art. 89; Yerasimos 1989", "Greek sources: Venizelos' statement in the Greek parliament (as cited on Wikipedia)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Ankara", "lat": 39.933, "lon": 32.86, "role": "Grand National Assembly"}, {"name": "Sèvres", "lat": 48.824, "lon": 2.211, "role": "treaty signed"}]}, {"id": "H6-046", "date": "1920-08-13", "date_end": "1920-12", "date_text": "1920 (Giresun ransom from 13 Aug)", "precision": "month", "title": "Escalation in 1920: Giresun, Samsun, Ordu", "summary": "In Giresun, Topal Osman, now mayor, had Greek houses destroyed and shops looted (February 1920, according to an Armenian Patriarchate report); from the night of 13 August 1920 he imprisoned all the Christian men and had five or six shot each evening until the community paid 300,000 lira, while women were raped. Greek men are deported inland from Giresun and Samsun. A British officer at Samsun reports Kemalist officers preaching the extermination of Christians, and placards at Ünye and Fatsa blaming the Christians for the occupation (spring 1920); Bishop Polykarpos of Ordu is arrested on his return from Kastamonu and taken to Ankara (early June 1920); in June, a French consular report says, the Kemalists kill Greeks mainly around Bafra. In November 1920 a Kemalist militia burns eight Greek villages near Vezirköprü with some 600 dead (Armenian Patriarchate report). In October Osman is given authority over the coast from İnebolu to Hopa. In December 1920, according to an Armenian Patriarchate report, Greek men of 18–34 are mobilised into labour battalions, and the arrests of Greek notables across Pontus begin, the prelude to the Amasya trials.", "people": ["Topal Osman", "Polykarpos of Kotyora"], "sources_primary": ["Rendel, FO memorandum of 20 March 1922 (FO E 3148/19/44)", "A.G.S. minutes, 1 and 15 Sept 1920, and A.G.S. memorandum, 20 Oct 1920, FO 371/5214, 5057 (Giresun; information from the Patriarchate's representatives; via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 129)", "Capt. J. S. Perring (Samsun), 31 March and 3 June 1920, FO 371/5047, 5050 (via Morris & Ze'evi, nn. 106–107)", "French consular representative at Samsun (de Laage de Meux) to Lépissier, 4 June and June 1920, CADN Nantes CPC/CFT/75 (via Georganopoulos 2023, nn. 107, 110)", "Armenian Patriarchate, Information Bureau, reports from Giresun (Feb and 31 Aug 1920), Vezirköprü (10 Nov 1920) and Samsun (Dec 1920) (via Kévorkian 2023, part III ch. 3, nn. 32, 92, 97)", "Greek Legation, London, memorandum by Greek army officers, 11 May 1921, FO 371/6512 (Osman's coastal command; via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 155)", "Fra Cirillo Zohrabian, A servizio dei fratelli, pp. 365–366 (persecution in eastern Pontus from December 1920; via Kyriakides & Nikoloudis 2026, p. 74)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 399, 401, 404", "Georganopoulos, 'The Evidence of the French Commission in Pontus on the Anti-Hellenic Persecutions … (1919–1920)', in Akçam, Kyriakidis et al. (eds) (Routledge 2023), p. 85", "Kévorkian, Parachever un génocide: Mustafa Kemal et l'élimination des rescapés arméniens et grecs (Odile Jacob 2023), part III ch. 3, section 'Vilayet de Trébizonde', nn. 32, 92, 97 (e-book, without print pages)", "Kyriakides (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias 2026; Greek ed. 2015), p. 74"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The Giresun ransom rests on the minutes of the Armenian-Greek Section of the British High Commission, which record what the Patriarchate's representatives told the British (Hofmann 2011, p. 67), and on Armenian Patriarchate reports. Kévorkian gives about sixty executed and dates the destruction of Greek houses to February 1920; Morris & Ze'evi date the imprisonment to 13 August 1920.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Perring (British officer, 1920); French consular representative at Samsun (1920); Rendel 1922; Morris & Ze'evi 2019; Georganopoulos 2023 (transmitter); Fr Cirillo (Catholic)", "Greek sources: A.G.S. minutes (information from the Patriarchate's representatives); Greek army officers' memorandum (1921)", "Armenian sources: Armenian Patriarchate reports (via Kévorkian 2023)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.39, "role": "hostage killings and ransom"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "arrests"}, {"name": "Ordu", "lat": 40.984, "lon": 37.879, "role": "bishop deported"}, {"name": "Fatsa", "lat": 41.03, "lon": 37.5, "role": "propaganda, pressure"}, {"name": "Vezirköprü", "lat": 41.143, "lon": 35.455, "role": "eight Greek villages burned, November 1920 (APC report)"}, {"name": "Ünye (Oinoe)", "lat": 41.127, "lon": 37.288, "role": "anti-Christian placards, spring 1920"}]}, {"id": "H6-047", "date": "1920-10-05", "date_end": "1920-11-14", "date_text": "5 Oct – 14 Nov 1920", "precision": "day", "title": "Venizelos' last Pontic plan, and his fall", "summary": "Venizelos proposes to Lloyd George an advance on Ankara and a landing at Trebizond to set up a Pontic republic; the British Cabinet defers the idea on 12 October. Venizelos loses the Greek election of 14 November 1920, which ends the Pontic project.", "people": ["Eleftherios Venizelos", "David Lloyd George"], "sources_primary": ["British Cabinet minutes, 12 Oct 1920"], "sources_modern": ["Bilgiç 2011", "Hassiotis 1985", "Yerasimos 1989"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: British Cabinet minutes, 12 Oct 1920; Yerasimos 1989", "Greek sources: Hassiotis 1985", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Bilgiç 2011"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "London", "lat": 51.507, "lon": -0.128, "role": "talks with Lloyd George"}, {"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "proposed landing"}]}, {"id": "H6-080", "date": "1920-10-20", "date_end": "1921-03-15", "date_text": "20 Oct 1920 – 15 Mar 1921", "precision": "month", "title": "The Sivas and Kastamonu independence tribunals in Pontus", "summary": "Independence tribunals, created by the Ankara assembly on 18 September 1920 against desertion and given jurisdiction over treason and espionage on 26 September, sit in the Pontus before the Samsun tribunal. The Sivas tribunal, mostly sitting at Amasya, heard 160 cases (besides desertion), passed 122 death sentences, of which 12 were carried out, and between 20 October 1920 and 15 March 1921 punished 454 Greeks for espionage, ignoring summonses, aiding bandits, desertion, joining Greek bands, 'Ponticism' (Pontusçuluk), banditry and 'suspicious behaviour'. The Kastamonu tribunal, covering Sinop and the western coast, passed 420 sentences between 16 October 1920 and 2 March 1921 and executed 11; the records do not say how many were Greek. All but the Ankara tribunal were abolished on 17 February 1921 and re-established in July.", "sources_primary": ["Tribunal dockets, TBMM archive (as used by Aybars, İstiklal Mahkemeleri, 1975, pp. 124–138)"], "sources_modern": ["Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919–1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide 1913–1923: New Perspectives (2019), p. 16"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The figures come from the tribunals' own dockets, as read in the parliamentary archive by the Turkish historian Aybars and reported by Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, Turkish journalists who call the events a genocide. They are counts of persons sentenced, not of the guilty, and the charges are the tribunals' own.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: tribunal dockets (counts of sentences: admissions)", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Aybars 1975; Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019 (transmitters)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amasya", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "usual seat of the Sivas Independence Tribunal"}, {"name": "Sivas", "lat": 39.748, "lon": 37.017, "role": "nominal seat of the tribunal"}, {"name": "Kastamonu", "lat": 41.376, "lon": 33.776, "role": "Kastamonu Independence Tribunal"}]}, {"id": "H6-048", "date": "1920-11-22", "date_text": "22 November 1920", "precision": "day", "title": "Wilson's arbitral award on the Turkish–Armenian frontier", "summary": "Wilson's 'Decision … respecting the Frontier between Turkey and Armenia' (Washington, 22 November 1920) awards Armenia the city and harbour of Trebizond and the 'Karshut' (Harşit) valley 'ending at the town of Tireboli'. The line runs from the Kelkit Chai north to Borgha Paya (about 38 km south-west of Gümüşhane), then along the Pontic crest to the sea 'approximately 9 kilometers eastward from the city of Kerasun', about 1 km west of Keşap. Kerasun and Ordu 'and hinterland' stay Turkish. Wilson notes that the Pontic request concerned 'the region stretching from Riza to a point west of Sinope' and that his jurisdiction 'does not include the possibility of decision or recommendation by me' on it; that the majority in the vilayet was Muslim; and that 'the Armenian element was undeniably inferior numerically to the Greek portion of the Christian minority'. The award was never applied: Armenia had already been defeated, and signed the Treaty of Alexandropol on 2-3 December 1920.", "people": ["Woodrow Wilson", "Bainbridge Colby"], "sources_primary": ["Wilson's arbitral decision, Washington, 22 Nov 1920, with Colby's despatch No. 671, in FRUS 1920 vol. III, doc. 949 (history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1920v03/d949)"], "sources_modern": ["Papian, Iran & the Caucasus 11:2 (2007)", "Hovannisian, The Republic of Armenia vol. 4 (1996)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Some works date the Treaty of Alexandropol to 2 November; the usual date is the night of 2–3 December 1920.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: FRUS 1920 vol. III, doc. 949", "Armenian sources: Hovannisian vol. 4; Papian 2007"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Keşap", "lat": 40.917, "lon": 38.512, "role": "boundary meets the coast about 1 km west"}, {"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.39, "role": "left to Turkey"}, {"name": "Tirebolu", "lat": 41.006, "lon": 38.815, "role": "awarded to Armenia"}, {"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "awarded to Armenia as its port"}, {"name": "Gyumri (Alexandropol)", "lat": 40.789, "lon": 43.848, "role": "Treaty of Alexandropol, 2–3 Dec 1920"}]}, {"id": "H6-049", "date": "1920-12-09", "date_end": "1922-02-08", "date_text": "9 Dec 1920 – 8 Feb 1922", "precision": "day", "title": "The Central Army (Merkez Ordusu) under Nureddin Pasha", "summary": "On 9 December 1920 Ankara forms the Central Army, about 15,000 men under Nureddin Pasha with headquarters at Amasya, to deal with the 'Pontic question' and the Kurdish Alevi rising of Koçgiri; it suppresses Koçgiri and carries out the operations and deportations in Pontus, working with irregulars such as Topal Osman's men. Martial law is declared in Canik, Tokat, Amasya and Çorum on 5 March 1921. Nureddin, who called the Greeks 'a snake' whose 'poison' was their women, is removed by the Assembly on 3 November 1921 on charges that included using irregular forces, taking a bribe from a Pontic Greek, ordering public plunder during the relocation of the Greeks and 'harming the Muslims by setting the stage for the Pontians to take to the mountains'; at Mustafa Kemal's insistence the Assembly dropped his trial in January 1922, and the army was abolished on 8 February 1922.", "people": ["Nureddin Pasha", "Topal Osman", "Mustafa Kemal", "Nureddin Pasha (Sakallı Nureddin)"], "sources_primary": ["TBMM secret session records (Gizli Celse Zabıtları), 5 Oct 1921 – 17 Jan 1922", "Nureddin's defence of 30 Dec 1921 and 3 Jan 1922 (Balcıoğlu 2000/2003)", "[Turkish official] Türk İstiklal Harbi series (Genelkurmay Harp Tarihi Başkanlığı), as cited on Wikipedia", "[Turkish documents] ATASE and TBMM records, via Balcıoğlu, İki İsyan (2003), pp. 86, 103-104, and Aybars, İstiklal Mahkemeleri (1975), pp. 124-125"], "sources_modern": ["Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919–1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide 1913–1923: New Perspectives (2019), pp. 12, 14, 24–25 (after Balcıoğlu, İki İsyan, pp. 86, 273)", "Biber, in Millî Mücadele'nin Yerel Tarihi vol. 6 (TÜBA), ch. 13", "Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği (2016), p. 130, after Balcıoğlu 2000 ('snake' quotation; see B1-022)", "Dağlıoğlu & Korucu, bianet 19 May 2018 (earlier version of the same work)", "Mango, Atatürk (1999), pp. 329-330", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), pp. 12, 16, 19, 24", "Çetinoğlu, 'Pontos'taki Düzmece İstiklal Mahkemeleri ve Yargılamaları' (posted 7 Jan 2020; reprinted in Çilingir 2016, pp. 229ff.) [Turkish writer], p. 4 (9 Feb 1921 Cabinet decree expelling priests; 3 Feb 1921 joint decision to arrest those involved in 'Ponticism')", "Morris & Ze'evi 2019, p. 404 ('Kemal knew how to get the worst out of his subordinates'); p. 406 (Nureddin charged for 'the mal-execution of his orders', as told to US diplomats); p. 415 (Yusuf Kemal: 'local officials' exaggerated zeal') [international scholars, Israel]", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019, p. 27 (Nureddin 'one of the primary perpetrators') [Turkish, critical scholarship]", "Hofmann 2011, p. 76 (no action against Osman after Merzifon) [international scholar, Germany]"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Who was responsible for the excesses of 1921 is disputed: Turkish deputies blamed Nureddin personally; others stress that Kemal protected him and that the deportation policy was Ankara's. According to the closed sessions of 16–17 January 1922, Nureddin had also ordered Topal Osman to kill the Koçgiri leaders who had agreed to negotiate, and had barred 56 Samsun notables who objected to the deportation of Greek women and children from travelling (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 14, n. 16). Morris & Ze'evi write that 'Kemal knew how to get the worst out of his subordinates' (p. 404), that Nureddin was charged with 'the mal-execution of his orders', as US diplomats were told (p. 406), and quote Yusuf Kemal on 'local officials' exaggerated zeal' (p. 415); Korucu & Dağlıoğlu (p. 27) call Nureddin 'one of the primary perpetrators'; Hofmann (2011, p. 76) notes that no action was taken against Osman after Merzifon. The Sivas tribunal's record (160 cases besides desertion, 122 death sentences, of which 12 were carried out) is not broken down by community in the summary we have read. Ankara's own description of the army's mission, against a 'Pontus' rising, is given under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H6-T04).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: TBMM secret session records, 1921-22; Nureddin's defence (admissions)", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019 (Turkish journalists, published chapter); Balcıoğlu; Çilingir; Biber (TÜBA) (dates)", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Türk İstiklal Harbi series; Aybars; Balcıoğlu (transmitters of dates and counts)", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019; Çetinoğlu", "Neutral sources: Mango 1999"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amasya", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "operations centre"}, {"name": "Sivas", "lat": 39.748, "lon": 37.017, "role": "army area"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "operations"}, {"name": "Tokat", "lat": 40.314, "lon": 36.554, "role": "martial law, 5 Mar 1921"}, {"name": "Çorum", "lat": 40.549, "lon": 34.954, "role": "martial law, 5 Mar 1921"}, {"name": "Amasya (Amaseia)", "lat": 40.654, "lon": 35.833, "role": "Central Army headquarters"}]}, {"id": "H6-078", "date": "1921", "date_end": "1923-02", "date_text": "1921 - February 1923", "precision": "year", "title": "Turkish state counts of Greeks killed in the Pontus operations, 1921–1923", "summary": "Turkish military and parliamentary records count the Greeks killed in the Pontus operations: 1,275 killed (of 4,444 'neutralised') between 29 December 1921 and 20 March 1922, and 3,777 'armed Greeks' killed between 20 March and 15 May 1922 (Interior Minister Fethi in closed session, 10 June 1922); about 15,000 killed or captured in the two months after 18 February 1922 (the 10th Division's commander); and, when the operations ended in February 1923, 11,198 Greeks killed and 10,886 surrendered. These are the state's own counts of the dead, as the records class them, and so are lower limits.", "sources_primary": ["TBMM Gizli Zabıt Ceridesi, Term I, vol. III, 10 June 1922, p. 373 (Fethi's figures)", "Central Army and 10th Division records (ATASE; not consulted), as used by Balcıoğlu, İki İsyan (2003), pp. 86–127"], "sources_modern": ["Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919–1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide 1913–1923: New Perspectives (2019), pp. 15, 25", "Dağlıoğlu & Korucu, bianet, 19 May 2018, citing Çilingir (earlier version)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The records class the dead as killed in operations against bands; that framing is given under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H6-T08). The numbers themselves are the state's own admissions of killings.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: TBMM closed session of 10 June 1922; Ankara military records (as used by Balcıoğlu)", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019 (published chapter, transmitters)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amasya", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "headquarters of the Central Army (the count covers the Pontus operations)"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "centre of the 1922 operations"}]}, {"id": "H6-050", "date": "1921-02-09", "date_end": "1921-02-12", "date_text": "February 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "Merzifon, Samsun and Zile: the searches and the first arrests for Amasya", "summary": "At the end of January 1921 the Central Army arrests 75 Greeks of Samsun for the Sivas Independence Tribunal, and in February Mustafa Kemal orders such arrests continued. In February a Muslim teacher of Anatolia College at Merzifon (named in Turkish accounts as Zeki Bey) is murdered by an unknown assailant. On 16 February the Central Army searches the college, the American hospital and the Samsun metropolis; no arms are found, but the searchers seize papers they deem 'subversive'. Four teachers and two students, all members of the college's Greek literary society, called 'Pontos', are arrested; the college is closed and 29 of its 32 missionaries expelled. According to an Armenian Patriarchate report, Bishop Euthymios of Zela and about 100 notables of Zile were deported on 15 February 1921, and the protosynkellos Platon is sent to the tribunal; on 21 July 1921 the same source reported that Euthymios had died in Amasya prison of ill-treatment.", "people": ["Euthymios of Zela", "Platon Aivazidis"], "sources_primary": ["Bristol, draft letter to the Secretary of State, 28 July 1921; Theodore Riggs to friends, 17 April 1921 (via Morris & Ze'evi, nn. 220–221)", "George White, Marsovan station report, 1 July 1921 (via Kyriakidis 2023, n. 108)", "Armenian Patriarchate, Information Bureau, Zile, 15 Feb 1921 and 21 July 1921 (via Kévorkian 2023, part III ch. 3, n. 98)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 412", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919–1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide 1913–1923: New Perspectives (2019), pp. 19, 24", "Kyriakidis, 'Testimonies of American Charitable and Missionary Organizations on the Genocide of the Pontic Greeks', in Akçam, Kyriakidis et al. (eds), The Genocide of the Christian Populations in the Ottoman Empire and its Aftermath (Routledge 2023), p. 57", "Kévorkian, Parachever un génocide: Mustafa Kemal et l'élimination des rescapés arméniens et grecs (Odile Jacob 2023), part III ch. 3, section 'Vilayet de Trébizonde', n. 98 (e-book, without print pages)", "Biber (TÜBA) ch. 13 (name of the teacher; dates)", "Hovannisian ed., Armenian Sebastia/Sivas (2004), p. 223 (not consulted)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "How many of the arrested were executed: two teachers and one student (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 412) or all six (Kyriakidis, p. 57). Biber dates the sending of Euthymios and Platon to the tribunal to 9 February 1921; the Armenian Patriarchate's report from Zile has Euthymios deported on 15 February. No source we have read says who killed the teacher. Ankara's use of the seized papers as proof of a Pontic conspiracy is under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H6-T05).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Bristol; Riggs; White (US) via Morris & Ze'evi 2019, p. 412 and Kyriakidis 2023, p. 57", "Armenian sources: Armenian Patriarchate reports (via Kévorkian 2023)", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019, pp. 19, 24; Biber (TÜBA) (name of the teacher, dates)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Merzifon (Marsovan), Anatolia College", "lat": 40.873, "lon": 35.463, "role": "searches and arrests"}, {"name": "Amasya", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "tribunal prison"}, {"name": "Zile", "lat": 40.303, "lon": 35.886, "role": "Bishop Euthymios and about 100 notables deported, 15 Feb 1921"}]}, {"id": "H6-051", "date": "1921-03", "date_end": "1921-06", "date_text": "March – June 1921", "precision": "month", "title": "The Koçgiri rebellion and its suppression (context)", "summary": "Alevi Kurds of the Koçgiri region (Zara, İmranlı) rise for autonomy. The Central Army crushes the revolt, using Topal Osman's Giresun men as the 47th Regiment; deputies later accused Nureddin of excesses in Sivas. The same units then turned to Pontus.", "people": ["Nureddin Pasha", "Topal Osman", "Hüseyin Avni Alparslan"], "sources_primary": ["TBMM secret session of 5 Oct 1921"], "sources_modern": ["Doğan 2014", "Alpaslan 2007", "bianet 2018"], "confidence": "high", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: TBMM secret session of 5 Oct 1921", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Doğan 2014; Alpaslan 2007; bianet 2018"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "İmranlı (Koçgiri)", "lat": 39.878, "lon": 38.113, "role": "rebellion area"}, {"name": "Zara", "lat": 39.896, "lon": 37.752, "role": "rebellion area"}]}, {"id": "H6-052", "date": "1921-03", "date_end": "1921-06", "date_text": "March – June 1921", "precision": "month", "title": "Labour-battalion call-up and village burnings in western Pontus", "summary": "In February 1921 Interior Minister Fethi, after visiting the region, urges Kemal to authorise 'more expeditious and larger scale' deportations. On 2 March the Assembly orders labour battalions for non-Muslims re-formed, and in March about 1,500 non-Muslim men of Havza, Merzifon, Tokat, Çorum, Sivas and Samsun are sent to them; on 18 April the battalions at Samsun, Havza, Merzifon, Sivas and Tokat held 597, 339, 204, 285 and 201 men, and in May they were sent east of the Trabzon–Erzincan line to stop desertions (Çilingir, pp. 112–114). On 16 April, according to an Armenian Patriarchate report, the men of Gümüşhane aged 15–50 are called up. On 26 March Greece declared a naval blockade of the Turkish Black Sea coast. From spring the Central Army and çetes attack Greek villages around Alaçam, Bafra, Çarşamba, Havza and Vezirköprü. For the operation in the Nebyan mountains the army's orders were to 'chasten the Greeks so they never again attempt banditry' and to 'destroy villages which serve as bandit shelters', and the deputy governor of Samsun proposed that the people of all the Greek villages of the area be expelled inland, so that they would nowhere be a majority, and Turks settled in their place (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, pp. 24–25, after Balcıoğlu). In May all Christian males over 12 at Alaçam are held and massacred. US officers at Samsun quoted an American missionary that about 100 Greek villages south of Bafra had been destroyed in a few days from 17 May, the villagers murdered and 'the priests … crucified', and judged the campaign 'directed by high authority—probably Angora'.", "people": ["Nureddin Pasha", "Topal Osman"], "sources_primary": ["C. S. Joyce, diary of USS Fox, Samsun, 12–28 May 1921; CO USS Overton, diary for May 1921 (via Morris & Ze'evi, nn. 165, 181, 183)", "War Office, 'Turkish Atrocities', 22 May 1922, with intercepted text of Fethi to Kemal, Feb 1921 (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 164)", "A.G.S. records (Yeghiayan, p. 236; Alaçam), via Hofmann 2011, p. 75", "Eleftheros Typos, 13 May 1921", "Armenian Patriarchate, Information Bureau, Samsun, June 1921 (Gümüşhane call-up; via Kévorkian 2023, n. 99)", "[Turkish military] ATASE, Klasör 730, Dosya 18 (orders of 2 and 12 March 1921; strengths of 18 April 1921), via Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], pp. 112-114", "[Turkish military] ATASE documents cited in Balcıoğlu, İki İsyan (2003), pp. 103-104, via Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), pp. 24-25"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 405, 407–408", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), pp. 73, 75", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919–1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide 1913–1923: New Perspectives (2019), p. 8", "Kyriakidis, 'Testimonies of American Charitable and Missionary Organizations on the Genocide of the Pontic Greeks', in Akçam, Kyriakidis et al. (eds), The Genocide of the Christian Populations in the Ottoman Empire and its Aftermath (Routledge 2023), p. 50", "Shenk, America's Black Sea Fleet (Naval Institute Press 2012)", "Biber (TÜBA) ch. 13 (blockade)", "Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], pp. 112-114", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), pp. 8, 12, 24-25", "See also B1-001: villages around Bafra and Alaçam destroyed from April-May 1921"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The 'about 100 villages' south of Bafra is a missionary's figure, relayed by the US destroyers Fox and Overton. The Alaçam massacre reached the British through the Patriarchate's representatives, in the minutes of the Armenian-Greek Section of the British High Commission. The orders' language of 'banditry', and the claim that the villages sheltered bands, are the army's own framing (H7-T04, H7-T08). The deputy governor's proposal is reported, not quoted.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: US Navy (USS Fox, Overton) via Morris & Ze'evi 2019; War Office (intercepted telegram)", "Greek sources: A.G.S. records (Patriarchate information) via Hofmann 2011; Eleftheros Typos", "Armenian sources: Armenian Patriarchate (via Kévorkian 2023)", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019, p. 8 (1,500 to labour battalions); Biber (blockade date)", "Ottoman state records: Central Army and Defence Ministry orders (admissions)", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Balcıoğlu (transmitter)", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Çilingir; Korucu & Dağlıoğlu"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Alaçam", "lat": 41.61, "lon": 35.607, "role": "massacre of men, May 1921"}, {"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.567, "lon": 35.906, "role": "villages attacked"}, {"name": "Çarşamba", "lat": 41.199, "lon": 36.722, "role": "villages attacked"}, {"name": "Havza", "lat": 40.97, "lon": 35.662, "role": "villages attacked"}, {"name": "Vezirköprü", "lat": 41.143, "lon": 35.455, "role": "villages attacked"}, {"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "labour battalion; deputy governor's proposal"}, {"name": "Merzifon", "lat": 40.874, "lon": 35.463, "role": "labour battalion"}, {"name": "Tokat", "lat": 40.314, "lon": 36.554, "role": "labour battalion"}, {"name": "Sivas (Sebasteia)", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 37.016, "role": "labour battalion"}, {"name": "Çorum", "lat": 40.55, "lon": 34.955, "role": "labour battalion"}, {"name": "Nebyan Dağı", "lat": 41.35, "lon": 36.0, "role": "operation against the bands and their villages (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H6-053", "date": "1921-06", "date_end": "1921-09", "date_text": "Summer 1921", "precision": "month", "title": "Topal Osman in Samsun, Merzifon, Giresun and Tirebolu", "summary": "In Samsun, nightly murders in spring 1921 are followed by the first deportation of 200 men on 28 May. Topal Osman's men enter the town; a Constantinople press dispatch quoted by the British diplomat Rendel says he killed ten Greeks on arrival, arrested about 800 Greek clerks of the American Tobacco Company and 1,500 other Greeks, and deported them inland. Seventy Turkish notables telegraphed Kemal that it was against their religion to massacre women and children, and the town's mufti issued a fatwa to the same effect; Kemal replied that exile was 'a merciful act', and Osman was recalled. At Merzifon, from 23–24 July, Osman's band and then local men, gendarmes and troops spend more than a week killing, raping and looting; men and boys sheltering in the French school, which is burned, are taken out and shot. Of 2,000–2,500 Christians almost all the men are killed, and the remaining Greeks deported. Osman then moves to Tirebolu and Giresun, where many Greeks are killed or deported; he is welcomed in Ankara and given command of 6,000 men.", "people": ["Topal Osman", "George W. Rendel"], "sources_primary": ["Rendel memorandum, 20 March 1922, FO 371/7876, p. 4, quoting a New York Times dispatch of 10 July 1921 (via Hofmann 2011, p. 71)", "A.G.S. minutes (Yeghiayan, p. 221; Osman recalled)", "D. M. Hosford, memorandum, 6 Dec 1921, FO 371/6538; Gertrude Anthony to Bristol, 1 Nov 1921 (Merzifon; via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 223)", "Harmsworth in the House of Commons, 7 Nov 1921 (Hansard vol. 148, pp. 43–44), in Le Martyre du Pont-Euxin et l'opinion publique internationale (Geneva 1922; Greek advocacy compilation, preface by A. Wuarin), p. 36", "Deposition of Mehmed Rauf, Ottoman officer, 'Les événements de Marzevan', Nov 1921, APC/PAJ (via Kévorkian 2023, part III ch. 3, n. 33)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 410–413", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), pp. 71, 76–77", "Kyriakidis, 'Testimonies of American Charitable and Missionary Organizations on the Genocide of the Pontic Greeks', in Akçam, Kyriakidis et al. (eds), The Genocide of the Christian Populations in the Ottoman Empire and its Aftermath (Routledge 2023), pp. 51, 57", "Kévorkian, Parachever un génocide: Mustafa Kemal et l'élimination des rescapés arméniens et grecs (Odile Jacob 2023), part III ch. 3, section 'Vilayet de Trébizonde', nn. 33–36 (e-book, without print pages)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The Merzifon toll is given as 'upwards of 700' (Hosford, quoted by Rendel; Hofmann 2011, p. 77); 950 (the Armenian-Greek Section, and the British government in the Commons, 7 November 1921); about 1,000 in one week, 24–31 July (Anthony, cited by Kyriakidis, p. 51); about 1,200 (Bristol and Anthony, cited by Morris & Ze'evi, n. 223); 'more than a thousand' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 411); and 300 Armenians and Greeks (the Ottoman officer Mehmed Rauf, cited by Kévorkian). The damage: about 400 Armenian houses burned and the Greek quarter left untouched (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 413), or the Greek quarter burned (Rauf's deposition; the Black Book). The 800 tobacco clerks come from a press dispatch quoted by Rendel. The nationalists' denials are under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H7-T06).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Hosford, Anthony, Bristol (US); Rendel (FO); British government (Commons); Morris & Ze'evi 2019; Hofmann 2011", "Greek sources: A.G.S. minutes (Patriarchate information); Black Book notes", "Armenian sources: Armenian Patriarchate reports (via Kévorkian 2023)", "Ottoman state records: deposition of the Ottoman officer Mehmed Rauf (admission, via the Armenian Patriarchate)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "arrests, June 1921"}, {"name": "Merzifon", "lat": 40.873, "lon": 35.463, "role": "massacre, summer 1921"}, {"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.39, "role": "killings and deportation, late summer 1921"}, {"name": "Tirebolu", "lat": 41.006, "lon": 38.815, "role": "killings and deportation, late summer 1921"}]}, {"id": "H6-055", "date": "1921-06-09", "date_end": "1921-06-16", "date_text": "9 – 16 June 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "İnebolu shelled; Ankara orders Greek men off the coast", "summary": "On 29 May 1921 the idea of moving Greek men of certain ages from the Black Sea coast inland is raised in Ankara. A Greek naval squadron arrives in the Black Sea in summer 1921, stops Turkish ships and takes passengers prisoner, and on 9 June lightly shells İnebolu, without casualties according to an Italian witness. On 12 June the Ankara government orders the removal inland of all Greeks aged 15 to 50 living on the coast, applied from 16 June through the Central Army; in July the whole Black Sea coast is declared a war zone as from 12 June.", "people": ["Nureddin Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["BCA 941/95-12 … 3.24.12 (12 June 1921) and the decree of 16 June 1921, via Biber (TÜBA) ch. 13, p. 436 nn. 75-77", "Commander C. H. Knox-Little, HMS Sportive, letter of proceedings 27 July – 3 Aug 1921, FO 371/6498 (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 174)", "Diary of USS Brooks, entry for 14 July 1921 (Count Schmeccia of Lloyd Triestino; via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 175)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 407, 410", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919–1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide 1913–1923: New Perspectives (2019), pp. 8, 12–13", "Biber (TÜBA) ch. 13", "Bartrop 2014", "Mango 2002"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The age band is 15–50 in the Turkish order and in the US naval notes on the Samsun round-up of 16 June (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 410), 15–55 in a British cabinet paper (CAB 24/131, cited by Biber). Morris & Ze'evi (p. 407) record the Italian Count Schmeccia's view that the İnebolu bombardment 'produced no casualties' and was 'merely a Turkish \"excuse for the massacres\"', and note that the campaign had begun weeks before. The studies of the Turkish documents differ slightly on the sequence: Biber (from BCA 941/95-12) has the war zone declared on 12 June and the decree on 16 June; Korucu & Dağlıoğlu (pp. 12–13) have the order issued on 12 June and applied on 16 June, and the war zone declared in July with effect from 12 June. Ankara's stated reasons are under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H6-T06). The Greek navy's documented shelling of Samsun came a year later, on 7 June 1922 (H6-061).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: BCA 941/95-12 (12 June 1921); decree of 16 June 1921", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Biber (TÜBA) ch. 13; Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019, pp. 12–13 (dates)", "Neutral sources: HMS Sportive report; USS Brooks diary (Schmeccia); Morris & Ze'evi 2019, pp. 407, 410; Bartrop 2014; Mango 2002"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "İnebolu", "lat": 41.975, "lon": 33.761, "role": "shelled by Greek warships, 9 June 1921"}, {"name": "Ankara", "lat": 39.933, "lon": 32.86, "role": "decisions of 12 and 16 June 1921"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "feared landing site"}]}, {"id": "H6-056", "date": "1921-06-16", "date_end": "1921-11", "date_text": "16 June – November 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "The 1921 deportations of Pontic men, then women and children", "summary": "Convoys of men leave Samsun (from 16 June), Bafra and Alaçam; Greek men of 15–50 are rounded up at Samsun from 16 June and, according to an Armenian Patriarchate report, at Ordu, Fatsa, Ünye and Çarşamba on 27 June. On the road the escorts kill many: a deportee of the third Samsun convoy wrote that 660 were killed by the gendarmes as the column descended a mountain; Cosmos Lilidas said that of his convoy of about 1,000, which left Samsun around 20 June, 292 remained after the guards opened fire near 'Djinbush'; Stanley Hopkins of Near East Relief reported 550 Greeks executed at Kavak on 26 June and 1,300 on 15 August; and Lazistan deputies told the Assembly that the first convoy from Samsun was attacked six hours outside the town. From mid-July the remaining women and children are ordered to go too: Admiral Bristol asked Kemal on 18 July to stop it, and the Apostolic Delegate Dolci asked him on 18 August to cancel an order to exile 5,000 women and children from near Samsun; Samsun's Muslim notables protested, and the deportations there were paused in July. The route ran Samsun-Kavak-Havza-Amasya-Tokat-Sivas-Harput, then on to Diyarbakır, Bitlis and Van. At Mezre-Harput Mark Ward registered 20,526 deportees between May 1921 and February 1922, about 18,000 of them Greeks. According to Central Army records, 63,844 Greeks had been moved by November 1921, or, district by district, 58,974 (Samsun 27,995; Amasya 14,000; Giresun 8,500; Ordu 4,910; Sivas 1,448; Tokat 1,000; Çorum 571; Sinop 550) to Sivas, Tokat, Yozgat, Çorum, Şebinkarahisar and further.", "people": ["Nureddin Pasha", "Topal Osman", "Mark H. Ward", "Edith Wood", "Forrest D. Yowell", "Stanley E. Hopkins"], "sources_primary": ["Greek Patriarchate summary of a letter from a Samsun deportee, Ayou-tepe; A.G.S. minutes, 14 Sept 1921 (660; via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 282)", "USS Sands, diary at Samsun, 30 Nov 1921 (Lilidas; via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 283)", "Hopkins trip reports, Sept–Oct 1921 (via Rendel, Hofmann 2011, pp. 75, 79, and Kyriakidis 2023, p. 51)", "TBMM Gizli Zabıt Ceridesi, 5 Oct 1921 (Lazistan deputies Ziya Hurşit and Osman; via Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019, p. 13)", "Bristol to Mustafa Kemal, 18 July 1921, and Yusuf Kemal's reply of 21 July 1921 (via Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019, p. 21; Kyriakidis 2023, p. 52)", "Dolci to Mustafa Kemal, telegram, 18 Aug 1921, ASV, Arch. Deleg. Turchia, Busta I. F. (via Kyriakides & Nikoloudis 2026, p. 79)", "Ward, diary extracts and table (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 295)", "Yowell and Ward to Jackson, 5 Apr 1922", "Central Army records (in Turkish archives)", "Armenian Patriarchate, Information Bureau, Samsun, June and Aug 1921 (via Kévorkian 2023, part III ch. 3, nn. 99–100)"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 408, 410–411, 415, 423, 425–426", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919–1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide 1913–1923: New Perspectives (2019), pp. 13, 21, 25 (after Balcıoğlu 2003, pp. 86–127)", "Biber (TÜBA) ch. 13, p. 438 n. 97, after Balcıoğlu 1991, p. 109 (63,844)", "Kyriakidis, 'Testimonies of American Charitable and Missionary Organizations on the Genocide of the Pontic Greeks', in Akçam, Kyriakidis et al. (eds), The Genocide of the Christian Populations in the Ottoman Empire and its Aftermath (Routledge 2023), pp. 51–53, 59", "Kyriakides (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias 2026; Greek ed. 2015), p. 79", "Kévorkian, Parachever un génocide: Mustafa Kemal et l'élimination des rescapés arméniens et grecs (Odile Jacob 2023), part III ch. 3, section 'Vilayet de Trébizonde', nn. 99–100 (e-book, without print pages)", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), pp. 75, 78–81", "Bartrop 2014 (about 21,000 from Samsun)", "Prott 2016", "B1 study (B1-002, B1-009, B1-011–B1-014)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Other figures: 216 killed near Kavak, 258 at 'Djib-is-han' and 700 of 850 at 'Tsakali on the Mamour-dagh', that is Çakallı on the Samsun–Kavak road (the Patriarchate, 13/26 November 1921, in Le Martyre du Pont-Euxin, p. 44); 660 and 530 in two groups (US destroyer logs, cited by Kyriakidis, p. 51); 3,000 killed around Kavak (one testimony, p. 52). The Armenian Patriarchate's Information Bureau reported 30,000 Greeks massacred between Ünye and Samsun by August and 6,500 of 7,000 conscripts killed on the Samsun–Kavak road on 2 August (Kévorkian, n. 100); these are far above any other source and are not confirmed. Ward's total is 20,526 in Morris & Ze'evi (p. 426); his pamphlet gives 20,378. The two Central Army counts (63,844 and 58,974) come from the same records through different works of Balcıoğlu; Çilingir's 'about 59,000' matches the second. Overall estimates: 'well over 35,000' deported from Pontus in 1921 (Rendel), and 'at least 75,000', of whom 20,000 survived (missionaries, reported by the US consul at Aleppo in March 1922; Morris & Ze'evi, p. 415). Of 30,000 who passed Sivas, 5,000 died before Harput and 5,000 escaped (Yowell and Ward; Morris & Ze'evi, p. 425).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: US Navy (Sands; logbooks); NER (Hopkins, Ward, Yowell); Bristol; Dolci (Holy See); Rendel; Morris & Ze'evi 2019; Kyriakidis 2023; Kyriakides & Nikoloudis 2026; Hofmann 2011; Bartrop 2014; Prott 2016", "Greek sources: deportee's letter (via the Patriarchate and A.G.S.); Black Book notes; Patriarchate letter of 13/26 Nov 1921", "Ottoman state records: Central Army records (63,844; 58,974); TBMM closed session of 5 Oct 1921 (Lazistan deputies' admission)", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Biber (TÜBA); Balcıoğlu; Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019; Çilingir (transmitters; dates)", "Armenian sources: Armenian Patriarchate reports (via Kévorkian 2023)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "waypoint 1: origin"}, {"name": "Kavak", "lat": 41.079, "lon": 36.044, "role": "waypoint 2: first convoy attacked"}, {"name": "Havza", "lat": 40.97, "lon": 35.662, "role": "waypoint 3"}, {"name": "Amasya", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "waypoint 4"}, {"name": "Tokat", "lat": 40.314, "lon": 36.554, "role": "waypoint 5"}, {"name": "Sivas", "lat": 39.748, "lon": 37.017, "role": "waypoint 6"}, {"name": "Harput", "lat": 38.706, "lon": 39.256, "role": "waypoint 7: gathering point"}, {"name": "Diyarbakır", "lat": 37.914, "lon": 40.23, "role": "waypoint 8"}, {"name": "Bitlis", "lat": 38.401, "lon": 42.108, "role": "waypoint 9"}, {"name": "Ordu", "lat": 40.984, "lon": 37.879, "role": "origin of July–Aug convoys"}, {"name": "Şebinkarahisar", "lat": 40.288, "lon": 38.424, "role": "destination for Ordu deportees"}, {"name": "Divriği", "lat": 39.371, "lon": 38.114, "role": "destination (Ward's table, 25 July)"}]}, {"id": "H6-057", "date": "1921-08", "date_end": "1921-09-12", "date_text": "August – 12 September 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "Trebizond men marched inland; Santa occupied and deported", "summary": "In early April 1921 elders of the Greek community of Trebizond, among them the publisher Nikos Kapetanidis and Matthaios Kofidis, are imprisoned on charges of planning a Pontic state; US naval reports have the men of Trebizond deported in groups of fifty twice a week in May and about 300 Greeks on their way to Erzurum by the end of July. In August over 500 Greek men who reported for paid 'fortification work' are imprisoned, deported in groups of fifty and many of them shot at night in groups of ten, according to two refugee witnesses. In early September the army orders the men of Santa aged 20-45 to enlist; on 6 September it occupies three of the Santa villages, and on 10-12 September the people of six villages and then Pistofantoi are deported by way of Erzurum, where typhus broke out. About 370 reached their place of exile in October 1921 and fewer than 200 came back in January 1923. Kourtidis' guerrillas and 300-400 women and children stay in the mountains and fight on until 1923.", "people": ["Efkleidis Kourtidis", "N. Topalidis"], "sources_primary": ["N. Topalidis 1952 (memoir)", "Chimonidis (Santa figures)", "Efraimidis 1972–74", "'Report Made from Information Given by John Eufremides and George Isaridi, Refugees from Pontus, November 1921', FO 371/7876 (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 284)", "US naval reports (via Kyriakidis 2023, pp. 52–53, after Shenk 2012, p. 110, and Shenk & Koktzoglou, The Greek Genocide, p. 339)", "Fra Cirillo Zohrabian, A servizio dei fratelli, pp. 371–373 (via Kyriakides & Nikoloudis 2026, p. 75)"], "sources_modern": ["Topalidis, 'Santa, Pontos after 1914', citing Fotiadis 2019", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 423", "Kyriakidis, 'Testimonies of American Charitable and Missionary Organizations on the Genocide of the Pontic Greeks', in Akçam, Kyriakidis et al. (eds), The Genocide of the Christian Populations in the Ottoman Empire and its Aftermath (Routledge 2023), pp. 52–53", "Kyriakides (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias 2026; Greek ed. 2015), p. 75"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The 'fortification work' episode of August 1921 rests on two Greek refugees' statements recorded by the British (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 423). The deportation of Trebizond's men in groups of fifty is dated May 1921 in the US naval reports cited by Kyriakidis, and May 1922 by Rumbold and by Morris & Ze'evi (p. 419; H6-060); it may have happened in both years. The place 'Hunuz' (Χουνούς) on the Santa route has not been identified.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: N. Topalidis 1952 (memoir); Chimonidis; Efraimidis 1972-74; Topalidis citing Fotiadis 2019; two refugee witnesses (via the FO and Morris & Ze'evi 2019, p. 423)", "Neutral sources: US naval reports (via Kyriakidis 2023); Fr Cirillo (Catholic memoir, via Kyriakides & Nikoloudis 2026, p. 75)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "men imprisoned and marched inland"}, {"name": "Santa (Dumanlı)", "lat": 40.6, "lon": 39.7, "role": "occupied 6 Sept; deported 10–12 Sept 1921"}, {"name": "Erzurum", "lat": 39.905, "lon": 41.268, "role": "deportation route; typhus"}, {"name": "Erzincan", "lat": 39.75, "lon": 39.5, "role": "about 200 sent on from Erzurum"}]}, {"id": "H6-058", "date": "1921-08-20", "date_end": "1921-09-21", "date_text": "17 August – December 1921 (the main hangings on 21 September)", "precision": "day", "title": "The Samsun Independence Tribunal at Amasya", "summary": "The Samsun Independence Tribunal (İstiklâl Mahkemesi), which began work on 17 August 1921 with jurisdiction over Tokat, Amasya, Samsun, Ordu, Giresun and Sivas but sitting at Amasya, tries Greek notables arrested since December 1920 on charges of working for a Pontic state. Those hanged include the former deputy Matthaios Kofidis, the journalist Nikos Kapetanidis, the Ottoman Bank director at Samsun Pavlos Papadopoulos, teachers of Anatolia College and the archimandrite Platon Aivazidis, about seventy years old; Bishop Euthymios of Zela had died in prison at Amasya. Chrysanthos, Germanos of Amaseia, Polykarpos of Neocaesarea and Laurentios of Chaldia were condemned in absentia. By its own records the tribunal had executed 177 people (three of them Muslims) and sentenced 74 to death in absentia by 10 October 1921, and by December it had passed 1,163 sentences, 622 of them death sentences (137 in absentia). Chrysanthos (pp. 775-782) writes that Greek clergy and notables were hanged 'by the hundreds', naming Kofidis as the representative of the metropolis of Trebizond; these are round words, not a count, and he does not name the tribunal.", "people": ["Emin Bey (Gevecioğlu)", "Matthaios Kofidis", "Nikos Kapetanidis", "Pavlos Papadopoulos", "Iordanis Totomanidis", "Euthymios of Zela", "Platon Aivazidis", "Chrysanthos", "Germanos Karavangelis", "Laurentios of Chaldia", "Euthymios Zelon"], "sources_primary": ["Samsun Independence Tribunal dockets, TBMM archive (as used by Aybars, İstiklal Mahkemeleri, 1975, pp. 168–174)", "Ehali (Samsun), lists of 12/25 Sept and 4 Oct 1921, as reprinted in Central Council of Pontus, Black Book: The Tragedy of Pontus 1914–1922 (Athens 1922), sect. VI, leaves 34–36 (archive.org scan; no printed page numbers, cited by section and scan leaf)", "Ecumenical Patriarch's telegram to the League of Nations quoting Ehali and Helal of 18, 19 and 25 Sept 1921 (The Scotsman, 7 Oct 1921; via Hofmann 2011, p. 75)", "A.G.S. minutes, 12 and 26 Oct 1921 (via Hofmann 2011, pp. 75–76)", "Hosford, memorandum, Oct 1921 (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 255)", "Locum tenens of the Patriarchate to the League of Nations, 13/26 Nov 1921, in Le Martyre du Pont-Euxin et l'opinion publique internationale (Geneva 1922; Greek advocacy compilation, preface by A. Wuarin), pp. 44–45", "Tepeyran, Belgelerle Kurtuluş Savaşı Anıları (1982), p. 81 (485 death sentences)", "Hellenic and British protests, 1921", "US Senate, Senator William H. King, 22 Dec 1921", "Chrysanthos 1933, Η Εκκλησία Τραπεζούντος (Αρχείον Πόντου 4–5), pp.775–782 (account of a participant)", "[Turkish press] list of those hanged, Ehali (Samsun), 25 Sep 1921: 155 names via Vergeti 1993, p. 77 (as cited on Wikipedia); 132 names in Çilingir, Pontos Gerçeği 1914-1923 (Istanbul: Belge, 2016) [Turkish writer], pp. 123-126 (from TBMM Arşivi, Rumuz i, Dosya 8, and the Ehali list)", "[Turkish documents] Samsun Independence Tribunal dockets, via Aybars, İstiklal Mahkemeleri (1975), pp. 172-174, cited by Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), p. 18", "[US] Bristol to SecState, 19 Oct 1921, NARA 767.68/155; Patriarchal vicar Dorotheos to Bristol, 24 Oct 1921 (via Bilgiç, Doğu Karadeniz Rumları (TTK, 2011), via Çetinoğlu, 'Pontos'taki Düzmece İstiklal Mahkemeleri ve Yargılamaları' (posted 7 Jan 2020; reprinted in Çilingir 2016, pp. 229ff.) [Turkish writer], pp. 8-9)", "[British] Rumbold to Curzon, 15 Oct 1921, Documents on British Foreign Policy (protest telegram of the Allied High Commissioners; via Çetinoğlu, p. 9)", "[US missionary; British] Hosford memorandum, Oct 1921; A.G.S. minutes, 12 Oct 1921, FO 371/6549 (via Morris & Ze'evi n. 255)", "[Turkish memoir] Tepeyran 1982, p. 81: 485 death sentences by the Independence Tribunal (as cited on Wikipedia)"], "sources_modern": ["Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919–1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide 1913–1923: New Perspectives (2019), pp. 16–18", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 416–417", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), pp. 74–76", "Kyriakides (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias 2026; Greek ed. 2015), p. 79 (72 hanged in the square of Amasya on 8/21 Sept 1921, after Fotiadis 2004, pp. 412–418)", "Tsirkinidis 1999, p. 192 (177 hanged, 44 in absentia)", "Hofmann, Kurzer Abriss … (aga-online.org), after Fotiadis", "Hofmann 2007, pp. 208-210", "Clark, Twice a Stranger (2006), p. 114", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP, 2019), pp. 416-417 and n. 255 (Shaw, From Empire to Republic, vol. 3 pt 1, pp. 1016, 1018: '485')", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919-1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide, 1913-1923: New Perspectives (Asia Minor and Pontos Hellenic Research Center, 2019), pp. 17-18", "Kyriakidis (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias Press, 2026), p. 79", "Tsirkinidis, At Last We Uprooted Them (1999), p. 192", "Hofmann, Kurzer Abriss … (aga-online.org), after Fotiadis (72 hanged, '8 and 21 Sept')", "Hofmann, Verfolgung, Vertreibung und Vernichtung der Christen im Osmanischen Reich 1912-1922 (2007), pp. 208-210", "Çetinoğlu, 'Pontos'taki Düzmece İstiklal Mahkemeleri ve Yargılamaları' (posted 7 Jan 2020; reprinted in Çilingir 2016, pp. 229ff.) [Turkish writer], pp. 4-10", "Hofmann 2011, p. 74 ('arbitrary death sentences') [international scholar, Germany]", "Agtzidis 2005, p. 212 ('trial-parody') [Greek]", "Çetinoğlu, 'Pontos'taki Düzmece İstiklal Mahkemeleri ve Yargılamaları', pp. 1–10 ('sham' courts; archives closed) [Turkish]"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "The court was the Samsun Independence Tribunal sitting at Amasya; a separate Amasya tribunal, created on 27 July 1922, never sat and was abolished on 27 November 1922 (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 18). There is no single confirmed figure for the hangings. By the tribunal's records 177 had been executed and 74 sentenced in absentia by 10 October 1921, of 622 death sentences in all (Aybars, cited by Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, pp. 17–18); Tsirkinidis gives 177 hanged and 44 in absentia; the 622 include those in absentia and cover the whole region and period, not only Amasya. Hosford gave about 250 hanged at Amasya in September, the Armenian-Greek Section 168 (Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 416–417); the Patriarch's telegram counted 168 Greeks and 3 Armenians named in the Samsun papers of 18, 19 and 25 September (Hofmann, p. 75); Tepeyran, and Shaw as cited by Morris & Ze'evi (n. 255), give 485 death sentences. The Ehali lists of the executed, reprinted in the Black Book, name about 60 (list of 12/25 September) and about 92 executed at Amasya 'on 8 September 1921', three of them Armenians; Vergeti counts the names as 155, Çilingir as 132. Kyriakidis (p. 79, after Fotiadis) has 72 hanged in the square of Amasya on 8/21 September 1921, one day in the Old and New Styles, which is probably what Hofmann gives as '8 and 21 September'. The teachers of Anatolia College executed are given as two teachers and one student (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 412) or four teachers and two students (Kyriakidis 2023, p. 57); the Patriarchate names Prof. Georges Papamarkou, D. Theocharidis and the missionary Paul Bacon. Platon is a protosynkellos or archimandrite in some accounts and 'Metropolitan of Samsun' or 'Metropolitan Vicar of Samsun' in others; Morris & Ze'evi repeat the confusion (p. 417). Scholars call the sentences 'arbitrary' (Hofmann 2011, p. 74), the trials a 'parody' (Agtzidis 2005, p. 212) and the courts 'sham' ones whose archives are still closed (Çetinoğlu). Turkish historiography's view of the trials, as the lawful suppression of a conspiracy, is under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H6-T04, H7-T07).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: tribunal dockets (via Aybars and Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019); Ehali lists (names and numbers executed)", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019; Aybars 1975; Tepeyran 1982 (485)", "Greek sources: Patriarchate letter and telegram; A.G.S. minutes (Patriarchate information); Black Book; Tsirkinidis 1999; Fotiadis", "Neutral sources: Hosford (US); Morris & Ze'evi 2019; Hofmann 2011; Kyriakides & Nikoloudis 2026; Clark 2006; British protests; US Senate (King)", "Greek sources: Chrysanthos 1933", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Ehali list; Aybars (dockets); Tepeyran 1982 (admissions: names and numbers)", "Turkish writers who argue for recognition: Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019; Çilingir 2016; Çetinoğlu (transmitters; flagged)", "Greek sources: Tsirkinidis 1999; Vergeti 1993; Fotiadis; Kyriakidis 2026", "Neutral sources: Bristol; Rumbold; Hosford; A.G.S.; Morris & Ze'evi; Hofmann; Clark"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Amasya", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "tribunal and hangings"}, {"name": "Merzifon", "lat": 40.873, "lon": 35.463, "role": "Anatolia College teachers and students"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "home of many of the accused"}, {"name": "Amasya (Amaseia)", "lat": 40.654, "lon": 35.833, "role": "tribunal and executions"}]}, {"id": "H6-059", "date": "1921-08-23", "date_end": "1921-09-13", "date_text": "23 Aug – 13 Sept 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "The battle of the Sakarya (context)", "summary": "The Greek army's advance on Ankara is stopped at the Sakarya. Topal Osman's 47th Regiment is operating in the Erbaa, Çakallı and Bafra districts at this time, and he is promoted. The Kemalist victory makes the end of Greek hopes in Pontus certain.", "people": ["Mustafa Kemal", "Topal Osman"], "sources_modern": ["Doğan 2014", "Alpaslan 2007"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "Turkish biographies (Alpaslan 2007; Doğan 2014) describe the regiment's operations as fighting Greek bands; that account is under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H6-T08).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: general military history of the Sakarya", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Doğan 2014; Alpaslan 2007 (places; promotion)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Sakarya front (Polatlı)", "lat": 39.584, "lon": 32.141, "role": "battle"}, {"name": "Erbaa", "lat": 40.669, "lon": 36.568, "role": "47th Regiment against bands"}]}, {"id": "H6-079", "date": "1921-09-01", "date_end": "1921-10-09", "date_text": "Early September and early October 1921", "precision": "month", "title": "A Greek band attacks a Turkish village near Samsun (US naval report)", "summary": "At about the time the Bafra women and children were being deported, the war diary of the US destroyer McFarland recorded that a Greek band of nearly a hundred had attacked a Turkish village about eight miles from Samsun, killed some villagers and burned their houses. A similar raid by Greek bands from the mountains seems to have been made in early October (USS Williamson). It is the first non-Turkish report of a Greek band attack on Muslims in Pontus that we have found.", "sources_primary": ["USS McFarland war diaries, 31 Aug – 8 Sept 1921, NA RG 45, box 832", "USS Williamson message to Bristol, 9 Oct 1921 (Bristol Papers, box 73)", "USS McFarland war diaries, 31 Aug – 8 Sept 1921, NA RG 45, box 832; USS Williamson message to Bristol, 9 Oct 1921 (Bristol Papers, box 73)"], "sources_modern": ["Shenk, 'Ethnic Cleansing, American Women, and the Admiral', in Shirinian (ed.), Genocide in the Ottoman Empire: Armenians, Assyrians, and Greeks, 1913–1923 (2017), p. 201", "B1 study", "Shenk, 'Ethnic Cleansing, American Women, and the Admiral', in Shirinian (ed.), Genocide in the Ottoman Empire: Armenians, Assyrians, and Greeks, 1913-1923 (Berghahn 2017), p. 201"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The village and the number killed are not given. The report is neutral and contemporary, but second-hand (the officers seldom left Samsun). It shows that band attacks on Muslims happened in 1921; it does not confirm the Turkish totals (H6-013).", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: US naval war diaries (via Shenk 2017)", "Neutral sources: US naval war diaries (via Shenk)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "US station ship; the attacked village lay about eight miles away (not named)"}, {"name": "Turkish village about 8 miles from Samsun (not named)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "band attack, early Sept 1921; plotted at Samsun"}]}, {"id": "H6-081", "date": "1922-02-18", "date_end": "1922-05-15", "date_text": "14/18 Feb – 15 May 1922", "precision": "day", "title": "Fethi's 1922 sweep of the Pontic mountains", "summary": "After the Central Army is dissolved, Interior Minister Fethi publishes a proclamation (14 February 1922) and launches a new operation on 18 February under the 10th Division. Its commander reported about 15,000 Greeks killed or captured in some two months; Fethi told a closed session that 3,777 'armed Greeks' had been killed between 20 March and 15 May (H6-078). In February the hinterland of Bafra was swept; a telegram from 21 Greek and Circassian refugees from Bafra says rewards were offered for heads and 'five sacks full of heads' were brought in, and that Bafra Circassians hid about 140 Greek neighbours. Metropolitan Kyrillos of Rhodopolis was arrested at Trebizond, about April 1922, accused of supplying the 'rebellious Greeks' with arms and food; a friendly judge saved him by transferring the case to the civil court, and he was freed in 1923.", "people": ["Ali Fethi (Okyar)", "Cemil Cahit (10th Division)", "Kyrillos of Rhodopolis"], "sources_primary": ["TBMM Gizli Zabıt Ceridesi, 10 June 1922 (via Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019, p. 15)", "Hakimiyet-i Milliye, 14 Feb 1922 (Fethi's proclamation)", "Telegram from 21 Greek and Circassian refugees from Bafra, enclosure in Lindley's despatch no. 286, 3 June 1922, FO 371/7879 (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 201)", "Cesarano (Apostolic Delegation) to Gasparri, 11 June 1923, ASV Arch. Deleg. Turchia 117, fasc. 629 (Kyrillos' account; via Kyriakides & Nikoloudis 2026, pp. 95–96)"], "sources_modern": ["Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919–1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide 1913–1923: New Perspectives (2019), pp. 15, 25", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 409–410", "Kyriakides (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias 2026; Greek ed. 2015), pp. 95–96"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The Turkish counts are state figures and class the dead as armed; the framing is under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H6-T08). The 'sacks of heads' is the refugees' allegation. Kyrillos' arrest date is reckoned from his statement in June 1923 that he had been arrested fourteen months earlier.", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Ottoman state records: TBMM closed session, 10 June 1922; 10th Division report (admissions of numbers killed)", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019 (transmitters)", "Greek sources: Bafra refugees' telegram (with Circassian co-signatories)", "Neutral sources: British FO; Apostolic Delegation (Kyrillos' account); Morris & Ze'evi 2019"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.567, "lon": 35.906, "role": "hinterland swept, February 1922"}, {"name": "Nebyan Dağı (approximate)", "lat": 41.38, "lon": 36.0, "role": "refuge of Greek bands and civilians"}, {"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "arrest of Metropolitan Kyrillos of Rhodopolis"}]}, {"id": "H6-060", "date": "1922-04-10", "date_end": "1922-06", "date_text": "April – June 1922", "precision": "day", "title": "The last deportations", "summary": "About 1,324 Greeks, mostly women and children, are deported from Samsun on 10–11 April and 1,462 women and children, many just down from the mountains, on 15 April; the wife of a Turkish officer later said they were marched into the hills near Kavak and murdered. From late April the whole Greek male population of the Trebizond region from the age of 15 is deported in groups of fifty every few days, and boys of 11 to 14 are rounded up for a camp near Cevizlik, though Trebizond's mayor and vali opposed the persecution. In May the head of the Capuchin mission wrote that in Trebizond only women and children remained of the Greeks, that in Samsun there were no men but a few old ones, that no Greek villages were left inland, and that Topal Osman's band was killing in the Giresun countryside, supported by the government and honoured by the Muslim population. By June 1922 American missionaries reported the Pontic villages empty; in May the British High Commissioner Rumbold and the Commons (15 May) took up the reports.", "people": ["William D. Leahy", "Horace Rumbold", "Topal Osman"], "sources_primary": ["CO USS Fox (Trammell), diary, 10 April 1922; E. G. Haas, USS Sturtevant, diary, 18 April and 1 May 1922; CO USS Childs, Samsun diary, 18 May 1922 (via Morris & Ze'evi, nn. 264–265, 271)", "Rumbold to FO, 25 April and 10 May 1922 (via Hofmann 2011, p. 80; Morris & Ze'evi, nn. 267, 270)", "Herbert Gibbons, Christian Science Monitor, Trebizond, 24 May 1922 (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 274; Kévorkian, n. 105)", "F. Lorenzo, Trebizond, 8 and 14 May 1922 (AGC H 97; ASV Arch. Deleg. Turchia 115, fasc. 612; via Kyriakides & Nikoloudis 2026, pp. 82–83)", "Mrs Crawford (Trebizond) to Peet, 25 April 1922, FO 371/7878 (via Kyriakidis 2023, n. 58)", "Theda Phelps to the State Department, 2 June 1922", "Hansard, 15 May 1922"], "sources_modern": ["Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), pp. 417–421", "Hofmann, 'Γενοκτονία εν ροή – Cumulative Genocide', in Hofmann, Bjørnlund & Meichanetsidis (eds), The Genocide of the Ottoman Greeks (Caratzas 2011), pp. 77–80", "Kyriakides (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias 2026; Greek ed. 2015), pp. 82–83", "Kyriakidis, 'Testimonies of American Charitable and Missionary Organizations on the Genocide of the Pontic Greeks', in Akçam, Kyriakidis et al. (eds), The Genocide of the Christian Populations in the Ottoman Empire and its Aftermath (Routledge 2023), pp. 49, 53", "Shenk, America's Black Sea Fleet (Naval Institute Press 2012)"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The killing of the 15 April deportees near Kavak rests on one Turkish officer's wife's statement (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 418). US destroyer logbooks count 5,400 women and children deported between November 1921 and 6 June 1922 (Kyriakidis 2023, p. 53).", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: US Navy (Fox, Sturtevant, Childs); Rumbold; Gibbons (US journalist); Capuchin letters (F. Lorenzo); Mrs Crawford and Phelps (US missionaries); Hansard; Morris & Ze'evi 2019; Hofmann 2011; Kyriakides & Nikoloudis 2026; Kyriakidis 2023"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "deportations, 10–15 April 1922"}, {"name": "Ordu", "lat": 40.984, "lon": 37.879, "role": "killings of women and children"}, {"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "men deported, May 1922"}, {"name": "Cevizlik (Maçka)", "lat": 40.813, "lon": 39.613, "role": "camp for deported boys and men, 1922"}]}, {"id": "H6-061", "date": "1922-06-07", "date_text": "7 June 1922", "precision": "day", "title": "The Greek navy shells Samsun", "summary": "A Greek squadron led by the battleship Kilkis fires about 400 rounds at Samsun over three hours, aiming at depots of arms arriving for the Kemalists. Damage and deaths are disputed; a US destroyer commander reported about 90 casualties. The raid had no military effect and could only harm the Greeks still in the region.", "sources_primary": ["New York Times, 11 and 12 June 1922", "New York Times, 11 and 12 June 1922 (via H6-061)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "A US destroyer commander reported about 90 casualties; the lower Turkish figures are under 'What the Turkish sources say' (H6-T09), as is Ankara's use of the bombardment as proof of Greek designs on the Pontus (H7-T04).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: New York Times, 11 and 12 June 1922; US destroyer report", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Doğanay 2006; Ünal 2022 (as cited on Wikipedia) for the course of the raid", "Neutral sources: New York Times, 11-12 June 1922; US destroyer report", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Doğanay 2006; Ünal 2022 (course of the raid, as cited on Wikipedia)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "shelled"}, {"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "bombarded"}]}, {"id": "H6-062", "date": "1922-08-26", "date_end": "1922-10-11", "date_text": "26 Aug – 11 Oct 1922", "precision": "day", "title": "Greek defeat in Anatolia (context)", "summary": "The Turkish Great Offensive (26 August) routs the Greek army; the Turkish army enters Smyrna on 9 September, and the city burns from 13 September. The armistice of Mudanya (11 October) ends the war. About a million Anatolian and Thracian Greeks flee to Greece in 1922, before any treaty.", "people": ["Mustafa Kemal"], "sources_modern": ["Smith 1973", "Clark 2006"], "confidence": "high", "basis": "neutral", "basis_detail": ["Neutral sources: Smith 1973; Clark 2006"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Smyrna", "lat": 38.419, "lon": 27.129, "role": "taken 9 Sept; fire from 13 Sept 1922"}, {"name": "Mudanya", "lat": 40.376, "lon": 28.883, "role": "armistice, 11 Oct 1922"}]}, {"id": "H6-063", "date": "1922-12", "date_text": "December 1922", "precision": "month", "title": "Death of Kotsa Nastas", "summary": "Anastasios Papadopoulos, the guerrilla leader of the Erbaa–Tokat mountains, is killed in December 1922, reportedly betrayed for the bounty; his head is displayed in Tokat. Pontic tradition credits his band of about 600 with sheltering some 12,000 people, mostly women and children, in 1921; the survivors were later settled at Neokaisareia in Pieria.", "people": ["Anastasios Papadopoulos (Kotsa Nastas)"], "sources_modern": ["Encyclopedia of Pontic Hellenism (Malliaris), via pontosworld", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 415 (a British observer put the armed Greeks in the mountains at 20,000–30,000, May 1922)"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "The numbers come from Pontic commemorative literature. A British estimate of about 12,000 people hiding in the mountains of Amasya and Bayburt (cited by Biber) is of the same order; another British observer, in May 1922, put the armed Greeks in the mountains at 20,000–30,000 (Rendel's note of a conversation with Col. Rawlinson, cited by Morris & Ze'evi, p. 415).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Greek sources: Encyclopedia of Pontic Hellenism (via pontosworld)", "Neutral sources: a British estimate of about 12,000 people in hiding (via Biber)"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Tokat", "lat": 40.314, "lon": 36.554, "role": "head displayed"}, {"name": "Topçam / Yaylacık hills near Erbaa", "lat": 40.78, "lon": 36.55, "role": "band area (approximate)"}]}, {"id": "H6-067", "date": "1923-04-01", "date_end": "1923-04-02", "date_text": "1–2 April 1923", "precision": "day", "title": "Death of Topal Osman", "summary": "After the disappearance of the Trabzon deputy Ali Şükrü (27 March), whose body is found on 1 April, Topal Osman is cornered at his house in Ankara, captured wounded and shot. His body is hung at the Assembly gate. He was later buried on Giresun castle, where he is honoured as a hero of the National Struggle.", "people": ["Topal Osman", "Ali Şükrü Bey", "Topal Osman Ağa"], "sources_primary": ["Tevhid-i Efkâr, 4 April 1923"], "sources_modern": ["Doğan 2014", "Doğan, Mustafa Kemal'in Muhafızı Topal Osman (2014), pp. 312-313", "Mango, Atatürk (1999)"], "confidence": "high", "disputed": "His responsibility for Ali Şükrü's murder is disputed (Doğan).", "basis": "mixed", "basis_detail": ["Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Tevhid-i Efkâr, 4 April 1923; Doğan 2014", "Neutral sources", "Turkish works, used for neutral facts: Doğan 2014", "Neutral sources: Mango 1999"], "main": true, "places": [{"name": "Ankara", "lat": 39.933, "lon": 32.86, "role": "killed"}, {"name": "Giresun castle", "lat": 40.919, "lon": 38.392, "role": "burial (1925)"}, {"name": "Ankara (Angora)", "lat": 39.933, "lon": 32.86, "role": "killed"}, {"name": "Giresun (Kerasous)", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.39, "role": "reburied 1925"}]}], "turkish": [{"id": "H6-013", "date": "1916", "date_end": "1922", "date_text": "1916-1922", "precision": "year", "title": "Violence by Pontic bands against Muslims: the Turkish claims", "summary": "Ottoman and Turkish sources record attacks by Greek bands on Muslims: road blocks, attacks on supply convoys, raids on Muslim villages and the looting of grain stores around Çarşamba and Bafra in 1916-18, with logistic help from the Russian command at Trebizond (Ottoman archives, via Duman 2012 and Aydın & Duman 2012). For 1921–22 the claims are quantified: Interior Minister Ali Fethi told the Assembly on 29 December 1921 that armed Greek bands had killed 1,303 Muslims, wounded 147 and damaged 3,303 dwellings, and that the Central Army had largely destroyed 50–60 bands of 180–200 men around Nebyan; in closed session on 10 June 1922 he added 69 killed in October–December 1921 and 82 in January–May 1922. Deputies claimed on 29 October 1921 that 60 Muslim villages had been burned in the Trebizond area and 30 in Samsun. The Ankara government's Pontus Meselesi (1922) counted 1,641 Muslims killed, 323 wounded, 3,723 dwellings burned and 1,800 robberies, and said that over 10,000 rifles had been distributed to the Greeks by Tsarist Russia and later Britain, of which only about 2,000 were surrendered. Two Lazistan deputies, attacking Nureddin in October 1921, said that Greeks fleeing his deportations had joined the bands and begun attacking Muslim villages. Turkish-language writers also describe the Bafra band leader Anton Pasha storming the Bafra gendarmerie post and holding soldiers hostage to free his wife (Çilingir 2014).", "places": [{"name": "Çarşamba", "lat": 41.199, "lon": 36.722, "role": "band raids 1916–18"}, {"name": "Bafra / Nebyan Dağı", "lat": 41.38, "lon": 36.0, "role": "band area; clashes with the Central Army, 1921 (approximate)"}, {"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "claimed village burnings in the district"}], "people": ["Ali Fethi (Okyar)", "Anton Pasha (Antonis Hacılefteryu)"], "sources_primary": ["TBMM secret sessions, 29 Oct and 29 Dec 1921", "Pontus Meselesi (Ankara government, 1922)", "BOA and ATASE documents (not consulted)", "TBMM Zabıt Ceridesi, 29 Dec 1921, pp. 240–241; TBMM Gizli Zabıt Ceridesi, 5 Oct 1921 and 10 June 1922 (via Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019, pp. 13–15)", "Pontus Meselesi (TBMM Government Press Directorate, 1922; modern edition 1995), as summarised by Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019, pp. 20–21"], "sources_modern": ["Duman, History Studies 4/2 (2012), 376-386", "Aydın & Duman, History Studies 4/2 (2012), 39-51", "Çakıcı 2018", "Biber (TÜBA) ch. 13", "Çilingir 2014 (HyeTert) (as cited on Wikipedia)", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919–1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide 1913–1923: New Perspectives (2019), pp. 13–15, 20–21", "Agtzidis 1992, p. 167; Agtzidis 2005, p. 184 (corroborating the general statements only) [Greek]"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "The Pontus Meselesi of 1922 is the Ankara government's own case, published by its press directorate. The village and death figures were stated in the Assembly in the weeks when Nureddin's conduct was under attack there; we found no document behind them. Çilingir writes sympathetically about Anton Pasha, so his account is not a nationalist one, but it is still a Turkish source alone.", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: TBMM statements (Ali Fethi; deputies); Pontus Meselesi 1922", "Turkish sources only: Ottoman archives via Duman 2012 and Aydın & Duman 2012; Çakıcı 2018; Biber (TÜBA); Çilingir 2014"], "main": false, "counter_evidence": "Partly corroborated, and those parts are given in the chapter's events: a US destroyer's war diary records a Greek band of nearly a hundred attacking a Turkish village about eight miles from Samsun in early September 1921, killing villagers and burning houses, and a similar raid in October (Shenk 2017, p. 201); and Morris & Ze'evi accept that wartime bands of draft evaders 'occasionally attacked Turkish villagers and gendarmes' (p. 383). A French officer's letter of 11/24 December 1917 shows that the Allies, with Russian officers, wanted a Greek volunteer regiment raised at Trebizond (Le Martyre du Pont-Euxin, 1922, pp. 12–13; H6-030), but Chrysanthos was cautious and no regiment is known to have been formed; Greek memoirs say the departing French commissioner let the Greek Red Cross pass its weapons to the Santa guerrillas in August 1920 (Georganopoulos 2023, p. 86 n. 118). A British observer put the armed Greeks in the mountains at 20,000–30,000 in May 1922 (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 415). Against the totals: no non-Turkish source confirms the casualty figures, which were stated in the Assembly while Nureddin's conduct was under attack; Morris & Ze'evi write that the agitation of 1919 produced 'almost no acts of overt rebellion' and very little anti-Turk terrorism (p. 383) and that Western observers 'uniformly asserted that there was no Pontine \"rebellion\"' (p. 406); and the Lazistan deputies themselves blamed the bands' growth on Nureddin's deportations (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 13). The figures here (1,303 and 1,641 Muslims killed; 3,303 and 3,723 dwellings; 60 and 30 villages; over 10,000 rifles from Russia and Britain) are found only in Turkish sources.", "overlaps_with": ["H7-004", "H7-T10", "H7-T08", "B1-T01"], "moved_to_main": ["Russian arms for the bands, as a general statement (Agtzidis 1992, p. 167) → H7-063 (chapter 10)", "post-armistice Muslim–Christian clashes with 'hundreds of victims' (Agtzidis 2005, p. 184) → H7-063 (chapter 10)"]}, {"id": "H6-T01", "date": "1916-04", "date_end": "1918-02", "date_text": "April 1916 - February 1918", "precision": "month", "title": "Greek and Armenian bands burn Turkish houses in the Russian-occupied zone", "summary": "Turkish historians (Çakıcı 2018, after Turkish refugee and archival sources) stress that during the Russian occupation Greek and Armenian bands burned Turkish houses in the occupied countryside of Trebizond, Rize and Gümüşhane.", "places": [{"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "seat of the occupation"}, {"name": "Rize", "lat": 41.025, "lon": 40.517, "role": "occupied zone"}, {"name": "Gümüşhane", "lat": 40.46, "lon": 39.481, "role": "occupied zone"}], "sources_primary": ["Turkish refugee and archival sources as used by Çakıcı 2018 (not consulted)"], "sources_modern": ["Çakıcı, KAREN 4/6 (2018), 331-345"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "The burnings are not counted.", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Çakıcı 2018"], "main": false, "counter_evidence": "No non-Turkish source for this was found. Greek accounts stress instead that Chrysanthos helped Muslims who stayed or returned to recover their property (H6-019). The two are not exclusive, and neither is quantified."}, {"id": "H6-T02", "date": "1916-04", "date_end": "1918-02", "date_text": "Spring 1916 - February 1918", "precision": "month", "title": "Muslim deaths in the flight of 1916: the Turkish figures", "summary": "Turkish works put the losses of the Muslim flight from the Russian-occupied zone very high: about 220,000 dead in the displacement (Kaya 2002) and a population decline of 204,130 (McCarthy 1998, as cited by Çakıcı). Many refugees are said to have died of hunger and epidemics on the way and in the reception areas (Öksüz & Usta 2008), and Trebizond city to have fallen from about 45,000 people to about half that by the end of the occupation.", "places": [{"name": "Trebizond", "lat": 41.005, "lon": 39.726, "role": "origin"}, {"name": "Kerasous (Giresun)", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.39, "role": "refugee destination"}, {"name": "Ordu (Kotyora)", "lat": 40.984, "lon": 37.879, "role": "refugee destination"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "refugee destination"}, {"name": "Merzifon", "lat": 40.873, "lon": 35.463, "role": "refugee destination"}, {"name": "Konya", "lat": 37.872, "lon": 32.485, "role": "refugee destination"}], "sources_primary": ["Ottoman refugee (muhacir) records used by Öksüz & Usta 2008 (not consulted)"], "sources_modern": ["Çakıcı 2018, citing Erdeha 1975, Kaya 2002, McCarthy 1998 and Öksüz & Usta 2008"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "The figures include deaths from all causes in a wider region than the occupied Pontus.", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Kaya 2002; Öksüz & Usta 2008; Erdeha 1975; Çakıcı 2018", "Other sources: McCarthy 1998, an American demographer (reached only through Çakıcı)"], "main": false, "counter_evidence": "The flight itself is confirmed by Chrysanthos and is told in the chapter (H6-017, H6-018). We have found no independent count of the deaths. A US consul's post-war inquiry noted that Samsun's Turks had 'endured heavy losses during the war, in combat and from disease' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 391), which supports heavy Muslim losses in the region in general but not these figures. McCarthy is an American scholar, not a Turkish source, but we know his figure only as cited by Çakıcı, and it is a fall in population from all causes, not a count of deaths."}, {"id": "H6-T03", "date": "1916-06", "date_end": "1917-03", "date_text": "June 1916 - March 1917", "precision": "month", "title": "The Ottoman security case for the deportations of 1916-17", "summary": "Ottoman orders and later Turkish historiography justify the removal of Greeks from the coast in 1916-17 as a military necessity: fear of Russian landings on an exposed coast, of Greek help to the Russian army, and of deserters' bands in the mountains. Enver's order of December 1916 speaks of 'purely military reasons'.", "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "notables deported"}, {"name": "Bafra (Pafra)", "lat": 41.567, "lon": 35.906, "role": "notables deported"}, {"name": "Çarşamba", "lat": 41.199, "lon": 36.722, "role": "notables deported"}, {"name": "Ordu", "lat": 40.984, "lon": 37.879, "role": "notables deported"}, {"name": "Tirebolu", "lat": 41.006, "lon": 38.815, "role": "notables deported"}, {"name": "Amasya", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "notables deported"}, {"name": "Constantinople", "lat": 41.008, "lon": 28.978, "role": "order issued"}, {"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "deserters held, report of 19 May 1916"}], "people": ["Enver Pasha", "Mehmed Talat Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["Ottoman cipher telegrams (BOA DH.ŞFR), as cited by Akçam 2012", "Talat's cable of January 1917 and Enver's order of December 1916 (their justifications)"], "sources_modern": ["Biber (TÜBA) ch. 13", "Aydın & Duman 2012; Biber (TÜBA)", "Morris & Ze'evi 2019, pp. 385, 490", "Pallavicini, January 1918, quoted in Agtzidis 2005, p. 155"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The orders themselves are told in the chapter (H6-026, H7-011); this entry gives their justification.", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: justification in Ottoman orders; Turkish historiography (Biber)", "Turkish sources only: justifications in Ottoman orders; Turkish historiography"], "main": false, "counter_evidence": "Partly corroborated: the Russians did land on the Lazistan coast in March 1916 (H6-015); many Greek men had deserted the labour battalions and some formed bands (H6-009); and Morris & Ze'evi, writing of the removals of 1915 from the Straits, grant that 'military concerns, without doubt, played a part', while adding that 'hovering in the background were nationalism and religion' (p. 388). Against the rest: the German consular telegram from Samsun of July 1916 called desertion and espionage 'a pretext to annihilate the entire people' and blamed the vali's fanaticism (Hofmann 2011, p. 57); Talat told the German ambassador in October 1916 that a deportation of all the Greeks of the coast had been planned earlier that year and stopped by Enver so as not to provoke Greece (p. 59); German diplomats learned from Austrian sources in February 1917 that the 'ban' on the Greeks of Samsun, carried out under the pretext of pursuing Greek bands, was 'in reality' a 'large scale' persecution (p. 60); Rafet Bey told the Austrian consul in November 1916 that they must do with the Greeks 'as we did with the Armenians' (H7-008); Pallavicini reported burned villages, and women, children and the old deported in winter (January 1917), and wrote in January 1918 that the deportations were not dictated by military reasons (quoted by Agtzidis 2005, p. 155); Talat had told an Austrian agent in January 1916 of 'the increased necessity for Turkey to get rid of Greeks' (H7-054); Metropolitan Germanos told US officers that 'the greatest cause' of the Bafra deportations was 'religion' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 391); and the Russian-held zone, where collaboration was most possible, was the one area not deported. Morris & Ze'evi see 'real persecution … orchestrated by Constantinople', though no CUP policy of genocide towards the Ottoman Greeks (p. 385; p. 490: 'centrally orchestrated efforts'), and Minasidis (2024), with most non-Turkish historians, reads security as the pretext for demographic homogenisation.", "merged_from": ["H6-T03"]}, {"id": "C5-T01", "date": "1918", "date_end": "1932", "date_text": "1918–1932", "precision": "decade", "title": "The Turkish-speaking Orthodox of Anatolia as ethnic Turks (Baykurt, İzzet Ulvi, Papa Eftim)", "summary": "Cami Baykurt (articles of 1918–19; Osmanlı Ülkesinde Hıristiyan Türkler, 2nd ed. 1932) and İzzet Ulvi (in Hâkimiyet-i Milliye) held that the Turkish-speaking Orthodox of Anatolia were Turks by descent who had become Christians, and Papa Eftim's Turkish Orthodox movement of 1921–23 took the same line. Applied to the Pontus, the thesis concerns the Turcophone Orthodox of the metropolis of Amaseia (Bafra, the Samsun–Çarşamba plain, Amasya) and the Urum of Erzurum and Kars. We know it only through Vryonis (1971, pp. 453–457) and have not consulted these texts.", "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.567, "lon": 35.906, "role": "Turkish-speaking Orthodox town of the Amaseia metropolis (subject of the claim)"}, {"name": "Amasya (Amaseia)", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "see of the metropolis whose Orthodox were largely Turcophone (subject of the claim)"}], "people": ["Cami Baykurt", "İzzet Ulvi", "Papa Eftim"], "sources_modern": ["Vryonis 1971, The Decline of Medieval Hellenism in Asia Minor, pp. 453–457 (the thesis and its holders, after Baykurt 1932, İzzet Ulvi, T. Ergene 1951, G. Jaeschke and H. J. Psomiades) [Greek diaspora, Greek-American]"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "A thesis found only in Turkish writers.", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Baykurt 1918–19/1932; İzzet Ulvi; Papa Eftim's movement (known only through Vryonis 1971)"], "main": false, "counter_evidence": "Vryonis 1971, pp. 453–457, sets out the thesis and argues for a gradual language shift among Anatolian Greeks; he dates the Turkish-origin thesis to the politics of 1918–23 (p. 455). For the Pontus: Ioannidis 1870 (pp. 180, 185–186) has the Turcophone Orthodox of Bafra and Çarşamba keeping Greek schools 'to recover their ancestral language' and Merzifon's Greeks coming from Cappadocia; the 1911 consular census (Kitromilides & Alexandris 1984, Table VI) counts them in the Patriarchate's dioceses; they were exchanged as Orthodox in 1923."}, {"id": "H6-T04", "date": "1919-05", "date_end": "1923", "date_text": "May 1919 - 1923", "precision": "year", "title": "The 'Pontus question': a separatist rising that made deportation necessary", "summary": "Turkish historiography (Biber in the TÜBA volumes; Duman; the Ankara government's Pontus Meselesi of 1922) treats the Pontic movement of 1919-23 as a Greek-backed separatist rising. Mustafa Kemal reported in late May 1919 that 'forty guerrilla' bands were killing Turks 'in an organized program' to 'establish a Pontus state', and in later reports that the real problem was the armed Greek groups, the Muslim bands being self-defence; on 24 April 1920 he told the Assembly that a Pontus government directed by committees in Athens and Istanbul aimed to rule the coast north of Amasya and Tokat, and on 13 August 1923 he announced that 'the Pontus Government … has been defeated along with its supporters'. Replying to Pope Benedict XV in March 1921, he said the Christians under the Assembly's government were 'altogether calm' and accused Greeks and Armenians of crimes. The bands are said to have been organised from Greece and supported by Pontic societies; the Central Army was formed in December 1920 against this movement as well as against Koçgiri; the deportations of 1921 were a necessary wartime measure; and the Amasya tribunal tried a real conspiracy.", "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "landing, 19 May 1919"}, {"name": "Amasya", "lat": 40.653, "lon": 35.833, "role": "tribunal and hangings"}, {"name": "Samsun (Amisos)", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "deserters held, report of 19 May 1916"}, {"name": "Giresun (Kerasous)", "lat": 40.913, "lon": 38.39, "role": "search for c. 300 deserters, winter 1916-17"}], "people": ["Mustafa Kemal (Atatürk)", "Nureddin Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["Mustafa Kemal's reports of May 1919 (as cited by Morris & Ze'evi, p. 384, after Shaw) and from Samsun (Belgelerle Türk Tarihi Dergisi 14, 1968; via Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019, pp. 23–24)", "TBMM Zabıt Ceridesi, 24 April 1920 and 11/13 Aug 1923 (via Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019, pp. 7, 11)", "Mustafa Kemal to Benedict XV, March 1921 (Carolla, doc. 99; via Kyriakides & Nikoloudis 2026, p. 76)", "Pontus Meselesi (Ankara government, 1922)", "Pontus Meselesi ve Yunanistan'ın Politikası (Turkish state archives, 1999)"], "sources_modern": ["Biber (TÜBA) ch. 13", "Duman 2012", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919–1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide 1913–1923: New Perspectives (2019), pp. 7, 11, 23–24", "Kyriakides (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias 2026; Greek ed. 2015), p. 76", "Mesut Çapa (works on the Pontus question)", "Aydın & Duman, History Studies 4.2 (2012)"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "The core of the Turkish national narrative on the Pontus.", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Kemal's reports, May 1919; Pontus Meselesi 1922; Biber (TÜBA); Duman 2012", "Turkish sources only: Turkish historiography (Çapa; Turkish state archives 1999; Aydın & Duman 2012)"], "main": false, "counter_evidence": "Partly corroborated: Pontic leaders abroad did seek a Pontic state (Chrysanthos' memorandum at Paris, May 1919; the Pontic-Armenian confederation plan, January 1920; Venizelos' proposal of a landing at Trebizond, October 1920: H6-040, H6-044, H6-047); the French commissioner at Trebizond reported in March 1919 that Chrysanthos, Germanos, Lavrentios and Konstantinidis led a movement for autonomy and then union with Greece (Georganopoulos 2023, p. 77); the British official Rendel wrote that the Greek ecclesiastical authorities 'had fostered a Greek national movement' in Pontus (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 383); and armed Greek bands existed (20,000–30,000 men in May 1922 according to a British observer; Morris & Ze'evi, p. 415). Against the rest: Morris & Ze'evi write that 'all this separatist hubbub had resulted, after the war, in \"almost no acts of overt rebellion\" and very little anti-Turk terrorism', that 'most Ottoman Greeks, in the Pontus as elsewhere in Anatolia, remained unmoved by ethnic-nationalist appeals' (p. 383), and that 'Western observers uniformly asserted that there was no Pontine \"rebellion,\" either underway or in preparation' (p. 406); even Justin McCarthy, whom they call sympathetic to the Turkish narrative, judged that 'the \"Pontus Republic\" revolutionaries were never a potent political or military force and would have been better dealt with by police than by deportation' (p. 382); an American observer in Trebizond reported in 1919 that many influential Greeks, and Chrysanthos himself, opposed separatism (p. 383); Venizelos told the Greek parliament in May 1920 that an independent Pontus could not be realised; Wilson declined to rule on the Pontic request; and the Amasya hangings, which included teachers, journalists and men already dead, drew Greek and British protests and a speech in the US Senate (H6-058).", "merged_from": ["H6-T04"], "overlaps_with": ["B1-T05"]}, {"id": "H6-T05", "date": "1921-02-12", "date_text": "12 February 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "The Merzifon search of February 1921 as proof of a Pontic conspiracy", "summary": "Turkish accounts (the Ankara government's Pontus Meselesi, 1922; Biber, TÜBA; bianet) present the killing of Zeki Bey, a Muslim teacher at Anatolia College, on 12 February 1921 and the papers seized in the searches that followed (a map labelled 'Pontus', a Greek flag, photographs of the football team and theatre club of the Greek students, papers of their 'Pontos' society, documents on Pontic autonomy) as evidence that the college and the Samsun metropolis were centres of a Pontic separatist organisation, which justified the arrests and the trials at Amasya.", "places": [{"name": "Merzifon (Marsovan), Anatolia College", "lat": 40.873, "lon": 35.463, "role": "searches and arrests"}], "people": ["Zeki Bey"], "sources_primary": ["Pontus Meselesi (1922), ch. 1 and 3, as summarised by Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019, pp. 20–21"], "sources_modern": ["Biber (TÜBA) ch. 13", "Dağlıoğlu & Korucu, bianet 2018", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919–1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide 1913–1923: New Perspectives (2019), pp. 19–21", "Kyriakidis, 'Testimonies of American Charitable and Missionary Organizations on the Genocide of the Pontic Greeks', in Akçam, Kyriakidis et al. (eds), The Genocide of the Christian Populations in the Ottoman Empire and its Aftermath (Routledge 2023), p. 57 (the items as described by the authorities)"], "confidence": "low", "disputed": "The killing itself is confirmed by American sources and is told in the chapter (H6-050); this entry gives the reading of it as proof of a conspiracy.", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Biber (TÜBA); bianet 2018"], "main": false, "counter_evidence": "The killing is corroborated by American sources: a Muslim teacher of the college was murdered 'by an unknown assailant', and the searches of the college and hospital found no arms (Bristol's draft letter of 28 July 1921, via Morris & Ze'evi, p. 412). Bristol, no friend of the Greeks, thought the Turks 'over-zealous and unreasonable' and the college president White called them 'sedition-mad' (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 412); White had asked the governor whether the 'Pontus' literary society should be suppressed and was told it could go on (Kyriakidis 2023, p. 57). The seized papers are held in over 160 dossiers in the closed TBMM archive (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 19). No source we have read says who killed the teacher."}, {"id": "H6-T08", "date": "1921-06", "date_end": "1923-02", "date_text": "June 1921 - February 1923", "precision": "month", "title": "Operations against 'bands': Ankara's account of the killings of 1921-23", "summary": "Turkish accounts present the killings of Greek men in 1921–23 as the result of fighting with Greek bands. Yusuf Kemal told the Allied high commissioners on 15 September 1921 that the roughly 1,300 men killed in the Kavak gorge on 15–16 August had died in battle after an attack by 'Greek bands' on Turks (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 408). Topal Osman's biographers describe his 47th Regiment as ambushed by Pontic bands at Havza and Merzifon and fighting Greek bands at Erbaa, Çakallı and Bafra (Alpaslan 2007, pp. 525-536; Doğan 2014). The Turkish records that count 1,275 Greeks killed in December 1921 – March 1922, 3,777 in March–May 1922, and 11,198 in all by February 1923 describe them as 'bandits' or 'armed Greeks' killed in operations (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019, pp. 15, 25).", "places": [{"name": "Bafra", "lat": 41.567, "lon": 35.906, "role": "siege, 5 June 1921; deportations 31 Aug and 19 Sept"}, {"name": "Kavak", "lat": 41.079, "lon": 36.044, "role": "killing of men, 15–16 Aug 1921"}, {"name": "Erbaa", "lat": 40.669, "lon": 36.568, "role": "47th Regiment against bands"}], "people": ["Topal Osman"], "sources_primary": ["Youssouf Kemal to the high commissioners, 15 Sept 1921, USNA RG 59, 860J.4016 (via Morris & Ze'evi, n. 189)", "TBMM closed session, 10 June 1922; Turkish military records (via Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019)"], "sources_modern": ["Alpaslan, Topal Osman Ağa (2007), pp. 525-536", "Doğan, Mustafa Kemal'in Muhafızı Topal Osman (2014)", "Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, 'Mapping Out the Turkish Documents on the Unweaving of Greeks from the Black Sea (The Pontic Genocide, 1919–1923)', in Shirinian (ed.), The Greek Genocide 1913–1923: New Perspectives (2019), pp. 15, 25", "Morris & Ze'evi, The Thirty-Year Genocide (Harvard UP 2019), p. 408"], "confidence": "low", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Ankara statements; Alpaslan 2007; Doğan 2014; Turkish records via Çilingir (framing)"], "main": false, "counter_evidence": "The numbers are given in the chapter as the state's own counts, without the framing (H6-078). Greek bands did fight in these hills (H6-009, H6-079), so some deaths in combat are likely, but no source separates combatants from civilians.", "overlaps_with": ["H7-T05", "B1-T06"]}, {"id": "H6-T06", "date": "1921-06-09", "date_end": "1921-06-16", "date_text": "9-16 June 1921", "precision": "day", "title": "Ankara's reasons for the deportation order of June 1921", "summary": "Ankara's stated reasons for declaring the coast a war zone and moving Greek men inland were the fear of a Greek landing at Samsun, the Greek bands, and Greeks signalling to Greek warships. Biber (TÜBA) presents the deportation as a security necessity. In a circular to the foreign representatives, forwarded to Rome by the Apostolic Delegate on 25 July 1921, Foreign Minister Yusuf Kemal said that bands of the 'Pontus' organisation were slaughtering the people of Muslim villages and that this was the reason for deporting the whole Greek population of the region; on 21 July he told Admiral Bristol that there was no order to deport civilians, only those who had supported the 'Pontus' movement or the rebels.", "places": [{"name": "İnebolu", "lat": 41.975, "lon": 33.761, "role": "shelled by Greek warships, 9 June 1921"}, {"name": "Ankara", "lat": 39.933, "lon": 32.86, "role": "decisions of 12 and 16 June 1921"}, {"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "feared landing site"}], "people": ["Nureddin Pasha"], "sources_primary": ["BCA 941/95-12 (12 June 1921) and the decree of 16 June 1921, via Biber", "Dolci to Van Rossum, 25 July 1921, Propaganda Fide, Nuova Serie vol. 692, p. 433rv (Yusuf Kemal's circular; via Kyriakides & Nikoloudis 2026, pp. 75–76)", "Yusuf Kemal to Bristol, 21 July 1921 (via Kyriakidis 2023, p. 52; Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019, p. 21)"], "sources_modern": ["Biber (TÜBA) ch. 13", "Kyriakides (trans. Nikoloudis), The Genocide of Orthodox Greeks and the Persecution of Christians in the Pontus Region according to Vatican Archives (Gorgias 2026; Greek ed. 2015), pp. 75–76", "Kyriakidis, 'Testimonies of American Charitable and Missionary Organizations on the Genocide of the Pontic Greeks', in Akçam, Kyriakidis et al. (eds), The Genocide of the Christian Populations in the Ottoman Empire and its Aftermath (Routledge 2023), p. 52"], "confidence": "medium", "disputed": "The order itself is told in the chapter (H6-055); this entry gives its justification.", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: justification in the Ankara documents; Biber (TÜBA)"], "main": false, "counter_evidence": "Partly corroborated: Greece declared a naval blockade of the Black Sea coast on 26 March 1921, and in summer 1921 a Greek squadron stopped Turkish ships, took passengers prisoner and lightly shelled İnebolu (HMS Sportive report, via Morris & Ze'evi, p. 407). Against the rest: the İnebolu shelling caused no casualties and was, in an Italian witness's words, 'merely a Turkish \"excuse for the massacres\"', the campaign having begun weeks earlier (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 407); the relocation of Greek men had been proposed in Ankara on 29 May, before the shelling (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 12); no Greek landing on the Pontic coast was attempted; and women and children were deported from the summer of 1921, which the stated reasons do not cover; the Interior Ministry's own order of 25 June did not recommend it (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 25), and Nureddin admitted it (B1-022).", "overlaps_with": ["H7-T04", "B1-T03"]}, {"id": "H6-T09", "date": "1922-06-07", "date_text": "7 June 1922", "precision": "day", "title": "Turkish casualty figures for the bombardment of Samsun", "summary": "Turkish accounts put the casualties of the Greek naval bombardment of Samsun at 4 soldiers killed and 3 wounded (Doğanay 2006; Ünal 2022, as cited on Wikipedia).", "places": [{"name": "Samsun", "lat": 41.287, "lon": 36.33, "role": "shelled"}], "sources_modern": ["Doğanay 2006; Ünal 2022 (as cited on Wikipedia)"], "confidence": "medium", "basis": "turkish_only", "basis_detail": ["Turkish sources only: Doğanay 2006; Ünal 2022"], "main": false, "counter_evidence": "We know both figures only as cited on Wikipedia and have not consulted the works themselves."}], "synthesis": "## From the constitution to the war, 1908–1914\n\nThe Young Turk revolution of 24 July 1908 was welcomed in Trebizond and Samsun by Greeks, Armenians and Muslims alike, and Pontic Greeks sat in the new parliament: Matthaios Kofidis was elected for Trebizond in 1908, 1912 and 1914 (Boura 1983) (H6-001). The goodwill did not last. Christians became liable for military service in 1909, and many young Pontic men left for Russia and America rather than serve (H6-003). A Muslim boycott of Greek merchants followed the Cretan crisis of 1909–10 and was revived by the Committee of Union and Progress after the Balkan Wars (H6-002, H6-004). A circular of 5 July 1914, sent also to Trebizond and Canik, ordered the boycott lifted while talks with Greece went on; Akçam (2012, pp. 83–86) reads it as a show for foreign opinion.\n\nIn 1914 bands of the Special Organization drove between 150,000 and 300,000 Greeks out of Eastern Thrace and the Aegean coast, beginning with the attack on Phocaea on 12 June (H6-005). The Pontus was barely touched that year, but between September and December the Interior Ministry had the property of the Greek communities of Trebizond, Ordu and Giresun listed, with its owners and its value (Akçam 2012, pp. 37–39) (AK-001).\n\n## The war years, 1914–1918\n\nThe war reached the Pontus in mid-November 1914, when the Russian fleet shelled Trebizond; a Capuchin missionary's diary for 16 November describes women and children crowding into the Catholic church and civilians killed (H6-008).\n\nNon-Muslim conscripts were disarmed and sent to unarmed labour battalions (*amele taburları*). By early 1915 most adult Pontic Greek men had been taken, and typhus, hunger and beatings killed very many of them. Those who deserted to the mountains became the first guerrillas (H7-001, H6-009). In Constantinople the police deported some 200 Greek community leaders on 8 March 1915, according to Morris & Ze'evi (p. 387); the Greek Legation reported up to 10,000 (H6-010).\n\n**The Armenians of Trebizond, 1915.** The vali, Cemal Azmi, directed the destruction of the Armenians of the vilayet (H6-006, H6-011). Their leaders were arrested in late June 1915 and convoys left from 1 July; the men were killed inland, and women and children were drowned at sea, as the US consul Heizer, the Italian consul Gorrini, a Turkish deputy and the governor of Giresun later testified. Topal Osman's Giresun band took part. At the Trabzon court-martial of 1919 Azmi and his associate Nail were sentenced to death in their absence (H6-039). In the Samsun district the Armenians had been deported by early July 1915, 26,374 of them by Talat's own register, and many converted under pressure; at Merzifon an American missionary reported \"thousands\" of Armenians hidden in the Greek mountain villages (Akçam 2012, pp. 123, 259, 292–296) (AK-002).\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch09_front.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch09_front.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1120\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of the eastern Pontus showing the Russian advance of 1916, the front from Tirebolu to Kemah held until February 1918, and the Greeks who left for Batum with the Russians.\"></a><figcaption>The war in the east, 1916–1918: the Russian advance of 1916 with its dates, the front held from July 1916 to February 1918, and the Pontic Greeks who left with the Russians in 1918. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\n**The Russian advance, 1916.** In February 1916 the Russians attacked the Ottoman line at Arhavi, with fire from the sea, and on 16 February Erzurum fell. They landed at Atina (Pazar) on 4 March and at Mapavri (Çayeli) on 5 March, took Rize on 6 March and reached the İyidere, the boundary between Trebizond and Lazistan, on 9 March. The Ottoman troops left Trebizond on the night of 15/16 April (Walton); the vali handed over the city and left on 16/17 April (Chrysanthos, who was there); the Russians entered on 18 April (5 April in the Old Style). Gümüşhane and Bayburt fell in July, and Erzincan on 25 July. From then until February 1918 the front stood on the Harşit river at Tirebolu and ran inland past Kelkit to the passage between Erzincan and Kemah (H6-015 to H6-018, H6-022).\n\n| Date (1916) | The front |\n|---|---|\n| 5–7 Feb | Russian attack at Arhavi; the Russians reach the Viçe (Fındıklı) line |\n| 16 Feb | Erzurum falls |\n| 4–6 Mar | landings at Atina (Pazar) and Mapavri (Çayeli); Rize taken |\n| 9 Mar | the İyidere, the Trebizond–Lazistan boundary |\n| 15–17 Apr | the Ottomans leave Trebizond |\n| 18 Apr | the Russians enter Trebizond |\n| mid-July | Gümüşhane and Bayburt |\n| 25 July | Erzincan |\n| July 1916 – Feb 1918 | the front on the Harşit at Tirebolu, then inland past Kelkit |\n\n**The occupation.** Before he left, Cemal Azmi handed the city to Metropolitan Chrysanthos, and the Russians kept him at the head of its civil administration (H6-018, H6-019). Greek sources say he helped the Muslims who stayed or came back to recover their property, and on 31 January 1918 the Ottoman commander Vehip Pasha thanked him in writing for \"the noble and paternal protection\" he had given \"the Turkish element\"; the letter is known only as Greek sources print it (Chrysanthos' memorandum of 1919, quoted in *Le Martyre du Pont-Euxin*, p. 9). A Catholic missionary wrote in March 1917 that the Catholics enjoyed liberty under the occupation (Kyriakidis 2026, p. 57). Tens of thousands of Muslims fled west ahead of the Russians; Chrysanthos confirms the flight from Trebizond, but the numbers, and the death toll, come only from Turkish sources (H6-017). The Allies' secret agreement of 26 April 1916 promised Trebizond to Russia (H6-020).\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch09_deportations.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch09_deportations.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1120\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of the Pontus west of the front showing the places from which Greeks were deported in 1915–17, with dates, the inland destinations named in the sources, and the bands of deserters in the mountains.\"></a><figcaption>The deportations of 1915–17 from the Ottoman-held coast: the places named in the sources with their dates, the destinations, and the bands of deserters in the mountains. The lines show direction only; most of the routes are not known. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\n**The deportations of 1915–17.** In the parts of the Pontus still under Ottoman control, west and south of the Russian front, the authorities deported the Greeks inland. The Ecumenical Patriarchate's record of 1919 gives the fate of each village, diocese by diocese, and the village pages of this atlas carry it, among them those of Kolonia (PT-001), of Torul, Argyroupolis, Cheriana and Kelkit (PT-002), and of Ordu and Fatsa (PT-005). Where its English text speaks of \"Amassia\" (pp. 120–122) it means the diocese of Amaseia, whose seat was Samsun; Morris & Ze'evi and Akçam took it for the town of Amasya.\n\nThe first removals came in December 1915, when villagers of the Ünye area were deported; a Greek Patriarchate report forwarded by the Americans says that men were killed and women raped (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 389) (H7-003). In the winter and spring of 1916, as the front fell back, the commander Kâmil Pasha ordered the Greek villagers behind his lines deported in midwinter, telling them \"We must suffer, so must you\" (an American missionary's letter, via Morris & Ze'evi, p. 389). On 15 April 1916, a Greek Legation report says, sixteen villages of the Vazelon district, some 6,000 people, were ordered to Argyroupolis and fled to the forests instead; 650 sheltered in the monastery with 130 refugees from Trebizond (Brown & Ion 1918, p. 45). When a group hiding in a cave near Kounaka surrendered, 26 young women threw themselves into the river near Gephyra (Grigoryan 2019, p. 281) (H6-014).\n\nOttoman policy on the coast was set out in a cable to Trebizond of 15 May 1916. Since a \"benign and tolerant policy\" was being followed toward Greece, the coastal Greeks were not all to be moved inland, only those in places judged militarily unsafe, and those moved were to go to Kastamonu and to districts of Sivas that had no Muslim refugees (Akçam 2012, pp. 106–107) (H6-014). In June and July 1916 the Greeks of the Kastamonu coast (İnebolu, Cide), of Taderesi in Ankara province and of Sinope were deported, those of Sinope with four hours' notice. The German consulate at Samsun reported on 15 or 16 July that those not killed would die of disease or hunger, that the vali's fanaticism was the cause and desertion and espionage the pretexts: *\"Ausweisung und Ausrottung sind türkisch gleiche Begriffe\"*, \"expulsion and extermination are, in Turkish usage, the same thing\". Hofmann (2011) gives the report to Vice-consul Kuckhoff on 16 July, while her own footnote names Heinrich Bergfeld as the signatory of telegram no. 129 of 15 July; the German file itself has not been found (H7-007).\n\nFrom 16 November 1916 to March 1917 the Greeks of the Tirebolu district were marched over the Pontic range to Şebinkarahisar, Koyulhisar and Suşehri (H6-024). Their losses are disputed: Valavanis has 15,195 reduced to about 12,000, Hofmann (2011, p. 62) 550 survivors of 16,750, without a source. In Samsun at the end of November 1916 the commander Rafet Bey told the Austrian consul Kwiatkowski that they must \"do with the Greeks as we did with the Armenians\". Three English versions of the report circulate; the volume of Vatican documents (p. 50, after Enepekides) dates it 29 November and quotes: \"We must at last do away with the Greeks. Today I sent patrols of gendarmes to the outskirts ordering them to kill any Greek they would come across\" (H7-008).\n\nIn December 1916 Enver ordered the Greeks removed from a coastal strip about 50 km deep, \"for purely military reasons\"; German diplomats were told it would be done more mildly than to the Armenians. The Ottomans' own allies thought the reasons a pretext: the Austrian ambassador Pallavicini wrote in January 1918 that the deportations were not dictated by military needs (quoted in Agtzidis 2005, p. 155), and Talat told the German ambassador Radowitz in October 1916 that a wholesale removal from the coast had been planned earlier that year and stopped by Enver so as not to provoke Greece (Hofmann 2011, pp. 59–60). The notables of Samsun, Bafra, Ordu, Tirebolu, Amasya and Çarşamba were deported, and about 200 Greeks were \"apparently hanged\" for desertion (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 390) (H6-026). Akçam (2012, pp. 109–111) dates the change of policy to the autumn of 1916 and quotes Talat's cable to Samsun of 11 January 1917: the Third Army had made the arrangements, and the aim was to take the coastal Greeks \"some thirty to fifty kilometers inland\". Ten days later the Samsun Greeks were assigned to villages in Sivas, Kastamonu, Ankara and Bolu (H7-011).\n\nAround Samsun the villages burned. Pallavicini's reports count eleven villages burned by 17 December and 28 by 15 January, and an Austrian report has the 3,000–4,000 people of Aylaskoy and Kadıköy driven inland at night (Hofmann 2011, p. 60). Samsun itself was emptied on 10, 11 and 13 January 1917. The Greek Legation's reports date the arrest of 80 notables and the round-up of Kadıköy and Aylaskoy to 27 December 1916, and list the villages of the Samsun district emptied by February 1917, all those of the coast as far as Alaçam (Brown & Ion 1918, pp. 48–55, 68–69). The deportees numbered 13,406 from Samsun by the commander Refet's own report, about 20,000 by the Greek Legation's count, or 30,000 according to Metropolitan Germanos Karavangelis, who told US officers that \"the greatest cause\" was \"religion\" (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 391) (H6-028, H7-053). Akçam (2012, p. 112) dates the systematic phase from the arrival of the Unionist leader Bahaeddin Şakir in December 1916; Talat himself asked Canik on 26 February 1917 which villages had been burnt. Instructions of January–March 1917 settled Muslim refugees in the emptied villages but ruled that Greek property, unlike Armenian, was not to be liquidated (AK-003).\n\nAt Kerasous and Ordu non-Greek sources confirm the Greek accounts. The Giresun Greeks were driven over the mountains towards Sivas in midwinter, although they had been promised they could stay at Kulakkaya (German reports, in Hofmann 2011, p. 60); the American relief treasurer reported them expelled by 13 January 1917; and the Greek Legation counted 23,000 people from 38 coastal villages on 7 February, and 22,356 deported from the district to January, village by village (Brown & Ion 1918, pp. 47, 67). In July 1917 an army order from Suşehri, reported by the American missionary Crawford, required that \"no Greek man over 16 and under 50 be found in Ordou\" by 12 July: \"as for the families, we will send further orders later\" (Kyriakidis 2023, pp. 47–48) (H6-027).\n\nGermany and Austria-Hungary protested. In February 1917 German diplomats concluded that the Samsun \"ban\", carried out \"under the pretext of pursuing Greek bands\", was a persecution on a \"large scale\" (Hofmann 2011, pp. 60–61) (H6-029). A sentence often attributed to the German chancellor Bethmann Hollweg (31 January 1917) is in none of the books we have read; a similar one is attributed to Talat on 31 January 1916, and the two may have been confused.\n\n**The guerrillas.** Bands of deserters formed at Santa, under Efkleidis Kourtidis; in the hills of Erbaa and Taşova, under Kapetan Iakof and then Anastasios Papadopoulos, \"Kotsa Nastas\"; and in the Çarşamba and Bafra hills, under \"Anton Pasha\", killed in 1917, whose name comes from Turkish-language writers (H6-009). Morris & Ze'evi accept that during the war these bands, \"composed chiefly of youngsters avoiding the draft\", \"occasionally attacked Turkish villagers and gendarmes\" (p. 383), though their note cites only Shaw. The Greek historian Agtzidis (1992, p. 167) writes that the bands began as village self-defence and names \"Russian aid\" among their sources of arms (H7-063). The detailed Turkish accounts of raids are given in the last section.\n\n**The Russian withdrawal.** After the armistice of Erzincan (18 December 1917; 5 December Old Style) the Russian troops began to leave Trebizond (H6-030). On 11/24 December the French representative in Tiflis, Colonel Chardigny, with the approval of his British and American colleagues, asked Chrysanthos to help raise a Greek volunteer regiment at Trebizond, within a Greek division under General Ananias, to keep order and defend the city against the Turks (*Martyre*, pp. 12–13). Chrysanthos was cautious, fearing to inflame the Muslims (Georganopoulos 2023, p. 74); he appealed to Vehip Pasha and armed some Greek villages for self-defence, Santa among them. Vehip retook Kelkit on 7 February 1918, Erzincan on 13 February, Bayburt on 19 February, Trebizond on 23 February and Erzurum on 12 March (H6-033). The head of the Capuchin mission recorded that the city's commander then announced orders from Constantinople to deport all the Greeks, women, old people and children included, that Chrysanthos protested, and that Turkish irregulars were burning and killing in the villages around the city (Kyriakidis 2026, pp. 62–63). Greek accounts credit Chrysanthos with saving the city's Greeks; whether the order was carried out is not known. Between 30,000 (Kwiatkowski) and 80,000 (Xanthopoulou-Kyriakou) Pontic Greeks left with the Russians, and the Capuchin diary records the departures for Batum (H6-032). At Erzincan and Erzurum Armenian irregulars killed Muslims as the front collapsed (H6-031).\n\n## The armistice and the Pontic question, 1918–1920\n\nAfter the armistice of Mudros (30 October 1918) the surviving deportees came home to burned villages (H6-036). An Armenian Patriarchate report put the Greeks of the Trebizond vilayet at 353,533 before the war, the figure of Soteriades' map of 1918, of whom 231,579 had been deported and 80,000 had died (via Kévorkian).\n\nThey found the way home blocked (H6-037). In January 1919 a US officer at Samsun reported that the authorities had armed the Turkish villagers of the Tsinik district in \"a carefully laid down plan … to eliminate the Greek and Armenian elements\" (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 398). The French commissioner at Trebizond, Paul Lépissier, reported returning refugees murdered as alleged revolutionaries in January, 600 returnees prevented from landing in late May, only 5,000–6,000 Greeks back in the Trebizond area by June, and organised persecution at Kerasous, Merzifon and Bulancak from 4 June (Georganopoulos 2023, pp. 76–80). In September British officers reported that \"public security all along the Black Sea Coast was very bad\" (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 398). Armenian Patriarchate reports describe Unionist networks handing out arms at Trebizond, the prefect and mayor of Ordu arming Muslims (March 1919), Laz bands raiding Christian villages near Ünye (April), and the murder of Greek notables at Sürmene, Giresun and Maçka (July) (Kévorkian). Near Merzifon returning refugees formed bands to defend themselves and recover their property, which, the Near East Relief reported in June 1919, gave the Turks a \"pretext\" (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 395).\n\nBy mid-1919 Topal Osman was mayor of Giresun. The Ottoman investigating judge at Trebizond, Karagueuzian, cited his appointment in June as proof that the government would not punish the killers of 1915; an Armenian Patriarchate report dates it to August (Kévorkian). A Catholic missionary held his roughly 600 Laz responsible for many murders in Giresun, and on 19 December 1919 the head of the Capuchin mission wrote that the Giresun nationalists had decided in council to \"get rid of the Christians\" but had put it off, keeping them as hostages until the decisions at Paris were known (Kyriakidis 2026, pp. 66–68). The figure of 12,000 Pontic Greeks killed in 1919, often quoted, is in none of the books we have read.\n\n**The Pontic claims.** In February 1919 the Trabzon Society for the Defence of National Rights was founded (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu 2019, p. 10); an Armenian Patriarchate report of 17 February describes notables implicated in the wartime massacres forming a \"pan-Turk committee\" (H6-038). The numbers claimed for the Greeks of the Pontus varied widely: 700,000 in a Greek memorandum, over 800,000 in the *Martyre*, some two million by Pontic spokesmen, while Western diplomats thought about 450,000 likely (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 382). The Greek count of 1910–12 had found 400,586 in the six metropolises.\n\nChrysanthos came to Paris between 29 April and 16 May 1919 with a plan for an independent Pontic republic, and met Wilson on 16 May (H6-040). Neutral observers read his position differently: Lépissier counted him among the leaders of a movement for autonomy and union with Greece (March 1919), while the American Chesbrough reported that summer that many influential Greeks of Trebizond, Chrysanthos among them, opposed separatism (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 383). Venizelos told the Greek parliament in May 1920 that independence was impossible, offered Lloyd George a landing at Trebizond on 5 October 1920, and lost the election of 14 November (H6-045, H6-047); the Kazantzakis mission was at Batum by 1 August 1919 (H6-043). A plan for a confederation with Armenia was discussed in December 1919 and January 1920 (H6-044). On 22 November 1920 Wilson's award gave Armenia Trebizond and the Harşit valley, declined to rule on the Pontic request, and noted that \"the Armenian element was undeniably inferior numerically to the Greek portion of the Christian minority\" (H6-048).\n\n## Under the Ankara government, 1919–1923\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch09_ankara.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch09_ankara.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1120\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of the Pontus from Zonguldak to Trebizond showing the places of killings, deportations, trials and attacks named for 1919–1923.\"></a><figcaption>Under the Ankara government, 1919–1923: the places of killings, deportations, trials and attacks, with the dates the sources give. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\n**1920.** In Giresun Topal Osman had Greek houses destroyed in February, according to an Armenian Patriarchate report, and from the night of 13 August he imprisoned the Christian men and had five or six shot each evening until a ransom of 300,000 lira was paid (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 401, from British minutes that record Patriarchate information). A British officer at Samsun reported Nationalist officers preaching the extermination of Christians and placards at Ünye and Fatsa blaming them (spring 1920), and Bishop Polykarpos of Ordu was taken to Ankara in early June (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 399, after a French consular report). In November an Armenian Patriarchate report has eight Greek villages near Vezirköprü burned, with 600 dead. From December 1920 the notables began to be arrested (H6-046).\n\n**The first tribunals and the Central Army.** The Sivas Independence Tribunal, usually sitting at Amasya, punished 454 Greeks between 20 October 1920 and 15 March 1921 for espionage, aiding bandits, desertion, joining bands and \"Ponticism\"; it passed 122 death sentences, of which 12 were carried out (its dockets, via Aybars; Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 16) (H6-080). On 9 December 1920 Ankara created the Central Army, about 15,000 men under Nureddin Pasha with headquarters at Amasya, to deal with the \"Pontic question\" and the Alevi rising at Koçgiri (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, pp. 12, 24) (H6-049, H6-051).\n\n**Merzifon and Samsun, February 1921.** In February a Muslim teacher of Anatolia College at Merzifon was murdered by an unknown assailant (Turkish accounts name him Zeki Bey). On 16 February the Central Army searched the college, the American hospital and the metropolis at Samsun. No arms were found, but four teachers and two students of the Greek students' \"Pontos\" society were arrested and the college was closed (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 412; Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 19) (H6-050). Ankara's use of the seized papers as proof of a conspiracy rests on Turkish sources alone and is given in the last section.\n\n**Spring 1921.** The interior minister Fethi urged Kemal in February to authorise \"more expeditious and larger scale\" deportations (an intercepted telegram; Morris & Ze'evi, p. 405). Labour battalions were formed again on 2 March and about 1,500 non-Muslim men sent to them. From 17 May about 100 Greek villages south of Bafra were destroyed, a missionary's figure relayed by US officers, who judged the campaign \"directed by high authority—probably Angora\" (Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 405, 407–408). The men of Alaçam over twelve years old were imprisoned and massacred in May (H6-052).\n\n**The order of June 1921.** Moving the Greek men inland was raised in Ankara on 29 May. A Greek squadron stopped Turkish ships and took passengers prisoner, and on 9 June it lightly shelled İnebolu, without casualties according to an Italian witness, who called the bombardment \"merely a Turkish 'excuse for the massacres'\" (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 407). On 12 June Ankara ordered all the Greeks of the coast aged 15 to 50 moved inland, from 16 June (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, pp. 12–13) (H6-055).\n\nIn Samsun the first 200 men had been deported on 28 May; Topal Osman's men arrested some 800 Greek clerks of the American Tobacco Company and 1,500 others (a press dispatch quoted by Rendel; Hofmann 2011, p. 71). Seventy Turkish notables telegraphed Kemal that it was against their religion to massacre women and children, and the mufti issued a fatwa; Kemal answered that exile was \"a merciful act\" (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 410). At Merzifon, from 23–24 July, Osman's band and then local men, gendarmes and troops killed, raped and looted for more than a week; of 2,000–2,500 Christians almost all the men were killed (pp. 411–413). The toll is given as \"upwards of 700\" by the missionary Hosford, 950 by the British, about 1,000 or 1,200 by the missionary Gertrude Anthony and Admiral Bristol, and 300 by the Ottoman officer Mehmed Rauf (H6-053).\n\n<figure class=\"nmap\"><a href=\"maps/ch10_marches.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"maps/ch10_marches.jpg\" width=\"2000\" height=\"1360\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Relief map of Anatolia from Samsun and Trebizond to Diyarbakır and Aleppo showing the main road of the deportations of 1921–22, a Bafra convoy of women and children, the other destinations of the order of 19 June 1921 and the road of the survivors to Aleppo.\"></a><figcaption>The roads of the deportations, 1921–22: from Samsun by Kavak, Havza, Amasya, Tokat and Sivas to Malatya, Harput and Diyarbakır, a convoy of women and children from Bafra, the men of Trebizond sent by Erzurum, and the survivors who reached Aleppo, with the numbers the sources give. Tap the map to enlarge it. Relief from the AWS Terrain Tiles; coast and rivers from Natural Earth.</figcaption></figure>\n\n**The convoys.** Convoys of men left Samsun, Bafra and Alaçam from mid-June (H6-056). A deportee of the third Samsun convoy wrote that 660 were killed by the gendarmes on a mountain descent; of Cosmos Lilidas' convoy of about 1,000, 292 remained after the guards opened fire (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 423). Stanley Hopkins of the Near East Relief reported 550 executed at Kavak on 26 June and 1,300 on 15 August (Kyriakidis 2023, p. 51), and Rendel's memorandum of 20 March 1922 has 1,300 shot near Kavak on 15 or 16 August (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 408; Hofmann 2011, p. 75). The Lazistan deputies told the Assembly that the first Samsun convoy had been attacked six hours from the town (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 13). From mid-July the women and children were ordered out too. Admiral Bristol asked Kemal on 18 July to stop it, and the Apostolic Delegate Dolci asked him on 18 August to cancel an order exiling 5,000 women and children from near Samsun (Kyriakidis 2026, p. 79); Samsun's Muslim notables protested, and the deportations there were paused in July (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 25). The Central Army's records count 63,844 people moved by November, or 58,974 by district: Samsun 27,995, Amasya 14,000, Giresun 8,500, Ordu 4,910. At Mezre-Harput the relief worker Mark Ward registered 20,526 deportees, about 18,000 of them Greeks (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 426).\n\n**Bafra.** Bafra was surrounded on Saturday 5/18 June 1921 (the two dates are the Julian and the Gregorian). The burning of men in churches, about 535 at Elezli and 500 at Selamelik, with 680 taken to Kocasu whose fate is unknown, rests on one Greek witness, Ioannis Dilmitzoglou, whom Morris & Ze'evi repeat. The US destroyer *Brooks* reported on 8 July that \"as many as 90 percent of deported Greeks have been killed\" in the Bafra area; written before any women were deported, the report concerns the men's convoys. US destroyers reported about 6,000 women and children deported around 31 August and 2,500 more on 19 September. Fotiadis gives convoys of 4,120 on 16 September and 1,900 on 24 September; these may be the same people under other dates, so the figures cannot simply be added together (H7-024).\n\n**A Greek band raid.** The war diary of the US destroyer *McFarland* records that in early September 1921 a Greek band of nearly a hundred attacked a Turkish village about eight miles from Samsun, killed some villagers and burned their houses, and that a similar raid followed in October (Shenk 2017, p. 201). It is the first neutral report we have found of a band attack on Muslims in the Pontus (H6-079).\n\n**Trebizond, 1921.** In early April the elders of the Greek community, Kofidis and the publisher Kapetanidis among them, were imprisoned (Fr Cirillo's memoir; Kyriakidis 2026, p. 75). US naval reports have the men deported in groups of fifty in May and 300 on the road to Erzurum by July (Kyriakidis 2023, pp. 52–53). In August more than 500 men who reported for \"fortification work\" were imprisoned, deported in groups of fifty and many of them shot, according to two refugee witnesses (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 423). The people of Santa were deported on 10–12 September (H6-057).\n\n**The Amasya hangings.** The Pontic notables were tried by the Samsun Independence Tribunal, which began work on 17 August 1921 and sat at Amasya (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, pp. 17–18) (H6-058). Among the hanged were Kofidis, Kapetanidis, the Ottoman Bank director Pavlos Papadopoulos, teachers of Anatolia College and the archimandrite Platon Aivazidis, about seventy years old; Bishop Euthymios of Zela had already died in prison. Chrysanthos, Germanos of Amaseia, Polykarpos of Neocaesarea and Laurentios of Chaldia were condemned in their absence. The counts differ. By the tribunal's own records it had executed 177 people, three of them Muslims, and sentenced 74 in their absence by 10 October 1921 (Tsirkinidis has 177 hanged and 44 sentenced in absentia), and it passed 622 death sentences among 1,163 sentences in all. Hosford put those hanged at Amasya in September at about 250, the British at 168; the Samsun papers cited by the Patriarch name 168 Greeks and 3 Armenians; the lists in the newspaper *Ehali*, reprinted in the Black Book, give about 155 names; and Tepeyran and Shaw give 485 death sentences. The 72 \"Greek martyrs\" were hanged on 8/21 September 1921, one day in the Julian and Gregorian calendars, not two. Greece and Britain protested, and Senator King raised the hangings in the US Senate.\n\n**The debate in Ankara.** The Lazistan deputies attacked Nureddin in the Assembly on 5 October 1921, saying he had ordered a massacre in Samsun and the deportation of women and children. The Assembly removed him on 3 November on charges that included using irregulars, taking a bribe from a Pontic Greek, ordering plunder during the deportations and \"harming the Muslims by setting the stage for the Pontians to take to the mountains\"; he had also forbidden 56 Samsun notables who objected to deporting women and children to travel. At Kemal's insistence the trial was dropped, and the Central Army was abolished on 8 February 1922 (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 14) (H6-049).\n\nThe state's own counts of the dead are 1,275 Greeks killed between 29 December 1921 and 20 March 1922, 3,777 \"armed Greeks\" between 20 March and 15 May 1922, and 11,198 killed and 10,886 surrendered by February 1923 (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, pp. 15, 25) (H6-078). They are minimum figures; their description of the dead as \"bandits\" is discussed in the last section.\n\n**1922.** On 15 February 1922 Topal Osman's irregulars burned the Greek villages above Ordu, herding about 170 women and children into two houses and setting them alight (a survivor, via an American missionary; Morris & Ze'evi, p. 416) (H7-033). Fethi's sweep of the mountains began on 18 February, and the 10th Division reported about 15,000 Greeks killed or captured in two months; a telegram from Greek and Circassian refugees of Bafra alleged rewards for heads and said that Circassians had hidden about 140 Greek neighbours (H6-081). About 1,324 Greeks, mostly women and children, were deported from Samsun on 10–11 April and 1,462 on 15 April (Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 417–418). From late April the whole Greek male population of the Trebizond region from 15 years upwards was deported in groups of fifty, and boys of 11 to 14 were rounded up for a camp near Cevizlik, though Trebizond's mayor and vali opposed it (pp. 419–420). In May the head of the Capuchin mission wrote that only women and children remained of the Greeks of Trebizond, that no Greek villages were left inland, and that Topal Osman's band was killing in the Giresun countryside, \"supported by the Turkish government and honoured by the Muslim population\" (Kyriakidis 2026, pp. 82–83). By June the villages were empty (H6-060). The Greek navy's shelling of Samsun on 7 June 1922 achieved nothing (H6-061). Kotsa Nastas was killed in December (H6-063).\n\n## The numbers, and who gives them\n\n| What | Figure | Who gives it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Greeks expelled from Thrace and the Aegean coast, 1914 | 150,000–200,000; about 300,000 | Bjørnlund 2008; Akçam 2012, p. 63 |\n| Greek leaders deported from Constantinople, March 1915 | about 200; up to 10,000 | Morris & Ze'evi, p. 387; Greek Legation |\n| Greeks of the Trebizond vilayet, deported and dead in the war | 353,533 in all; 231,579 deported; 80,000 dead | Armenian Patriarchate report, via Kévorkian (353,533 is Soteriades' figure of 1918) |\n| Deported from the Samsun area, winter 1916–17 | 13,406 (Samsun); about 20,000; about 30,000 | Refet's report; Greek Legation; Karavangelis |\n| Tirebolu–Elevi losses, 1916–17 | 15,195 reduced to about 12,000; 550 survivors of 16,750 | Valavanis; Hofmann 2011, p. 62 (no source) |\n| Greeks of the Pontus, as claimed in 1919–22 | 700,000; over 800,000; about 2 million; about 450,000 | Greek memorandum; *Martyre*; Pontic spokesmen; Western diplomats (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 382) |\n| Moved inland in 1921 | 63,844 by November; 58,974 by district | Central Army records (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, p. 25) |\n| Deported from the Pontus in 1921, estimates | \"well over 35,000\"; \"at least 75,000\", 20,000 surviving | Rendel, 28 December 1921; missionaries via the US consul at Aleppo (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 415) |\n| Deportees through Mezre-Harput, May 1921 – Feb 1922 | 20,526, about 18,000 of them Greeks | Ward (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 426) |\n| Killed near Kavak, 1921 | 330 and 660; 550 (26 June) and 1,300 (15 Aug); 1,300 (15 or 16 Aug); at least 900 | Black Book; Hopkins; Rendel; the Greek delegate to the League |\n| Killed at Merzifon, July 1921 | 300; upwards of 700; 950; about 1,000–1,200 | Rauf; Hosford; British; Anthony and Bristol |\n| Bafra men deported who died, June 1921 | \"as many as 90 percent\" | USS *Brooks*, 8 July 1921 |\n| Bafra women and children deported | about 6,000 (31 Aug); 2,500 (19 Sept) | US Navy; Fotiadis' 4,120 and 1,900 may be the same people |\n| Samsun Independence Tribunal | 177 executed and 74 condemned in absence by 10 Oct 1921; 622 death sentences among 1,163 | the tribunal's records, via Korucu & Dağlıoğlu |\n| Hanged at Amasya, September 1921 | 72 (8/21 Sept); 168; about 250; about 155 names | Fotiadis; British; Hosford; *Ehali* lists |\n| Greeks killed in the Pontus operations, Dec 1921 – Feb 1923 | 1,275; 3,777; 11,198 in all | Turkish state counts (Korucu & Dağlıoğlu, pp. 15, 25) |\n| Armed Greeks in the mountains | over 18,000 at the peak, about 1920; 20,000–30,000 in May 1922 | Agtzidis 1992, p. 167; a British observer (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 415) |\n| Waiting at the ports, March 1923 | Samsun 10,000; Trabzon 3,000; Ordu 1,500 | US naval diaries (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 463) |\n\nTwo sets of figures are left out of this table: the Turkish counts of Muslims killed by Greek bands, which are given in the last section, and the Armenian Patriarchate's 30,000 Greeks massacred between Ünye and Samsun and 6,500 of 7,000 conscripts killed on the Kavak road (Kévorkian), which no other source supports.\n\n## Where historians disagree\n\n**Security or extermination, 1916–17?** The Ottoman case is military, and it is set out in the last section. Writing of the removals from the Straits in 1915, Morris & Ze'evi grant that \"military concerns, without doubt, played a part\", with \"nationalism and religion\" in the background (p. 388). For the Pontus the evidence weighs against the security reading: the German consul's \"pretexts\", Rafet's words, Talat's admission that a wholesale removal had been planned, the German verdict of February 1917, and Germanos' \"religion\". Akçam, a Turkish-born historian working abroad, called the wartime removals \"relocation from the coastal areas into the interior out of military necessity\" in 2012 (preface, p. xvii), and in 2023 placed them within a wider, centrally run demographic policy. The body of his 2012 book is more precise: the removals of 1915–16 were area-by-area military measures (p. 106); the evacuations of 1916–18 were carried out \"with great brutality\" (p. 113, quoting Toynbee); Muslims were settled at once in the emptied villages, so no return was intended (p. 113); and the treatment of the Greeks differed from that of the Armenians \"in scope, intent, and motivation\" (p. 123). Vehip Pasha, by Minasidis' account, shielded some communities near Giresun, though German reports say the Giresun Greeks were driven inland.\n\n**Rebellion or self-defence, 1919–22?** Turkish historiography treats the Pontic bands as a Greek-backed rising that made deportation necessary. Neutral sources confirm that bands existed and sometimes attacked Muslims, that the Allies once wanted a Greek regiment raised at Trebizond (December 1917), and that some returning refugees armed themselves in 1919. The same sources say that the agitation produced \"almost no acts of overt rebellion\" and that \"Western observers uniformly asserted that there was no Pontine 'rebellion'\" (Morris & Ze'evi, pp. 383, 406), and they show that the deportations fell on women, children and orphans. Turkish deputies at the time blamed the growth of the bands on Nureddin's methods.\n\n**Were the Paris plans realistic?** Venizelos never backed independence wholeheartedly, and neutral observers even disagreed on whether Chrysanthos did.\n\n**Who was responsible in 1921?** Nureddin and Topal Osman, or Ankara? The mutasarrıf of Bafra told US officers that the orders came from Ankara. The interior ministry's own order of 25 June did not recommend deporting women and children; Nureddin did, and Kemal protected him.\n\n**Dates.** Several turn on the double dating of Greek sources: Bafra was surrounded on 5/18 June 1921, and the Amasya hangings were on 8/21 September 1921. At Trebizond in 1916 the Ottoman troops left on the night of 15/16 April and the vali on 16/17 April, and the Russians entered on 18 April. The Erzincan armistice is dated 5 or 18 December 1917.\n\n## Legend and evidence\n\nThe Bafra church burnings rest on one Greek eyewitness, Dilmitzoglou, whom Morris & Ze'evi repeat; the deportation of Bafra's men, women and children and the deaths on the road are attested independently by US naval and Near East Relief reports. Vehip Pasha's letter of thanks to Chrysanthos is known only as Greek sources print it. Some guerrilla numbers, such as 60,000 people saved or 12,000 sheltered by Kotsa Nastas, are Pontic tradition. Agtzidis gives more than 18,000 armed men at the peak, about 1920 (1992, p. 167); the British estimate of 20,000–30,000 armed men in the mountains in 1922 is of the same order but counts something different. Kemal's \"forty bands\" of May 1919 are reported by Morris & Ze'evi, after Shaw (p. 384), who judge that \"the reality was different\" (p. 383); Kemal's own report of 22 May 1919 counted thirteen Turkish bands as well.\n\n## The Greeks of the Pontus and their neighbours\n\nThe Armenians were the first victims, in 1915; on paper they were partners in 1919–20, and in 1920 the beneficiaries of Wilson's line. At Harput in 1921 Armenian survivors shared what they had with the Greek deportees (Garougian, via Hofmann 2011, pp. 72–73).\n\nThe violence was led by the state and by irregulars, but Turks also opposed it: the seventy Samsun notables and the mufti in 1921, the 56 notables banned for objecting, the mayor and vali of Trebizond in 1922, the Lazistan and Trebizond deputies in the Assembly, Turkish neighbours who warned Greeks, and a Turkish notable who said that \"what had happened made him ashamed to be a Turk\". The Circassians of Bafra hid about 140 Greek neighbours in 1922 and signed the refugees' telegram with them (H6-081). Laz irregulars made up much of Topal Osman's force, 2,000–3,000 at Merzifon according to an Ottoman officer and about 600 in Giresun according to a Catholic missionary; in 1920 Lazistan's Defence of Rights committee claimed 225,000 Laz and 54 Greeks in Lazistan (Georganopoulos 2023, p. 83). The Alevis of Koçgiri suffered at the hands of the same Central Army. The Russians were protectors and occupiers from 1916 to 1918, then the destination of exiles, and later, under Stalin, deporters.\n\n## The sources\n\n**Witnesses and documents.** For the frontier, the source is the text of Wilson's award (FRUS 1920, vol. III, doc. 949). The reports of Austro-Hungarian and German consuls and diplomats (Kwiatkowski, Pallavicini, Kuckhoff, Radowitz, Kühlmann) are the best contemporary evidence for 1916–17, coming from the Ottomans' own allies; they reach us through Hofmann, Fotiadis and Enepekides, and the originals have not been seen. The Ottoman and Ankara state records, among them Refet's report, the Central Army's counts, the closed sessions of the Assembly and the tribunal dockets, reach us through Aybars and through Korucu & Dağlıoğlu; they are admissions of what the state did, while their justifications are given in the last section. The reports of the US Navy, the US consuls and the Near East Relief (Ward, Yowell, Hopkins, Anthony, Hosford, Phelps, Crawford) are eyewitness accounts, reliable on what their authors saw, though the naval officers seldom left Samsun. The French commission at Trebizond (Lépissier, 1919–20), known through Georganopoulos, was neutral and close to events. The Catholic missionaries and Vatican diplomats (F. Lorenzo, Fr Cirillo, Mgr Naslian, Dolci), published by Kyriakidis and Nikoloudis, were neutral witnesses who wrote cautiously for fear of the authorities; they are the strongest new evidence for Trebizond in 1918 and 1922–23, though some passages come from Fr Cirillo's later memoir rather than his letters. Rendel's Foreign Office memoranda of 28 December 1921 and 20 March 1922 and the minutes of the British High Commission's Armenian-Greek Section are British compilations of Patriarchate information. The Armenian Patriarchate's reports, known through Kévorkian, are contemporary and detailed for 1919–20, but their casualty totals for 1921 are far above all other sources. Chrysanthos (1933), the Greek Patriarchate (1919), the Black Book (1922) and Valavanis (1925) make the Pontic case: rich in local detail, inflated in their figures. *Le Martyre du Pont-Euxin* (Geneva 1922), a Greek advocacy compilation, is useful for the documents it reprints, not for its own figures.\n\n**Historians.** Morris & Ze'evi (2019) give exact US and British archival references; their framing of a \"thirty-year genocide\" is debated, and for Bafra they repeat the Greek eyewitness. Hofmann (2011) is rich in German and Austrian documents, via Fotiadis, though some figures lack a source on the page. Kyriakidis & Nikoloudis (2026) present the Vatican documents; the documents are neutral, the framing is the authors'. The volume edited by Akçam, Kyriakidis and others (2023) contains Kyriakidis on American missionary testimony, Georganopoulos on the French commission, from the Nantes archives, and Chatzikyriakidis on the archive of the Pontic National Council. Kévorkian (2023) uses chiefly the Armenian Patriarchate's reports. Korucu & Dağlıoğlu (2019), Turkish journalists who call the events a genocide, transcribe and map the Turkish documents: the Assembly debates, the tribunals and the state counts. Shenk (2012, 2017) is reliable on the American observations, and Grigoryan (2019) on the Greek Legation's report of 1916 on Vazelon. Akçam (2012) quotes the Ottoman cipher cables on the Greeks, Pontic ones among them, with their archive references; the provincial archive of Trebizond, by his account, was dumped at sea in 1982 (pp. 21–22, after Ayşe Hür), but the Interior Ministry's side of the correspondence survives in the central archive in Istanbul. Minasidis (2024), Bjørnlund (2008) and Kévorkian (2011) are standard on 1914–17. Turkish works (Biber, Bilgin and Yazıcı, Duman, Aydın and Duman, Çakıcı, Alpaslan and Doğan) are used for dates and for the state's own records; their reading of events is set out in the last section. Allen & Muratoff, McMeekin, Erickson, Hovannisian, Ladas, Eddy, Pentzopoulos, Clark, Bugay and Polian give the context.\n\n## What the Turkish sources say\n\nThese claims rest on Turkish sources alone; each is given with what other sources say. Some parts are confirmed by neutral or Greek sources and are told in the chapter above: occasional band attacks on Muslims, the murder of a Muslim teacher at Merzifon in February 1921, and, in general terms, Russian arms for the bands and clashes between Muslims and Christians after the armistice with \"hundreds of victims\" (Agtzidis 1992, p. 167; 2005, p. 184) (H7-063).\n\n**The war years, 1916–18**\n- **Muslim deaths in the flight of 1916.** About 220,000 dead (Kaya), or a fall in population of 204,130 (the American demographer McCarthy, as cited by Çakıcı). *Limits:* there is no non-Turkish count, though a US consul noted heavy wartime losses among the Turks of Samsun in general.\n- **Bands burning Turkish houses in the occupied zone.** Neither confirmed nor contradicted by any other source we have found.\n- **The security case for 1916–17.** *Partly confirmed:* the Russian landings and the deserters were real, and Morris & Ze'evi grant that military concerns played a part in the removals of 1915 from the Straits. *Against:* the German consul's \"pretexts\", Talat's admission, the German verdict of February 1917, Rafet's words and Germanos' \"religion\".\n\n**The bands and the \"Pontus question\", 1916–22**\n- **Band violence against Muslims.** Fethi told the Assembly on 29 December 1921 that bands had killed 1,303 Muslims and damaged 3,303 dwellings; *Pontus Meselesi* (1922) counted 1,641 killed and claimed that over 10,000 rifles had been handed out by Tsarist Russia and Britain. *Partly confirmed:* the US naval report of a raid near Samsun (September 1921), occasional wartime attacks, the Allies' plan for a Greek regiment in 1917, and, in general terms, Russian arms and post-armistice clashes. *Against the totals:* no non-Turkish confirmation; they were stated while Nureddin was under attack, and the Lazistan deputies blamed the bands on his deportations.\n- **The \"Pontus question\" as a separatist rising.** Kemal's \"forty guerrilla\" bands (May 1919), his \"Pontus government\" directed from Athens and Istanbul (1920) and \"defeated\" (1923). *Partly confirmed:* Pontic leaders sought a state, Rendel said the Greek Church had fostered a national movement, and armed bands existed. *Against:* \"almost no acts of overt rebellion\" (Morris & Ze'evi, p. 383); \"Western observers uniformly asserted that there was no Pontine 'rebellion'\" (p. 406); even McCarthy judged the \"Pontus Republic\" revolutionaries a matter for the police, not for deportation (p. 382).\n\n**The deportations and the killings of 1921**\n- **The Merzifon papers as proof of a conspiracy.** *Against:* no arms were found; Bristol called the Turks \"over-zealous and unreasonable\"; the governor had allowed the \"Pontos\" literary society.\n- **Ankara's reasons for the order of June 1921,** including Yusuf Kemal's circular blaming the \"Pontus\" bands. *Partly confirmed:* the blockade and the Greek squadron's seizures of ships. *Against:* İnebolu had no casualties and was \"merely a Turkish 'excuse for the massacres'\"; the removal had been proposed before it; women and children were deported against the interior ministry's own order.\n- **The denial of Merzifon.** Yusuf Kemal called it a \"legend\" (15 September 1921), and later said that \"the troops simply got from under control\". *Against:* the American witnesses.\n- **\"Operations against bands\".** The dead of Kavak fell \"in battle\", and the state counted \"bandits\" killed. *Against:* Near East Relief and naval reports of escorts firing on deportees, and the Lazistan deputies' own admission. The counts are used in the chapter without this description.\n\n**1922–23**\n- **The bombardment of Samsun, 7 June 1922.** Four soldiers killed and three wounded, in Turkish accounts, against about 90 casualties reported by a US destroyer commander.\n- **The departures from Trebizond in 1923.** \"Voluntary\", Turkey said at Lausanne. *Against:* the *Manchester Guardian*'s correspondent and the Catholic missionaries, who record an order to leave within fifteen days (at the end, five) and the plunder of the departing Greeks' houses.\n"};
